    prov:wasAttributedTo "Yusuf Altunel" ;
    skos:definition "A domain-neutral, reusable schema for backlog-and-roadmap governance across independent developments. It standardises five concept groups that recur in every product/engineering effort: (1) work items and their containers, (2) a closed lifecycle whose Done state is a constrained fact rather than asserted prose, (3) completion Evidence anchored to real delivered artifacts (a named passing test, a release carrying its package SHA-256, a file hash, or a recorded review), (4) method-parameterised prioritisation scores, and (5) a roadmap modelled as a derived projection over the backlog rather than an independently maintained list. Adoption is declared by an AdoptionProfile carrying a ConformanceLevel (L1 Core / L2 Evidence-Bound / L3 Governed), which the paired SHACL suite reads to decide which constraints are enforced, so one framework serves developments at different governance maturity without forking the vocabulary. This TBox defines vocabulary only; the paired SHACL shapes supply enforcement, the SPARQL rules supply derivation (container status, next-item selection, milestone status), and an execution bridge asserts observed real-world verification facts back into the ABox."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Scrum Guide 2020 (Product Backlog, Increment, Definition of Done); SAFe 6.0 (Epic/Feature/Story ladder, WSJF); Now-Next-Later roadmap practice; Intercom RICE prioritisation; W3C OWL 2, SHACL, SHACL-AF."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "v1.64.0 (MINOR, additive) — adopts another registrant's ceremony-coverage handover: a direct check of Sprint Planning/Review/Retrospective functionality found real, checked gaps in what this framework tracks automatically, distinct from human-ceremony modeling (which RegisterSession already, deliberately excludes). Adds hasSprintGoal (optional string on Iteration, deliberately unstructured for the same reason hasScoreRationale is, and deliberately optional: an Iteration without one is not itself a defect). No class added or removed; +7 triples. Separately confirmed NOT a gap: a distinct DefinitionOfDone for classification-type work, since DefinitionOfDone/DoDCriterion are already open, freely-extensible classes any lineage can add to without any TBox change."@en ,
    "v1.63.0 (MINOR, additive) — adopts Proposal_BRSF_ProductScopeKind_v1_0_0.md from code-abundance-rdodi-v1.6.1: this session's own ScopeLayer, applied at Layer_Product's boundary between what capability is delivered and how well/safely/transparently it works, had no vocabulary for that distinction, verified by searching the whole TBox for 'functional', 'non-functional', 'FURPS', 'ISO 25010' and finding only the OWL reserved term. Adds ProductScopeKind (Kind_Functional, Kind_NonFunctional; dcterms:source ISO/IEC 25010), hasProductScopeKind (domain ScopeDeliverable, narrower than hasScopeLayer's three-class domain, deliberately: only a Deliverable makes a capability-or-quality claim), and ProductScopeKindShape (advisory, mirroring BothLayersShape's own pattern). Test-driven against this package's own register per the proposal's own verification method and this discipline's G30, not added as schema alone: Del_OntGovernance (a capability claim) classified Kind_Functional, Del_OntRuleExec (a reliability guarantee about how that capability behaves) classified Kind_NonFunctional — a real mixed result on unforced data. The proposing session's own three deliverables (cross-checked directly against its repository, not summarized) give a second, independent mixed case: two non-functional, one functional."@en ,
    "v1.62.0 (PATCH, provenance only) — closes two findings from an external oe-pack governance handover (2026-08-25), both about TaskType and DesignConcern members shipping no dcterms:source and no isFrameworkOriginal declaration, silently indistinguishable from terms nobody sourced (Ex_InventedPractice / PracticeGroundingShape). Task_DesignDefinition, Task_SystemAnalysis, Task_Implementation, Task_Integration and Task_Verification now carry dcterms:source citing their specific ISO/IEC/IEEE 12207:2017 sub-clause (6.4.5-6.4.9), each flagged as clause-number-confirmed via secondary sources only, pending final confirmation against the primary paywalled text. Concern_Data, Concern_Interface, Concern_Interaction, Concern_Architecture and Concern_Security now carry isFrameworkOriginal: the parent DesignConcern class already cites Satzinger et al. ch.6 for the five-way split, but no single converging source was found for each concern's specific per-story analysis framing, independently re-checked this release against a fresh search of the source's own chapter structure. No class, property or individual added or removed; +10 triples total, 0 SHACL violations before or after, PracticeGroundingShape advisory count dropped by exactly 10."@en ,
    "v1.0.0 — generalisation of an adopting project's product-backlog deposit (an OE Pack registrant deposit, http://example.org/product-backlog 1.3.0) into a project-independent subject. Per L-105 this is an extension of that implementation, not a parallel one: every pb: term has a declared alignment in backlog_alignment_productbacklog_v1_0_0.ttl. Per L-68 the hyphenated/abbreviated form (product-backlog / pb:) is replaced by a single full-word prefix (backlog:); per L-69 the retained terminology anchors to the Scrum Guide and SAFe rather than to project-internal names. The deposit files themselves are NOT modified by this release (L-X7 / B1: the owning session decides supersession)."@en ,
    "v1.23.0 (MINOR, additions only) — an initiative-kind layer taken from ISO/IEC/IEEE 14764:2022 and 12207 rather than invented. The standard's own structure is a 2x2, not a list: maintenance work is classified by the TIMING of the change (reactive or proactive) and its GOAL (correction or enhancement), and the four familiar names are the cells. Corrective is reactive correction, preventive is proactive correction, adaptive is reactive enhancement, perfective is proactive enhancement. Recording the two axes and deriving the category, rather than picking a name from a list, is what makes the classification arguable: a reader can disagree with 'this was proactive' in a way they cannot disagree with 'this was perfective'. ModificationRequest and ProblemReport are 14764's own terms for what arrives and triggers the work."@en ,
    "v1.22.0 (MINOR, additions only) — makes the scope-first adaptation a governed procedure rather than a document someone follows carefully. Four ordered stages, each with a control point that must be recorded before the next may begin: assess the current order, run a fit-gap between the standing scope and the work actually underway, rule on whether the boundary is unchanged or must be rewritten, and only then re-link the lineage. The ruling is the branch the owner asked for: an unchanged boundary needs only re-linking, while a boundary with gaps needs rewriting and the goals and objectives beneath it revisited. Gates carry an executable check and an expected result, on the same pattern CrossCuttingInvariant already uses, so a stage cannot be declared passed on assertion alone."@en ,
    "v1.21.0 (MINOR, additions only) — records the ceremony order STRUCTURALLY instead of in prose. Every link in the intent chain already points from the later-written element to the earlier one: a Goal points at its Mission, an Objective at its Goal. The direction IS the order, and no date is needed to read it. scopeRealizesObjective points ScopeStatement at Objective, which encodes scope-written-last — the retired order, baked into the vocabulary. fillsScope is the reverse: an Objective names the scope it fills, so the scope must exist before the objective can be written. Deliberately NOT declared owl:inverseOf scopeRealizesObjective: making them inverses would let a reasoner materialise either from the other and destroy the very signal the direction carries."@en ,
    "v1.20.0 (MINOR, additions only) — BRF-EP9. A deployment must say on what basis its contents were chosen, and a release taking lower-scored work while higher-scored deliverable work waited must record why. Deliberately NOT a rule that a release must always take the top score: an ordering is a model and is sometimes wrong, and a rule with no exception path is bypassed the first time it is. What is enforced is that the exception is VISIBLE — the same shape as the ranking-fork resolution this subject already carries."@en ,
    "v1.19.0 (MINOR, additions only) — adds a fourth conformance level, L4_LineageEnforced, in which the lineage checks that were advisory become violations and every objective must carry a measurement rather than an intention. Added as a NEW level rather than by promoting advisories inside L3, because promotion would silently break every existing L3 adopter and because the tiering principle this framework already documents says a level is a claim an adopter makes, not one imposed on them. Existing L1, L2 and L3 behaviour is byte-identical. Also adds DeploymentUnit, so an iteration can be connected to what actually ships, and the epic-as-deployment-unit misuse becomes stateable rather than merely discouraged."@en ,
    "v1.18.0 (MINOR, additions only) — BRF-EP4, the last epic in the framework's own register. Makes human involvement in automated development a first-class register fact. The design question was settled before the build by the register's Ex_Modality exclusion: human WORK assigned to a person is a work item with a human modality and competes for capacity; human INTERACTION with work is an event on that item carrying its own cost and does not compete, because otherwise every review needs a score and the backlog fills with process. Reuse was checked first per L-110: Role, TeamRole, transitionedBy, decidedBy, ReviewEvidence, TransitionEvent and the cost dimensions all exist and are used rather than duplicated. What is new is the modality of execution, the kind of interaction, and the in-the-loop versus on-the-loop distinction, which is a fact about whether a transition is GATED — expressible only once something says so."@en ,
    "v1.17.0 (MINOR, additions only) — BRF-EP3 from the register. Cost becomes multi-dimensional: an item worked by an automated agent under human review carries token spend, environment cost and human time as separate named dimensions with their own units, and a budget is compared against their sum. No modality-specific predicate is minted — no tokenCost, no gpuHours — per the register's Ex_Modality exclusion: LLM-driven development is one modality among several and a predicate named for it would privilege it. Complexity likewise enters as a dimension rather than a property, per Ex_Complexity. Both were settled in the scope statement before this build started, which is the point of having one."@en ,
    "v1.16.0 (MINOR, domain widening — backwards compatible) — iterationStart and iterationEnd were domained on Iteration alone, so an Increment could not carry the period its own definition presupposes: a releasable body of work completed BY A STATED POINT has a period by construction. Found by backfilling shipped releases into an Increment and watching the burn-down skip it silently. Same shape as the roadmap-rank widening at subject v1.11.0 and caught the same way, by using the vocabulary rather than reading it."@en ,
    "v1.15.0 (MINOR, additions only) — the time-phased baseline the subject previously refused, admitted by an owner-decided ScopeChange rather than by deleting the exclusion that refused it. Ex_Schedule stays in the register as the record of the earlier decision; L-112 forbids editing a record of a past decision to match a later one. Adds a KickOff to anchor a plan to a real moment, planned start and finish so a baseline exists, a duration so a network can be traversed, and the widening of Rebaseline to reach the plan so that moving a baseline is a recorded move rather than a quiet edit. Note what this does NOT change: horizons remain ordinal, and hasRoadmapRank remains an ordering rather than a date. A plan and a roadmap now coexist and are deliberately different things."@en ,
    "v1.14.0 (MINOR, additions only) — BRF-EP2 from the framework's own register, ranked second at 4.00. Adds only what CANNOT be derived. Cycle time, item age, throughput and velocity are computed by the report from startedAt, finishedAt and the iteration period; storing them would duplicate a derivable fact, which is the defect L-91 names one level down. What could not be derived was the iteration's own period, without which velocity has no denominator, and a Forecast — because a forecast is a CLAIM about the future and the framework's standing position is that a claim must state what would make it wrong."@en ,
    "v1.13.0 (MINOR, additions only) — BRF-EP1 from the framework's own register, which the computed ranking put first at 6.50. Distinguishes work that carries independent value and is prioritised on a product backlog from subordinate execution work produced when a backlog item is planned into an iteration. Deliberately does NOT repurpose backlog:Task: its own definition says a Task must be tracked, PRIORITISED and evidenced like any other work item, so it is a non-user-facing product backlog item, not a sprint task, and redefining it would silently change the meaning of every register already using it. ExecutionTask is therefore a new ninth kind rather than a re-reading of an existing one."@en ,
    "v1.12.0 (MINOR, additions only) — makes the difference between a MEASURED estimate and a JUDGED one visible. hasCostBasis already recorded what an estimate was based on, in prose, and nothing distinguished a basis that was EXECUTED from one that was reasoned. An adopting project had already demonstrated the better practice without the vocabulary to declare it, setting an objective baseline by test-driving a trial declaration against a scratch copy of its live register and recording the resulting figure as measured rather than estimated. This release names that: a closed EstimationBasisKind, a link from a measured estimate or score to the MetricObservation that produced it, and constraints requiring a measured claim to name its observation. It does not forbid judgement — most estimates are judgements and saying so is honest — it forbids a judgement being indistinguishable from a measurement."@en ,
    "v1.11.0 (MINOR, domain widening — backwards compatible) — an adopting session ranked epics on a roadmap and the suite reported zero violations. It should not have. rankedOnRoadmap and hasRoadmapRank were domained on WorkItemContainer alone, while scheduledInHorizon already unioned WorkItem and WorkItemContainer, so an epic could legitimately be PLACED in a horizon but not RANKED in it. Worse, the two classes are declared disjoint, so under OWL 2 DL the domain would infer the epic to be a container and the register becomes logically INCONSISTENT — invisibly, because SHACL performs no such inference and an rdfs:domain is not a constraint. This package has never carried a reasoner attestation and disclosed that at registration; this is the first case where it cost an adopter a silently unsound register. Both properties now union the two classes, matching scheduledInHorizon and the way roadmaps are actually used."@en ,
    "v1.10.0 (MINOR, additions only) — closes the largest unguarded opt-out in the framework. Measured: declaring L1_Core suppresses 43 of 101 constraints, and on the shipped negative fixture changing that one token drops 306 violations to 101. Every other opt-out here — notYetScoreable, ScopeExclusion, Rebaseline, ScopeChange, accepted risk, ranking-fork resolution — requires a written rationale, and four of them require an owner decision. The conformance level, which suppresses more than all of them combined, required neither. The tier mechanism exists as an ADOPTION RAMP: a framework demanding everything on day one is adopted by nobody. Its misuse is as permanent shelter — a ramp used as a parking space. What is added therefore does not forbid a low level; it makes the choice owner-decided, reasoned, dated, and directional, so that standing still is visible rather than free."@en ,
    "v1.9.0 (MINOR, definition enrichment plus one clarification) — responds to an adopting project that registered a user-facing story whose Definition of Done named which files would change and which fields would be added, and which the suite accepted as complete because nothing distinguished describing a change from describing a behaviour. Per L-110 no UIScope flag or sibling term is minted on one producer's evidence: backlog:Story is ALREADY defined as the user-facing kind, so the flag exists, and backlog:AcceptanceCriterion is ALREADY defined in terms of observable behaviour, so the concept exists. What was missing was that neither said so forcefully enough to be actionable, and nothing surfaced the failure. Both definitions are enriched and an advisory is added. Also clarifies that hasSuccessMetric's range is nominal at L1."@en ,
    "v1.8.0 (MINOR, additions only) — adds hasRankingForkResolution, so an owner records ONCE which model resolves pickup when throughput and launch-scoped ranking disagree. Raised by an adopting project carrying a legitimate, long-running disagreement that every session re-argued because the register had nowhere to say the owner had already decided. It does not weaken the rule that the fork stays visible: both models are still computed and the disagreement notice is unchanged; the recorded decision is printed beside it, never instead of it. This is the same pattern the framework already applies to scope changes, re-baselines and accepted risks — an unavoidable judgement gets a place to be recorded, or it is remade informally every time."@en ,
    "v1.7.0 (MINOR, additions only) — closes an asymmetry found by audit: the framework constrained what a register must SAY and said nothing about what an adopting development must SHIP, so a register could be fully conformant in one working copy and unreproducible everywhere else. Adds RegisterPackage and RegisterArtifact with a closed five-role set, a package version, a mandatory manifest, and retention of roadmap report runs as files so progress has a trajectory rather than only a current state. Filename and version rules are NOT restated here: artifacts reference the configuration subject's existing NamingConvention individuals by IRI, and the package checker reads their filenamePattern from the pack at check time, so there is one source of truth for naming and it is the one that already owns it."@en ,
    "v1.6.0 (MINOR, additions only) — completes the intent chain and closes the scope boundary. Adds Mission as the root above goals, so every value claim traces mission-goal-objective-observation and terminates in something falsifiable; binds scope to the objectives it realises and derives both scope completion and scope outcome, so a development can report that it delivered everything it promised and still failed; adds ScopeChange so scope may grow but never invisibly; adds ExternalDependency over a closed six-type taxonomy with EnhancementProposal, encoding the rule that a change needed from a party outside the development is proposed to them and never scheduled here; and adds RegisterSession for the provenance of register edits, deliberately scoped to who changed what after verifying what, not to meetings."@en ,
    "v1.5.0 (MINOR, additions only) — closes a falsifiability gap demonstrated by an adversarial register that passed every gate at conformance level L3 while being arbitrary: a WSJF score of 9.9 over components computing to 0.3, evidence marked verified with the method 'asserted', an objective with a metric, baseline and target that was never observed and whose deadline had passed, a milestone months overdue with no outcome, acceptance criteria reading 'Given, When, Then.', and scores six months older than the last completion. Everything added here exists so a register can be WRONG: metric observations and target direction, derived objective and milestone outcomes including Missed, explicit owner-decided re-baselining, actual effort against estimate, and tool-named verification. A plan that cannot fail cannot succeed either."@en ,
    "v1.4.0 (MINOR, additions only) — closes the gaps found by a fit-gap analysis against the agile literature and standards: the Scrum Guide 2020 artifact-commitment structure, OntoAgile (DYNA 86(209), 2019), Strode's dependency taxonomy (Information Systems Frontiers 18(1), 2016) and the Kanban flow measures. Adds decomposition (the epic-feature-story ladder had no part-whole relation at all), artifact commitments, typed dependencies, impediments as distinct from dependencies, flow start and finish points with a WIP-limit policy, team and open-ended team roles with capacity, and the canonical user-story clauses. Method-level concepts from OntoAgile — agile values, principles, practices, activities and agility assessment — are deliberately NOT adopted: they describe how a method is performed, which is a different subject from the register this framework governs."@en ,
    "v1.3.0 (MINOR, additions only) — closes the linkage gaps found by a relational audit: containers had no dependency on other containers and no place on the roadmap, acceptance criteria had no link to the evidence that proves them, there was no test-harness concept tying a completion claim to the criteria it must satisfy, and the closed lifecycle enumeration had no workflow saying which moves between states are permitted or under what guard. Adds containerDependsOn with its derived counterpart, roadmap placement and declared rank for containers, attestsCriterion, TestHarness with derived completeness, effectiveDefinitionOfDone inherited from containers, and Workflow / StateTransition / TransitionEvent. Domains of scheduledInHorizon, contributesToMilestone, hasDependencyDisclosure and the range of disclosesDependencyOn are widened to containers, which is backwards-compatible."@en ,
    "v1.2.0 (MINOR, additions only) — closes the concept-completeness gaps found by an audit against a twenty-concept industrial/academic checklist, which measured 10 of 20 present. Adds the intent layer (goal, objective with success metric, benefit with evidenced realisation, opportunity), explicit scope with owner-decided exclusions, refinement events that make the Ready state earned rather than assumed, unit-neutral cost estimates carrying their basis, investment categories and product lifecycle phase so that building and maintaining are arbitrated by policy rather than by a single score, portfolio capacity policy, and ImplementationProject as the container the project-level Definition of Done applies to. Risk is NOT minted here: the pack's risk subject already defines risk per ISO 31000:2018 as the effect of uncertainty on objectives, so this release delegates to risk:Risk and risk:Mitigation and adds only the binding properties and the constraint that an untreated risk must be explicitly accepted."@en ,
    "v1.1.0 (MINOR, additions only) — closes the primary-source coverage gap. v1.0.0 was authored without access to the governing standard document (BACKLOG_ROADMAP_STANDARD_v1_0_0.md, adopted 2026-07-27, SHA-256 a58a6c8911b391c4…), which returned HTTP 404 at the time; when the document was supplied, the BP-D31 concept-coverage gate measured 22.2% (8 of 36 named concepts demonstrated). This version adds the blueprint layer, the launch-readiness prioritisation model and its arbitration with throughput ranking, binary scoreability/gap discipline, capability tagging, dependency disclosure, document supersession, the computed roadmap report with its required sections, methodology-rule provenance, release-gate vocabulary, and the owner/builder role split. No term from v1.0.0 was removed or renamed; the domain of hasPriorityScore is widened to include containers, which is backwards-compatible."@en .

#################################################################
#  Discipline umbrella (cross-subject *Concept pattern)
#################################################################

backlog:BacklogConcept a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Backlog Concept"@en ;
    skos:definition "Discipline-root umbrella for every concept this subject introduces about backlog and roadmap governance, placed under core:Concept so the framework participates in the pack's cross-subject concept taxonomy rather than standing apart from it."@en .

#################################################################
#  1. Work items
#################################################################

backlog:WorkItem a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Work Item"@en ;
    skos:definition "A single tracked unit of intended, deliverable functionality or change, identified by an identifier unique across its register, carrying exactly one lifecycle state, at most one priority score per prioritisation method, optional container membership, optional dependencies, and — when Done under an evidence-bound profile — at least one verified Evidence artifact. The root of the framework: everything a development claims it will do, is doing, or has done is an instance of this class or one of its kinds."@en .

backlog:Initiative a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Initiative"@en ;
    skos:definition "A portfolio-granularity work item expressing a strategic outcome that spans multiple epics and usually multiple increments; its completion is always derived from constituent work, never asserted directly."@en .

backlog:Epic a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Epic"@en ;
    skos:definition "A large body of work decomposed into, or delivered across, multiple features or stories; its completion is typically derived from the completion of its constituent work rather than from a single test."@en .

backlog:Feature a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Feature"@en ;
    skos:definition "A work item that delivers a coherent, demonstrable capability to a user or system within a single increment, sized between an epic and a story."@en .

backlog:Story a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Story"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bill Wake, INVEST in Good Stories (2003) — the S is Small: a story sized to be completed within one iteration"@en ;
    skos:definition "A single, independently deliverable user-facing slice of functionality, small enough to be completed within one iteration and to be attested by a named test."@en .

backlog:Task a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Task"@en ;
    skos:definition "A unit of execution work that is not itself user-facing (build step, migration, configuration change) but must still be tracked, prioritised and evidenced like any other work item."@en .

backlog:Defect a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Defect"@en ;
    skos:definition "A work item recording observed behaviour that diverges from specified or expected behaviour; its Done evidence is normally a regression test that fails before the fix and passes after it."@en .

backlog:Spike a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Spike"@en ;
    skos:definition "A time-boxed investigation whose deliverable is knowledge (a decision, a measurement, a prototype discarded afterwards) rather than shipped functionality; its Evidence is a recorded finding artifact, not a passing feature test."@en .

backlog:Enabler a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Enabler"@en ;
    skos:definition "A work item that builds architectural, infrastructural or compliance capability required by other work items rather than by an end user directly; it is prioritised in the same register so that infrastructure work cannot become invisible untracked effort."@en .

[] a owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( backlog:Initiative backlog:Epic backlog:Feature backlog:Story
                 backlog:Task backlog:Defect backlog:Spike backlog:Enabler ) .

#################################################################
#  2. Containers
#################################################################

backlog:WorkItemContainer a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Work Item Container"@en ;
    skos:definition "Any named grouping of work items whose lifecycle state is derived from its members rather than asserted independently. Introduced as a common superclass so the derivation rule is written once and applies to every grouping kind a development chooses to use."@en .

backlog:Backlog a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:WorkItemContainer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Backlog"@en ;
    skos:definition "The single ordered register of all work items for one product or development. The framework's source of truth: scope, progress and next are read from it, never maintained separately in prose."@en .

backlog:Package a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:WorkItemContainer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Package"@en ;
    skos:definition "A deployable business function: a coherent group of work items that together deliver meaningful, manually-testable functionality the business can release as a unit. Its state is derived (Done only when every member is Done), never asserted."@en .

backlog:Increment a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:WorkItemContainer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Increment"@en ;
    skos:definition "The cumulative, releasable body of work completed by a stated point in time; the unit against which release evidence is anchored."@en .

backlog:Iteration a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:WorkItemContainer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Iteration"@en ;
    skos:definition "A fixed-length time box within which a selected subset of work items is worked; used for cadence and forecasting, not for defining deliverable scope."@en .

#################################################################
#  3. Lifecycle
#################################################################

backlog:LifecycleState a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Proposed backlog:Ready backlog:InProgress backlog:Done backlog:Cancelled ) ;
    rdfs:label "Lifecycle State"@en ;
    skos:definition "The state of a work item or container within its delivery lifecycle. A closed enumeration of exactly five individuals — Proposed, Ready, InProgress, Done, Cancelled — so that any other asserted state is a semantic error a reasoner detects rather than free-text a reader must interpret."@en .

backlog:Proposed a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:LifecycleState ;
    rdfs:label "Proposed"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recorded and prioritisable, but not yet refined to the point where work could start; acceptance criteria may still be absent."@en .

backlog:Ready a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:LifecycleState ;
    rdfs:label "Ready"@en ;
    skos:definition "Refined and unblocked: acceptance criteria exist and every dependency is Done, so work can start without further clarification. The state the next-item selection query draws from."@en .

backlog:InProgress a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:LifecycleState ;
    rdfs:label "In Progress"@en ;
    skos:definition "Being worked; partial delivery may exist but completion evidence is absent or not yet verified."@en .

backlog:Done a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:LifecycleState ;
    rdfs:label "Done"@en ;
    skos:definition "Complete AND backed by evidence. Under an evidence-bound profile (L2 or L3) the SHACL suite refuses this state to any item lacking at least one Evidence whose evidenceVerified flag is true, so 'done' can never be a prose claim."@en .

backlog:Cancelled a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:LifecycleState ;
    rdfs:label "Cancelled"@en ;
    skos:definition "Withdrawn without delivery. A terminal state that carries no evidence obligation but must record a rationale, so abandoned scope stays visible instead of silently disappearing from the register."@en .

[] a owl:AllDifferent ; owl:distinctMembers ( backlog:Proposed backlog:Ready backlog:InProgress backlog:Done backlog:Cancelled ) .

#################################################################
#  4. Evidence
#################################################################

backlog:Evidence a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Completion Evidence"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, durable artifact that proves a work item is Done. A subclass of core:Artifact because it is a thing that exists in the workspace, not a claim about one. Evidence becomes trustworthy only once its evidenceVerified flag has been set true by an execution bridge that confirmed the artifact actually exists — and, for a test, currently passes — outside the graph."@en .

backlog:TestEvidence a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:Evidence ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Test Evidence"@en ;
    skos:definition "Completion evidence that is a named, currently-passing automated test, connected to the testing subject via attestedByTest and recording the spec path and test identifier so the bridge verifies the exact test rather than the file as a whole."@en .

backlog:ReleaseEvidence a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:Evidence ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Release Evidence"@en ;
    skos:definition "Completion evidence that is a delivered, immutable release: a version whose delivered package carries a SHA-256 manifest hash, connected to the release-history subject via deliveredInRelease. The package hash is the anchor that makes 'what shipped' checkable, replacing the failed practice of hashing backlog prose."@en .

backlog:ArtifactEvidence a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:Evidence ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Artifact Evidence"@en ;
    skos:definition "Completion evidence that is a specific delivered file — a document, dataset, model or ontology — identified by path and content hash, for work whose deliverable is neither a test nor a release."@en .

backlog:ReviewEvidence a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:Evidence ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Review Evidence"@en ;
    skos:definition "Completion evidence that is a recorded human judgement (approval, sign-off, acceptance) identified by reviewer and durable record reference. The weakest evidence kind: admissible only where no executable check exists, and never sufficient on its own at conformance level L3."@en .

#################################################################
#  5. Definition of Done & acceptance
#################################################################

backlog:DefinitionOfDone a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Definition of Done"@en ;
    skos:definition "The standing set of criteria every work item in a scope must satisfy before it may enter the Done state. Modelled as a first-class individual with executable criteria rather than as prose, because a Definition of Done is only enforceable when a meta-gate can make each criterion falsifiable."@en .

backlog:DoDCriterion a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Definition-of-Done Criterion"@en ;
    skos:definition "One falsifiable clause of a Definition of Done, carrying the query or command that decides it and the honest current result of running that check. A criterion with no check text is decorative and is rejected by the SHACL suite."@en .

backlog:AcceptanceCriterion a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Acceptance Criterion"@en ;
    skos:definition "A per-item condition, normally written in Given/When/Then form, that defines what observable behaviour the item must exhibit to be accepted. Carried on the item itself so acceptance text cannot drift away from the register into a separate document. Observable behaviour is the whole of it: an enumeration of the files that will change, the fields that will be added, or the tables that will be migrated describes the CHANGE and not the BEHAVIOUR, and is therefore not an acceptance criterion however precisely it is written. The test is whether someone who never sees the diff could tell, from this text alone, whether the item works — what a user does, what they then see, and what happens when it goes wrong. For an item with a user-facing surface that includes the surface's own behaviour: the states of a control, its default, whether a choice persists, what an empty result looks like, and who is permitted to see it. A criterion that only a reader of the implementation can evaluate has moved the acceptance decision inside the work it was supposed to judge."@en .

#################################################################
#  6. Prioritisation
#################################################################

backlog:PrioritizationMethod a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Prioritization Method"@en ;
    skos:definition "A named, reproducible procedure for converting item attributes into a comparable priority number or rank. Left deliberately open (not an owl:oneOf enumeration) because developments legitimately adopt methods beyond the ones shipped; what the framework fixes is that every score must name its method, not which methods exist."@en .

backlog:PriorityScore a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Priority Score"@en ;
    skos:definition "A computed priority value attached to a work item, carrying the method that produced it and the time it was computed. Reified as its own individual — rather than a bare number on the item — so that scores from different methods coexist without collision and so a stale score is detectable by its timestamp."@en .

backlog:WSJFScore a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:PriorityScore ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "WSJF Score"@en ;
    skos:definition "A priority score computed as Cost of Delay divided by Job Size, where Cost of Delay is the sum of user-business value, time criticality, and risk-reduction/opportunity-enablement. Retains the component values so the number can be recomputed and challenged rather than merely trusted."@en .

backlog:RICEScore a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:PriorityScore ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "RICE Score"@en ;
    skos:definition "A priority score computed as Reach x Impact x Confidence divided by Effort, optionally multiplied by a dependency factor that demotes items whose prerequisites are not yet Done. Retains its components for the same recomputability reason as WSJF."@en .

#################################################################
#  7. Roadmap (a projection of the backlog, never an independent list)
#################################################################

backlog:RoadmapElement a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Roadmap Element"@en ;
    skos:definition "Any concept belonging to the time-facing projection of a backlog. Grouped under one superclass to make the framework's central roadmap claim structural: roadmap elements reference work items that already exist in the register, and never introduce scope of their own."@en .

backlog:Roadmap a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:RoadmapElement ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Roadmap"@en ;
    skos:definition "A derived, time-ordered view over exactly one backlog, presenting its items by horizon and milestone. Because it is a projection, a roadmap cannot contain an item absent from its backlog; the SHACL suite treats such an item as a violation rather than as new scope."@en .

backlog:RoadmapHorizon a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:RoadmapElement ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Now backlog:Next backlog:Later ) ;
    rdfs:label "Roadmap Horizon"@en ;
    skos:definition "The commitment band a work item sits in on the roadmap. A closed enumeration of exactly three individuals — Now, Next, Later — chosen over date-pinned columns because horizon bands carry honest uncertainty, whereas dates on unstarted work carry false precision."@en .

backlog:Now a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:RoadmapHorizon ;
    rdfs:label "Now"@en ;
    skos:definition "Committed and actively worked in the current increment; items here are expected to be Ready or InProgress."@en .

backlog:Next a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:RoadmapHorizon ;
    rdfs:label "Next"@en ;
    skos:definition "Intended for the increment after the current one; refined enough to be estimated but not committed."@en .

backlog:Later a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:RoadmapHorizon ;
    rdfs:label "Later"@en ;
    skos:definition "Directional intent beyond the next increment; deliberately unrefined, and never to be reported to stakeholders as a schedule."@en .

[] a owl:AllDifferent ; owl:distinctMembers ( backlog:Now backlog:Next backlog:Later ) .

backlog:Milestone a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:RoadmapElement ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Milestone"@en ;
    skos:definition "A named point of external significance — a launch, an audit, a contractual date — whose achievement is derived from the completion of the work items that contribute to it, so a milestone can never be reported green while its contributing work is not Done."@en .

#################################################################
#  8. Invariants & audit
#################################################################

backlog:CrossCuttingInvariant a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Cross-Cutting Invariant"@en ;
    skos:definition "A standing property that must hold across the system regardless of which work item is being worked, carried as an executable check with an honest status rather than as a paragraph of intent. Makes 'we always check X' an assertion a gate can run and fail."@en .

backlog:InvariantStatus a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Holds backlog:Violated backlog:NotYetEnforceable ) ;
    rdfs:label "Invariant Status"@en ;
    skos:definition "The honest current result of an invariant's check. Closed enumeration of three individuals; the third exists specifically so that an invariant whose enforcing work item is not yet Done is reported as unenforceable rather than silently as passing."@en .

backlog:Holds a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:InvariantStatus ;
    rdfs:label "Holds"@en ;
    skos:definition "The check was executed in this pass and returned the expected result."@en .

backlog:Violated a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:InvariantStatus ;
    rdfs:label "Violated"@en ;
    skos:definition "The check was executed in this pass and returned an unexpected result; the invariant is currently broken."@en .

backlog:NotYetEnforceable a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:InvariantStatus ;
    rdfs:label "Not Yet Enforceable"@en ;
    skos:definition "The check cannot yet be executed meaningfully because the work item that would enforce the invariant is not Done. Must never be reported as passing."@en .

[] a owl:AllDifferent ; owl:distinctMembers ( backlog:Holds backlog:Violated backlog:NotYetEnforceable ) .

#################################################################
#  9. Adoption profile & conformance levels (the reuse mechanism)
#################################################################

backlog:AdoptionProfile a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Adoption Profile"@en ;
    skos:definition "One development's declared adoption of this framework: which backlog it governs, which optional facets it uses, and which conformance level it claims. The profile is what makes a single vocabulary reusable across developments of different maturity — the SHACL suite reads the claimed level and enforces exactly the constraints that level promises, so a level-1 adopter is not failed by evidence rules it never claimed and a level-3 adopter cannot quietly skip them."@en .

backlog:ConformanceLevel a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:L1_Core backlog:L2_EvidenceBound backlog:L3_Governed backlog:L4_LineageEnforced ) ;
    rdfs:label "Conformance Level"@en ;
    skos:definition "The graded strength of a development's claim on this framework. Closed enumeration of four individuals, each a strict superset of the previous, so 'conformant' is always a measured statement about which constraint set passed rather than an unqualified adjective."@en .

backlog:L1_Core a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ConformanceLevel ;
    rdfs:label "L1 Core"@en ;
    skos:definition "Every work item carries a unique identifier, exactly one lifecycle state, and well-typed container and dependency references. Structural integrity only: nothing is claimed about how completion is proven."@en .

backlog:L2_EvidenceBound a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ConformanceLevel ;
    rdfs:label "L2 Evidence-Bound"@en ;
    skos:definition "L1 plus: no item may hold the Done state without at least one Evidence whose evidenceVerified flag is true, every Definition-of-Done criterion carries executable check text, and container state is derived rather than asserted. This is the level at which 'done' stops being prose."@en .

backlog:L3_Governed a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ConformanceLevel ;
    rdfs:label "L3 Governed"@en ;
    skos:definition "L2 plus: Done items are anchored to a release carrying a package SHA-256 or to a named passing test (review evidence alone is insufficient), each item records when it was last individually re-verified, cross-cutting invariants are executable with honest statuses, and the reconciliation gate runs as a mandatory release gate rather than as an available script."@en .

[] a owl:AllDifferent ; owl:distinctMembers ( backlog:L1_Core backlog:L2_EvidenceBound backlog:L3_Governed ) .

backlog:FrameworkFacet a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:CoreFacet backlog:EvidenceFacet backlog:PrioritizationFacet
                backlog:RoadmapFacet backlog:InvariantFacet backlog:AuditFacet ) ;
    rdfs:label "Framework Facet"@en ;
    skos:definition "A separable part of the framework a development may adopt or omit. Closed enumeration of six individuals; declaring facets explicitly prevents the common failure where an unused part of a standard is assumed to be in force by one reader and ignored by another."@en .

backlog:CoreFacet a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:FrameworkFacet ;
    rdfs:label "Core Facet"@en ;
    skos:definition "Work items, containers and the lifecycle enumeration. Mandatory: every adoption includes it."@en .

backlog:EvidenceFacet a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:FrameworkFacet ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence Facet"@en ;
    skos:definition "Evidence classes, the verification flag, and the Definition-of-Done criteria. Required at conformance level L2 and above."@en .

backlog:PrioritizationFacet a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:FrameworkFacet ;
    rdfs:label "Prioritization Facet"@en ;
    skos:definition "Method-parameterised priority scores and the next-item selection rule."@en .

backlog:RoadmapFacet a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:FrameworkFacet ;
    rdfs:label "Roadmap Facet"@en ;
    skos:definition "Roadmaps, horizons and milestones as projections over the backlog."@en .

backlog:InvariantFacet a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:FrameworkFacet ;
    rdfs:label "Invariant Facet"@en ;
    skos:definition "Cross-cutting invariants with executable checks and honest statuses. Required at conformance level L3."@en .

backlog:AuditFacet a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:FrameworkFacet ;
    rdfs:label "Audit Facet"@en ;
    skos:definition "Per-item re-verification timestamps and audit notes, so coverage of a reconciliation pass is recorded item by item rather than implied. Required at conformance level L3."@en .

[] a owl:AllDifferent ; owl:distinctMembers ( backlog:CoreFacet backlog:EvidenceFacet backlog:PrioritizationFacet
                                              backlog:RoadmapFacet backlog:InvariantFacet backlog:AuditFacet ) .

#################################################################
#  Top-level disjointness
#################################################################

[] a owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( backlog:WorkItem backlog:WorkItemContainer backlog:LifecycleState
                 backlog:Evidence backlog:PriorityScore backlog:PrioritizationMethod
                 backlog:RoadmapElement backlog:CrossCuttingInvariant
                 backlog:DefinitionOfDone backlog:DoDCriterion backlog:AcceptanceCriterion
                 backlog:AdoptionProfile backlog:ConformanceLevel backlog:FrameworkFacet
                 backlog:InvariantStatus ) .

#################################################################
#  Object properties
#################################################################

backlog:hasState a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:WorkItem backlog:WorkItemContainer ) ] ;
    rdfs:range backlog:LifecycleState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has state"@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates a work item or container to its single lifecycle state. Functional, so two contradictory states on one subject are a reasoner-detected inconsistency rather than a reporting discrepancy someone must notice."@en .

backlog:derivedState a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItemContainer ;
    rdfs:range backlog:LifecycleState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "derived state"@en ;
    skos:definition "The container state computed by the derivation rules from member states. Written by the rule engine, never by hand; a container whose asserted state disagrees with its derived state is a SHACL violation."@en .

backlog:derivedMilestoneState a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Milestone ;
    rdfs:range backlog:LifecycleState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "derived milestone state"@en ;
    skos:definition "The milestone state computed by the derivation rules from the states of its contributing work items. Held on a separate property from container state because a milestone is a roadmap element, not a container, and conflating the two would let a milestone be reported achieved by membership rather than by contribution."@en .

backlog:hasEvidence a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:range backlog:Evidence ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has evidence"@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates a work item to the Evidence artifact or artifacts that prove its completion. At conformance level L2 and above at least one verified instance is required before the item may hold the Done state."@en .

backlog:memberOfContainer a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:range backlog:WorkItemContainer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "member of container"@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates a work item to a container it belongs to. The single relation the container-state derivation rule traverses, so membership is the only thing that has to be maintained for container reporting to stay correct."@en .

backlog:hasMember a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    owl:inverseOf backlog:memberOfContainer ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItemContainer ;
    rdfs:range backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has member"@en ;
    skos:definition "Inverse of memberOfContainer, declared once and in this direction only so no consumer has to reconcile two independently asserted inverse pairs."@en .

backlog:dependsOn a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:TransitiveProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:range backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "depends on"@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates a work item to another that must be Done before it can start. Transitive, so indirect blocking is visible to the next-item selection rule. Deliberately NOT declared irreflexive or asymmetric: OWL 2 DL forbids those characteristics on a non-simple (transitive) property, so dependency cycles are detected by a SHACL constraint instead of by the reasoner."@en .

backlog:blocks a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    owl:inverseOf backlog:dependsOn ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:range backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "blocks"@en ;
    skos:definition "Inverse of dependsOn, declared once and in this direction only; provided because roadmap and impact queries read more naturally forward than backward."@en .

backlog:attestedByTest a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:TestEvidence ;
    rdfs:range testing:Test ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "attested by test"@en ;
    skos:definition "Connects test evidence to the test that attests completion, keeping the register bound to the testing subject's taxonomy instead of duplicating a local notion of test."@en .

backlog:deliveredInRelease a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ReleaseEvidence ;
    rdfs:range orh:ReleaseEvent ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "delivered in release"@en ;
    skos:definition "Connects release evidence to the release event in which the item shipped, keeping delivery provenance in the governed release record rather than in a per-item note."@en .

backlog:hasPriorityScore a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:WorkItem backlog:WorkItemContainer ) ] ;
    rdfs:range backlog:PriorityScore ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has priority score"@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates a work item to a computed priority score. Multiple scores are permitted only when they name different methods; two live scores from one method on one item is a violation."@en .

backlog:scoredByMethod a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:PriorityScore ;
    rdfs:range backlog:PrioritizationMethod ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "scored by method"@en ;
    skos:definition "The method that produced this score. Functional and mandatory, because an unattributed priority number cannot be recomputed, compared or challenged."@en .

backlog:hasAcceptanceCriterion a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:range backlog:AcceptanceCriterion ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has acceptance criterion"@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates a work item to the conditions under which it will be accepted, held on the item rather than in a separate specification document."@en .

backlog:appliesDefinitionOfDone a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:WorkItem backlog:WorkItemContainer ) ] ;
    rdfs:range backlog:DefinitionOfDone ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "applies definition of done"@en ;
    skos:definition "Declares which Definition of Done governs a work item or container, so the applicable criteria are resolvable from the register instead of assumed from team habit."@en .

backlog:hasDoDCriterion a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:DefinitionOfDone ;
    rdfs:range backlog:DoDCriterion ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has criterion"@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates a Definition of Done to its individual falsifiable clauses."@en .

backlog:hasCriterionStatus a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:DoDCriterion ;
    rdfs:range backlog:InvariantStatus ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has criterion status"@en ;
    skos:definition "The honest current result of running this criterion's check, drawn from the same three-value enumeration as invariants so a criterion whose enforcing work is incomplete is reported as unenforceable rather than as passing."@en .

backlog:hasInvariantStatus a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:CrossCuttingInvariant ;
    rdfs:range backlog:InvariantStatus ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has invariant status"@en ;
    skos:definition "The honest current result of this invariant's check."@en .

backlog:tracksItem a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:CrossCuttingInvariant ;
    rdfs:range backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "tracks item"@en ;
    skos:definition "The work item whose completion would make this invariant enforceable, so an unenforceable invariant always points at the work that would fix that."@en .

backlog:roadmapOf a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Roadmap ;
    rdfs:range backlog:Backlog ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "roadmap of"@en ;
    skos:definition "The single backlog a roadmap projects. Functional: a roadmap spanning two registers would reintroduce exactly the untraceable scope this framework exists to prevent."@en .

backlog:scheduledInHorizon a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:WorkItem backlog:WorkItemContainer ) ] ;
    rdfs:range backlog:RoadmapHorizon ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "scheduled in horizon"@en ;
    skos:definition "The commitment band this item occupies on the roadmap. Functional, so an item cannot be simultaneously advertised as Now to one audience and Later to another."@en .

backlog:contributesToMilestone a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:WorkItem backlog:WorkItemContainer ) ] ;
    rdfs:range backlog:Milestone ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "contributes to milestone"@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates a work item to a milestone whose achievement depends on it; the milestone-status rule traverses this relation and nothing else."@en .

backlog:governsBacklog a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:AdoptionProfile ;
    rdfs:range backlog:Backlog ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "governs backlog"@en ;
    skos:definition "The backlog register this adoption profile applies to."@en .

backlog:hasConformanceLevel a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:AdoptionProfile ;
    rdfs:range backlog:ConformanceLevel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has conformance level"@en ;
    skos:definition "The single level this adoption claims. The SHACL suite reads this property to decide which constraint tier is in force, which is why it is functional and mandatory on every profile."@en .

backlog:adoptsFacet a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:AdoptionProfile ;
    rdfs:range backlog:FrameworkFacet ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "adopts facet"@en ;
    skos:definition "A part of the framework this adoption puts in force. Facets required by the claimed conformance level must be present; omitting one is a violation rather than a silent downgrade."@en .

#################################################################
#  Datatype properties
#################################################################

backlog:hasIdentifier a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:WorkItem backlog:WorkItemContainer backlog:Milestone ) ] ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has identifier"@en ;
    skos:definition "The unique human-facing identifier of a work item, container or milestone. Functional per subject; uniqueness across the whole register is enforced by SHACL, so identifier reuse after a deletion is structurally impossible rather than merely discouraged."@en .

backlog:hasTitle a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:WorkItem backlog:WorkItemContainer backlog:Milestone backlog:Roadmap ) ] ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has title"@en ;
    skos:definition "A short human-readable title or summary."@en .

backlog:hasRationale a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has rationale"@en ;
    skos:definition "Why this item exists, or — for a cancelled item — why it was withdrawn. Required on cancellation so abandoned scope leaves a reason behind it."@en .

backlog:hasGherkinText a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:AcceptanceCriterion ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has Gherkin text"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Given/When/Then text of an acceptance criterion, carried as a literal on the criterion individual."@en .

backlog:hasCheckQuery a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:CrossCuttingInvariant backlog:DoDCriterion ) ] ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has check query"@en ;
    skos:definition "The SPARQL query or shell command that executes this check. Its presence is what separates an enforceable criterion from a decorative one, which is why the SHACL suite requires it."@en .

backlog:hasExpectedResult a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:CrossCuttingInvariant backlog:DoDCriterion ) ] ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has expected result"@en ;
    skos:definition "The result the check must produce for the criterion to be considered satisfied. Recorded alongside the check because a query without a stated expectation cannot fail."@en .

backlog:evidenceVerified a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Evidence ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "evidence verified"@en ;
    skos:definition "A world-check fact asserted by the execution bridge: true if and only if the evidence artifact was confirmed to exist — and, for a test, to pass — in the real workspace at reconciliation time. The ontology cannot read the filesystem, so this is the one fact that must come from outside the graph; absence must be read as unverified, never as probably-fine."@en .

backlog:verifiedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Evidence ;
    rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "verified at"@en ;
    skos:definition "When the execution bridge last confirmed this evidence, so a stale verification is detectable by age rather than assumed current."@en .

backlog:hasVerificationMethod a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Evidence ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has verification method"@en ;
    skos:definition "How the bridge checked this evidence — the command run, the hash compared, the endpoint queried — recorded in the same record as the result so a pass claim always carries its method."@en .

backlog:hasTestSpec a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:TestEvidence ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has test spec"@en ;
    skos:definition "The test specification file path, machine-readable so the bridge can confirm the file exists and execute it."@en .

backlog:hasTestId a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:TestEvidence ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has test id"@en ;
    skos:definition "The identifier of the specific test within the spec, so the bridge verifies the exact test rather than the file as a whole."@en .

backlog:hasReleaseVersion a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ReleaseEvidence ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has release version"@en ;
    skos:definition "The delivered version string this evidence refers to."@en .

backlog:hasPackageSHA256 a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ReleaseEvidence ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has package SHA-256"@en ;
    skos:definition "The SHA-256 manifest hash of the delivered package. The immutable anchor the bridge re-computes against the real delivered artifact; hashes belong here, on what shipped, never on backlog prose."@en .

backlog:hasArtifactPath a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ArtifactEvidence ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has artifact path"@en ;
    skos:definition "The path of the delivered file this evidence refers to."@en .

backlog:hasArtifactSHA256 a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ArtifactEvidence ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has artifact SHA-256"@en ;
    skos:definition "The SHA-256 hash of the delivered file, so the evidence remains valid only for the exact bytes that were reviewed."@en .

backlog:hasReviewer a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ReviewEvidence ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has reviewer"@en ;
    skos:definition "Who recorded the judgement this evidence rests on."@en .

backlog:hasReviewRecordRef a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ReviewEvidence ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has review record reference"@en ;
    skos:definition "A durable reference to where the judgement is recorded, so review evidence points at something retrievable rather than at a remembered conversation."@en .

backlog:hasScoreValue a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:PriorityScore ;
    rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has score value"@en ;
    skos:definition "The computed priority number; higher means more urgent under every method the framework ships."@en .

backlog:scoredAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:PriorityScore ;
    rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "scored at"@en ;
    skos:definition "When this score was computed. Present because rankings are snapshots: a score is evidence about the moment it was taken, not a standing property of the item."@en .

backlog:hasScoreRationale a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:PriorityScore ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has score rationale"@en ;
    skos:definition "The reasoning behind the component values, so a score can be argued with rather than merely disputed."@en .

backlog:hasUserBusinessValue a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WSJFScore ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has user-business value"@en ;
    skos:definition "The user and business value component of a WSJF cost of delay."@en .

backlog:hasTimeCriticality a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WSJFScore ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has time criticality"@en ;
    skos:definition "The decay-of-value-over-time component of a WSJF cost of delay."@en .

backlog:hasRiskReductionValue a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WSJFScore ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has risk-reduction / opportunity-enablement value"@en ;
    skos:definition "The risk-reduction and opportunity-enablement component of a WSJF cost of delay."@en .

backlog:hasJobSize a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WSJFScore ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has job size"@en ;
    skos:definition "The WSJF divisor: relative effort to deliver the item."@en .

backlog:hasReach a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:RICEScore ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has reach"@en ;
    skos:definition "How many subjects the item affects in a stated period — the R of RICE."@en .

backlog:hasImpact a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:RICEScore ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has impact"@en ;
    skos:definition "How much the item moves the target outcome per affected subject — the I of RICE."@en .

backlog:hasConfidence a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:RICEScore ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has confidence"@en ;
    skos:definition "How well-evidenced the reach and impact estimates are, expressed as a fraction — the C of RICE."@en .

backlog:hasEffort a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:RICEScore ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has effort"@en ;
    skos:definition "Estimated total work to deliver — the E of RICE, and the divisor of the score."@en .

backlog:hasDepFactor a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:RICEScore ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has dependency factor"@en ;
    skos:definition "A multiplier below one that demotes an item whose prerequisites are not yet Done, so blocked work cannot outrank startable work purely on nominal value."@en .

backlog:lastAuditedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "last audited at"@en ;
    skos:definition "When this specific item was last directly re-verified against live artifacts during an item-by-item reconciliation pass. Set only when the item itself was checked, never inferred from other items being checked, from the register conforming overall, or from elapsed time; absence means unchecked."@en .

backlog:hasAuditNote a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has audit note"@en ;
    skos:definition "What the last reconciliation pass found for this item, including a claim it disproved."@en .

backlog:hasTargetDate a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Milestone ;
    rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has target date"@en ;
    skos:definition "The externally significant date a milestone is aimed at. Carried on milestones only — never on individual items — because dates on unstarted work are false precision."@en .

backlog:bindsNamespace a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:AdoptionProfile ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "binds namespace"@en ;
    skos:definition "The namespace in which the adopting development writes its own register instances, recorded so a consumer can tell whose data a merged graph contains."@en .

backlog:profileAppliesTo a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:AdoptionProfile ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "profile applies to"@en ;
    skos:definition "The name of the development this adoption profile speaks for."@en .

#################################################################
#  10. Blueprint layer (v1.1.0)
#     The layer the backlog is scoped AGAINST. Separated from the
#     register because a blueprint describes what the domain is,
#     while a backlog describes what will be built about it.
#################################################################

backlog:Blueprint a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Blueprint"@en ;
    skos:definition "The model of what a product domain is — its first-class entities, their life cycles, and the compliance obligations they carry — against which a backlog is scoped. Kept distinct from the backlog because a blueprint describes the domain and a feature list describes intentions about it; conflating them lets scope be invented at item-writing time instead of derived from the domain. A backlog is meaningful only relative to the blueprint it claims to cover."@en .

backlog:DomainEntity a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Domain Entity"@en ;
    skos:definition "A first-class thing the product is about — a client, a practitioner, a service, an appointment, a financial record. Each carries the full set of life-cycle stages that must be swept when checking coverage, so that a stage nobody thought to build for is a detectable absence rather than an unasked question."@en .

backlog:EntityLifecycleStage a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Stage_Creation backlog:Stage_ActiveUse backlog:Stage_SuspensionException backlog:Stage_Termination ) ;
    rdfs:label "Entity Lifecycle Stage"@en ;
    skos:definition "A stage in the real life of a domain entity: creation, active use, suspension or exception handling, and termination or closure. Closed enumeration of four individuals. Named EntityLifecycleStage rather than LifecycleStage to keep it unmistakably distinct from backlog:LifecycleState, which is the delivery state of a work item; the two are unrelated and a reader who confuses them will mis-model both."@en .

backlog:Stage_Creation a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:EntityLifecycleStage ;
    rdfs:label "Creation"@en ;
    skos:definition "The entity comes into existence: registration, intake, provisioning."@en .

backlog:Stage_ActiveUse a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:EntityLifecycleStage ;
    rdfs:label "Active use"@en ;
    skos:definition "The entity is operated on in the normal course of business."@en .

backlog:Stage_SuspensionException a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:EntityLifecycleStage ;
    rdfs:label "Suspension / exception"@en ;
    skos:definition "The entity is paused, blocked, cancelled, disputed or otherwise handled outside the happy path. The stage most often absent from a backlog, because titles describe the happy path."@en .

backlog:Stage_Termination a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:EntityLifecycleStage ;
    rdfs:label "Termination / closure"@en ;
    skos:definition "The entity leaves the system: deletion, archival, closure, settlement."@en .

[] a owl:AllDifferent ; owl:distinctMembers ( backlog:Stage_Creation backlog:Stage_ActiveUse
                                              backlog:Stage_SuspensionException backlog:Stage_Termination ) .

backlog:ComplianceObligation a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Compliance Obligation"@en ;
    skos:definition "A duty the domain imposes independently of any feature request — retention, consent, auditability, professional certification. Modelled in the blueprint so that an obligation without backlog coverage is a recorded gap rather than a discovery made during an audit."@en .

backlog:BlueprintGap a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Blueprint Gap"@en ;
    skos:definition "An explicitly recorded absence: a life-cycle stage or compliance obligation with no backlog coverage. Recorded as an individual because a gap is a defect in the register, not a silence in it; an unwritten gap is indistinguishable from a stage nobody ever considered."@en .

backlog:CapabilityClass a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Capability Class"@en ;
    skos:definition "A named, first-class grouping of capability that owns a distinct business concern — business continuity and support, access governance, data architecture. First-class because a concern scattered across three groupings with no owner is invisible to prioritisation; the classes a development uses are its own, so this class ships unpopulated by design and is populated in the adopting register."@en .

backlog:EnforcementDomain a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Enforcement Domain"@en ;
    skos:definition "How a capability's correctness is enforced — by an ontology and its constraints, by design review, or by code and tests — as distinct from what the capability does. Classifying by enforcement mode tells you which gate must pass before the capability can be believed. Left open and unpopulated here: the classification scheme belongs to the adopting development, which binds its own individuals rather than inheriting another project's vocabulary."@en .

backlog:hasDomainEntity a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Blueprint ; rdfs:range backlog:DomainEntity ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has domain entity"@en ;
    skos:definition "The first-class entities a blueprint models."@en .

backlog:hasObligation a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Blueprint ; rdfs:range backlog:ComplianceObligation ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has obligation"@en ;
    skos:definition "The compliance obligations a blueprint carries."@en .

backlog:blueprintFor a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Blueprint ; rdfs:range backlog:Backlog ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "blueprint for"@en ;
    skos:definition "The backlog register this blueprint scopes, so coverage can be computed between exactly one pair."@en .

backlog:coversEntity a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ; rdfs:range backlog:DomainEntity ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "covers entity"@en ;
    skos:definition "The domain entity this work item builds capability for."@en .

backlog:coversStage a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ; rdfs:range backlog:EntityLifecycleStage ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "covers stage"@en ;
    skos:definition "The life-cycle stage this work item covers for the entity it names. The pair (coversEntity, coversStage) is what the blueprint sweep measures; an uncovered pair must appear as a BlueprintGap."@en .

backlog:coversObligation a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ; rdfs:range backlog:ComplianceObligation ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "covers obligation"@en ;
    skos:definition "The compliance obligation this work item discharges."@en .

backlog:belongsToCapabilityClass a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:WorkItem backlog:WorkItemContainer ) ] ;
    rdfs:range backlog:CapabilityClass ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "belongs to capability class"@en ;
    skos:definition "The first-class business concern this item or container serves."@en .

backlog:hasEnforcementDomain a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:WorkItem backlog:WorkItemContainer ) ] ;
    rdfs:range backlog:EnforcementDomain ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has enforcement domain"@en ;
    skos:definition "How this item's or container's correctness is enforced."@en .

backlog:gapForEntity a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:BlueprintGap ; rdfs:range backlog:DomainEntity ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "gap for entity"@en ;
    skos:definition "The entity whose coverage is missing."@en .

backlog:gapForStage a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:BlueprintGap ; rdfs:range backlog:EntityLifecycleStage ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "gap for stage"@en ;
    skos:definition "The life-cycle stage whose coverage is missing."@en .

backlog:remediatedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:BlueprintGap ; rdfs:range backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "remediated by"@en ;
    skos:definition "The work item created to close this gap, when one exists. Absence means the gap is recorded but unowned — which is a visible state, not a silent one."@en .

backlog:hasGapReason a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:BlueprintGap ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has gap reason"@en ;
    skos:definition "Why the coverage is absent: out of scope, deferred, or simply not yet built. Required, because an unexplained gap cannot be triaged."@en .

backlog:verifiedAgainstCode a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "verified against code"@en ;
    skos:definition "True when this item's claimed coverage was checked against running code rather than inferred from its own title. Titles lie by omission — they describe what someone intended to build, not what the running system does — so a coverage claim that has only been read is materially weaker than one that has been executed."@en .

backlog:hasCodeVerificationNote a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has code verification note"@en ;
    skos:definition "What the check against running code actually found, including a divergence it disproved. Required whenever verifiedAgainstCode is asserted true, so the claim carries its method."@en .

#################################################################
#  11. Scoreability & gap discipline (v1.1.0)
#################################################################

backlog:notYetScoreable a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "not yet scoreable"@en ;
    skos:definition "An explicit declaration that this item cannot yet carry a priority score. Every open item must be either scored or flagged this way with a written reason; an item that is neither is a silent gap, which the suite treats as a defect in the register rather than as an acceptable blank. An honest 'not yet scoreable, and here is exactly why' outranks a fabricated number that makes a report look decisive."@en .

backlog:hasScoreabilityReason a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has scoreability reason"@en ;
    skos:definition "Why an item is not yet scoreable — genuinely undeveloped scope, an owner decision pending, a dependency whose cost is unknown. Required alongside the flag, because the flag without a reason is the silent gap wearing a label."@en .

#################################################################
#  12. Capability tagging & dependency disclosure (v1.1.0)
#################################################################

backlog:isBusinessCapability a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItemContainer ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "is business capability"@en ;
    skos:definition "True when a container is a coherent, independently shippable business capability; false when it is a real but non-shippable grouping — a bag of unrelated point fixes, or generic mechanics with no business meaning of their own. Containers tagged false are excluded from capability-level ranking rather than allowed to dilute it: ranking a bag of point fixes as a deployable capability is the same category error as ranking an undecomposed epic against real stories, one level up."@en .

backlog:DependencyDisclosure a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Dependency Disclosure"@en ;
    skos:definition "A record that a dependency was stated in prose or in a judgement — 'this only matters once X exists', 'this reuses the existing Y' — together with the edge it must become. The moment a dependency is said out loud it is a dependsOn edge, not a sentence: an undisclosed-but-real dependency is worse than no dependency modelling at all, because the register looks resolved. The suite requires the corresponding edge to exist."@en .

backlog:hasDependencyDisclosure a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:WorkItem backlog:WorkItemContainer ) ] ; rdfs:range backlog:DependencyDisclosure ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has dependency disclosure"@en ;
    skos:definition "A disclosure attached to the item whose dependency was stated."@en .

backlog:disclosesDependencyOn a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:DependencyDisclosure ;
    rdfs:range [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:WorkItem backlog:WorkItemContainer ) ] ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "discloses dependency on"@en ;
    skos:definition "The item the disclosure says is depended upon; the suite requires a matching dependsOn edge from the disclosing item."@en .

backlog:hasDisclosureSource a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:DependencyDisclosure ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has disclosure source"@en ;
    skos:definition "Where the dependency was stated — the sentence, the analysis, the conversation — so the edge can be traced back to the judgement that produced it."@en .

backlog:hasExternalBlocker a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItemContainer ; rdfs:range backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has external blocker"@en ;
    skos:definition "A not-Done item outside this container that a member depends on, computed by rule. Its existence is why full member completion is not deployability: being 100% member-complete is one input to job size, never a substitute for checking real dependency edges before calling anything ready."@en .

#################################################################
#  13. Launch-readiness model and ranking arbitration (v1.1.0)
#################################################################

backlog:Role a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Owner backlog:Builder ) ;
    rdfs:label "Role"@en ;
    skos:definition "Who is entitled to make a given decision. Closed enumeration of two individuals, because the framework's central governance split is between decisions that require business judgement and decisions that are computed or reasoned; blurring them is how a computed ranking ends up overriding a business call nobody delegated."@en .

backlog:Owner a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:Role ;
    rdfs:label "Owner"@en ;
    skos:definition "Makes the calls that genuinely require business judgement — what is in launch scope, which live defect blocks launch versus can be deferred, single-tenant versus multi-tenant. These are never computed, and silence is never approval of a scope decision that was not actually given."@en .

backlog:Builder a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:Role ;
    rdfs:label "Builder"@en ;
    skos:definition "Performs the multi-factor scoring judgements with written rationale, maintains the gates and tooling, and surfaces every disclosed dependency and recorded gap for the owner's visibility rather than resolving them quietly."@en .

backlog:isLaunchGate a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:WorkItem backlog:WorkItemContainer ) ] ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "is launch gate"@en ;
    skos:definition "True when this must be complete before first deployment. A launch gate answers a categorically different question from a throughput score — what must be done before launch, in what order — and is therefore an owner declaration, never a value derived from any ranking."@en .

backlog:hasLaunchPriority a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:WorkItem backlog:WorkItemContainer ) ] ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has launch priority"@en ;
    skos:definition "The owner-declared order in which launch gates must clear, lowest first. An ordinal business decision, not a computed score, and never reconciled with a throughput score by arithmetic."@en .

backlog:decidedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:WorkItem backlog:WorkItemContainer backlog:PriorityScore
                                              backlog:Goal backlog:Objective backlog:ScopeExclusion backlog:Opportunity ) ] ;
    rdfs:range backlog:Role ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "decided by"@en ;
    skos:definition "Which role made this decision. The suite requires launch-gate declarations to be decided by the Owner and container-level scores to be decided by the Builder, so a computed number can never quietly occupy a slot reserved for business judgement."@en .

backlog:hasDecisionRationale a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:WorkItem backlog:WorkItemContainer backlog:PriorityScore ) ] ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has decision rationale"@en ;
    skos:definition "The written reasoning behind an owner declaration or a multi-factor judgement, kept with the decision so a later 'why is X ahead of Y' has a citable answer rather than a fresh opinion."@en .

backlog:isAveragedFromMembers a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:PriorityScore ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "is averaged from members"@en ;
    skos:definition "True when a container-level score was produced by averaging member scores. The suite rejects it: averaging re-uses numbers computed for unrelated reasons on unrelated items and is not a business-value judgement about the capability as a whole. A container score must be a genuine multi-factor judgement carrying its own rationale."@en .

backlog:RankingModel a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:ThroughputRanking backlog:LaunchScopedRanking ) ;
    rdfs:label "Ranking Model"@en ;
    skos:definition "Which question a ranking answers. Closed enumeration of two individuals, kept explicit because conflating them is the most expensive recurring error this framework guards against: the two models answer different questions and will disagree, and the disagreement must stay visible rather than being resolved silently in one direction."@en .

backlog:ThroughputRanking a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:RankingModel ;
    rdfs:label "Throughput ranking"@en ;
    skos:definition "Ranks the whole register by score under a named method, answering what the best next increment of capacity is for an already-live product. It divides value by cost, so a large essential capability can and will rank below a small cheap one — correct for this question, wrong for the launch question."@en .

backlog:LaunchScopedRanking a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:RankingModel ;
    rdfs:label "Launch-scoped ranking"@en ;
    skos:definition "Ranks only within the active launch-priority gate plus cross-cutting prerequisites, answering what must clear before first deployment. It collapses back to throughput ranking automatically once every launch gate is closed, so the scoping is a state of the register rather than a mode someone must remember to switch off."@en .

[] a owl:AllDifferent ; owl:distinctMembers ( backlog:ThroughputRanking backlog:LaunchScopedRanking ) .

#################################################################
#  14. Governed documents & supersession (v1.1.0)
#################################################################

backlog:GovernedDocument a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Governed Document"@en ;
    skos:definition "A document whose currency is tracked — a standard, a report, a methodology note. Tracked because anyone following an old link must see why it is stale and where the live source is now, rather than a missing file or, worse, content that is silently wrong."@en .

backlog:DocumentStatus a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Live backlog:Superseded ) ;
    rdfs:label "Document Status"@en ;
    skos:definition "Whether a governed document is current or retired. Closed enumeration of two individuals; a retired document is marked in place, never deleted."@en .

backlog:Live a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:DocumentStatus ;
    rdfs:label "Live"@en ;
    skos:definition "Current and safe to act on."@en .

backlog:Superseded a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:DocumentStatus ;
    rdfs:label "Superseded"@en ;
    skos:definition "Retired but retained in place, carrying a pointer to what replaced it and the reason, so the trail from an old reference to the live source stays walkable."@en .

[] a owl:AllDifferent ; owl:distinctMembers ( backlog:Live backlog:Superseded ) .

backlog:hasDocumentStatus a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:GovernedDocument ; rdfs:range backlog:DocumentStatus ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has document status"@en ;
    skos:definition "The single currency status of a governed document."@en .

backlog:supersededBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:GovernedDocument ; rdfs:range backlog:GovernedDocument ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "superseded by"@en ;
    skos:definition "What replaced this document. Required whenever the status is Superseded, because a retirement marker without a forward pointer sends the reader nowhere."@en .

backlog:hasSupersessionReason a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:GovernedDocument ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has supersession reason"@en ;
    skos:definition "Why this document was retired, kept alongside the replacement rather than deleted with it."@en .

#################################################################
#  15. Computed roadmap report (v1.1.0)
#################################################################

backlog:RoadmapReport a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:RoadmapElement ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Roadmap Report"@en ;
    skos:definition "One execution of the roadmap computation over a register, carrying its run timestamp and the sections it produced. A report is an event, not a document: the moment a computed value is copied into prose it begins decaying, and a report that is recalled rather than re-run is a memory of a decision, not a decision."@en .

backlog:ReportSection a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Report Section"@en ;
    skos:definition "A section a compliant roadmap report must contain. Enumerated as individuals rather than left to the reporting script, so a section quietly dropped from an implementation is a detectable omission rather than an absence nobody notices."@en .

backlog:reportOf a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:RoadmapReport ; rdfs:range backlog:Backlog ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "report of"@en ;
    skos:definition "The register this report was computed from."@en .

backlog:hasSection a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:RoadmapReport ; rdfs:range backlog:ReportSection ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has section"@en ;
    skos:definition "A section this run actually produced."@en .

backlog:hasRunTimestamp a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:RoadmapReport ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has run timestamp"@en ;
    skos:definition "When this report was computed. The property that makes a stale answer detectable: a recommendation is traceable to the run that produced it, and a run from an earlier turn is not evidence about the register now."@en .

backlog:derivedInReport a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ; rdfs:range backlog:RoadmapReport ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "derived in report"@en ;
    skos:definition "Records that this item was named as next by that report run. An answer to 'what should we do next' is only as good as the run behind it, so the recommendation points at its run rather than standing on its own."@en .

backlog:underRankingModel a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ; rdfs:range backlog:RankingModel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "under ranking model"@en ;
    skos:definition "Which of the two models produced this recommendation, so the two answers stay distinguishable when they disagree."@en .

#################################################################
#  16. Methodology rules & release gates (v1.1.0)
#################################################################

backlog:MethodologyRule a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Methodology Rule"@en ;
    skos:definition "A governing rule of the method itself, carrying permanently the disagreement or defect it closes, its exact logic, and the concrete incident that surfaced it. A rule is never silently replaced: the reasoning for superseding an earlier rule is kept beside the new one, because a rule stripped of its incident becomes a convention nobody can argue with or retire."@en .

backlog:hasRuleLogic a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:MethodologyRule ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has rule logic"@en ;
    skos:definition "The exact behaviour the rule mandates, stated precisely enough to implement or refute."@en .

backlog:closesDisagreement a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:MethodologyRule ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "closes disagreement"@en ;
    skos:definition "The recurring dispute or defect this rule settles, so its purpose survives the people who remember the argument."@en .

backlog:hasMotivatingIncident a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:MethodologyRule ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has motivating incident"@en ;
    skos:definition "The concrete failure that produced the rule. Required: a rule without an incident is theoretical best practice, and theoretical best practice is the first thing dropped under pressure."@en .

backlog:supersedesRule a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:MethodologyRule ; rdfs:range backlog:MethodologyRule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "supersedes rule"@en ;
    skos:definition "The earlier rule this one replaces, retained rather than deleted."@en .

backlog:ReleaseGate a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Release Gate"@en ;
    skos:definition "One check in the release sequence that must pass before any state is trusted. Modelled as individuals carrying their executable command so the gate set is itself inspectable, and a gate quietly skipped by a script is a detectable difference rather than an invisible one."@en .

backlog:hasGateCommand a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ReleaseGate ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has gate command"@en ;
    skos:definition "The command that executes this gate."@en .

backlog:hasGateOrder a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ReleaseGate ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has gate order"@en ;
    skos:definition "Where this gate sits in the sequence; earlier gates guard the inputs the later ones assume."@en .

#################################################################
#  Disjointness for the v1.1.0 additions
#################################################################

[] a owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( backlog:Blueprint backlog:DomainEntity backlog:EntityLifecycleStage
                 backlog:ComplianceObligation backlog:BlueprintGap backlog:CapabilityClass
                 backlog:EnforcementDomain backlog:DependencyDisclosure backlog:Role
                 backlog:RankingModel backlog:GovernedDocument backlog:DocumentStatus
                 backlog:ReportSection backlog:MethodologyRule backlog:ReleaseGate ) .

#################################################################
#  17. Intent layer: goals, objectives, benefits, opportunities (v1.2.0)
#     Why the work exists, and what its completion is supposed to buy.
#     Kept above the register because a backlog without stated intent
#     can be delivered in full and still deliver nothing.
#################################################################

backlog:Goal a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Goal"@en ;
    skos:definition "A durable outcome the product or programme exists to achieve, stated in terms of the change it makes for someone rather than the functionality that delivers it. Owner-decided and rarely revised; objectives are the measurable steps toward it. Modelled so that every priority argument can terminate in something other than opinion: an item's value claim is ultimately a claim about a goal."@en .

backlog:Objective a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Objective"@en ;
    skos:definition "A measurable, time-bounded target that advances a goal, carrying at least one success metric with a baseline and a target value. The suite refuses an objective without a metric: an objective that cannot be measured cannot be missed, and an objective that cannot be missed does not constrain any decision."@en .

backlog:Benefit a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Benefit"@en ;
    skos:definition "A specific improvement expected to follow from completing work, owned by a named stakeholder and attached to the objective it advances. Benefits are claimed before delivery and realised after it, and the framework treats a realisation claim exactly as it treats a Done claim: it must point at verified Evidence, because an unevidenced benefit is the most expensive kind of prose in a portfolio."@en .

backlog:Opportunity a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Opportunity"@en ;
    skos:definition "An identified upside uncertainty that is not yet committed work — the mirror of a risk in the ISO 31000 sense, where uncertainty may have positive as well as negative effect on objectives. Held separately from the register so that unfunded ideas are visible without inflating scope, and converted into a work item explicitly when someone decides to pursue it."@en .

backlog:contributesToGoal a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Objective ; rdfs:range backlog:Goal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "contributes to goal"@en ;
    skos:definition "The goal this objective advances."@en .

backlog:pursuesObjective a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:WorkItem backlog:WorkItemContainer ) ] ;
    rdfs:range backlog:Objective ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "pursues objective"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective this work is meant to advance. The link that makes 'why are we building this' answerable from the register rather than from memory."@en .

backlog:hasSuccessMetric a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Objective ; rdfs:range core:Metric ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has success metric"@en ;
    skos:definition "The metric by which this objective is judged, referenced in the pack's core metric vocabulary rather than re-declared locally, so measurement stays one subject's responsibility. The declared range is NOMINAL, not enforced: no shape at any conformance level applies sh:class to this property, deliberately, because core is a separately-adopted OE subject and an adopter that has not vendored it is not thereby out of conformance. An L1 adopter may point this property at a project-local metric class and remain fully conformant; an adopter that has vendored core should point it at core:Metric so that measurement semantics stay in the subject that owns them. Stated explicitly after an adopting project derived the same conclusion independently and disclosed its local substitution as a workaround — it was not a workaround, it was the intended latitude, and the intent had never been written down."@en .

backlog:hasBaselineValue a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Objective ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has baseline value"@en ;
    skos:definition "Where the metric stood when the objective was set. Without it, a target is a number with no claim attached."@en .

backlog:hasTargetValue a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Objective ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has target value"@en ;
    skos:definition "Where the metric must stand for the objective to be met."@en .

backlog:hasObjectiveDeadline a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Objective ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has objective deadline"@en ;
    skos:definition "When the target must be met by; an objective with no horizon cannot be missed on time."@en .

backlog:expectsBenefit a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:WorkItem backlog:WorkItemContainer ) ] ;
    rdfs:range backlog:Benefit ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "expects benefit"@en ;
    skos:definition "The benefit this work is expected to produce."@en .

backlog:benefitFor a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Benefit ; rdfs:range backlog:Objective ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "benefit for"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective this benefit advances; a benefit that advances no objective is a preference, not a benefit."@en .

backlog:hasBenefitOwner a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Benefit ; rdfs:range core:Stakeholder ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has benefit owner"@en ;
    skos:definition "The stakeholder accountable for the benefit actually arriving, referenced in the pack's core stakeholder vocabulary. Unowned benefits are never chased."@en .

backlog:hasBenefitStatement a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Benefit ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has benefit statement"@en ;
    skos:definition "What improves, for whom, and how it will be observed."@en .

backlog:benefitRealized a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Benefit ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "benefit realized"@en ;
    skos:definition "Asserted true only when the improvement has been observed after delivery, not when the work shipped. Shipping is an input to a benefit, never the benefit itself."@en .

backlog:benefitRealizedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Benefit ; rdfs:range backlog:Evidence ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "benefit realized by"@en ;
    skos:definition "The verified Evidence supporting a realisation claim — a measurement, a report, a dated observation. The suite requires it before benefitRealized may be true, on the same principle that governs Done."@en .

backlog:opportunityFor a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Opportunity ;
    rdfs:range [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:Goal backlog:Objective ) ] ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "opportunity for"@en ;
    skos:definition "The goal or objective this opportunity would advance if pursued."@en .

backlog:hasOpportunityStatement a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Opportunity ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has opportunity statement"@en ;
    skos:definition "What the upside is, what would have to be true to capture it, and what it would cost to find out."@en .

backlog:convertedToItem a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Opportunity ; rdfs:range backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "converted to item"@en ;
    skos:definition "The work item created when someone decided to pursue this opportunity, so the decision to fund an idea is visible as an event rather than as an item appearing in the register with no history."@en .

#################################################################
#  18. Scope (v1.2.0)
#################################################################

backlog:ScopeStatement a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Scope Statement"@en ;
    skos:definition "The declared boundary of a delivery effort: what it covers and, just as importantly, what it has been decided not to cover. Attached to a container so that 'in scope' is a checkable membership question rather than a recollection of a conversation."@en .

backlog:ScopeExclusion a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Scope Exclusion"@en ;
    skos:definition "An explicit, owner-decided record that a concern is out of scope, with the reasoning kept beside it. Exclusions are recorded rather than left implicit because the cheapest scope decision to re-litigate is the one nobody wrote down, and because an unrecorded exclusion is indistinguishable from an oversight."@en .

backlog:hasScopeStatement a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItemContainer ; rdfs:range backlog:ScopeStatement ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has scope statement"@en ;
    skos:definition "The declared boundary governing this container."@en .

backlog:hasScopeExclusion a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ScopeStatement ; rdfs:range backlog:ScopeExclusion ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has scope exclusion"@en ;
    skos:definition "A concern this scope statement deliberately excludes."@en .

backlog:hasScopeStatementText a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ScopeStatement ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has scope statement text"@en ;
    skos:definition "The boundary in prose, for the reader; the machine-checkable part is membership and the exclusion list."@en .

backlog:excludesConcern a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ScopeExclusion ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "excludes concern"@en ;
    skos:definition "What is excluded, named precisely enough that someone can tell whether a later request falls inside it."@en .

backlog:hasExclusionRationale a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ScopeExclusion ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has exclusion rationale"@en ;
    skos:definition "Why it is excluded. Required: an exclusion without a reason cannot be reviewed when circumstances change, only reversed by whoever argues hardest."@en .

#################################################################
#  19. Refinement / grooming (v1.2.0)
#################################################################

backlog:RefinementEvent a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Refinement Event"@en ;
    skos:definition "A dated act of making an item ready: adding acceptance criteria, sizing it, resolving an ambiguity, splitting it. Recorded as an event because readiness is something that was done to an item at a moment, by someone, not a property it acquires by sitting in the register long enough. The suite refuses the Ready state to an item with no refinement on record."@en .

backlog:refines a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:RefinementEvent ; rdfs:range backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "refines"@en ;
    skos:definition "The item this refinement acted on."@en .

backlog:refinedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:RefinementEvent ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "refined at"@en ;
    skos:definition "When the refinement happened, so staleness is detectable when the item has since changed."@en .

backlog:hasRefinementOutcome a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:RefinementEvent ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has refinement outcome"@en ;
    skos:definition "What actually changed — criteria added, item split, estimate agreed, question raised. A refinement with no outcome was a meeting, not a refinement."@en .

backlog:refinedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:RefinementEvent ; rdfs:range backlog:Role ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "refined by"@en ;
    skos:definition "Which role performed the refinement."@en .

#################################################################
#  20. Cost, risk and opportunity binding (v1.2.0)
#################################################################

backlog:CostEstimate a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Cost Estimate"@en ;
    skos:definition "An estimate of what work will cost, carrying its basis and its confidence rather than a naked number. Deliberately unit-neutral — effort, money and elapsed time are all recorded through the same structure — because the discipline the framework enforces is that an estimate discloses how it was derived, not which currency it is in."@en .

backlog:hasCostEstimate a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:WorkItem backlog:WorkItemContainer ) ] ;
    rdfs:range backlog:CostEstimate ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has cost estimate"@en ;
    skos:definition "The cost estimate attached to this work."@en .

backlog:hasEffortEstimate a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:CostEstimate ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has effort estimate"@en ;
    skos:definition "The estimated quantity, in whatever unit the estimating basis names."@en .

backlog:hasCostBasis a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:CostEstimate ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has cost basis"@en ;
    skos:definition "How the number was derived — analogous estimation from a named prior item, three-point, expert judgement, measured throughput — and in what unit. Required: an estimate whose basis is unstated cannot be challenged, improved, or reused."@en .

backlog:hasEstimateConfidence a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:CostEstimate ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has estimate confidence"@en ;
    skos:definition "How well-founded the estimate is, as a fraction, so that a guess and a measurement are not presented as the same kind of thing."@en .

backlog:estimatedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:CostEstimate ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "estimated at"@en ;
    skos:definition "When the estimate was made; estimates are snapshots of what was known then."@en .

backlog:hasRisk a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:WorkItem backlog:WorkItemContainer backlog:Objective ) ] ;
    rdfs:range risk:Risk ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has risk"@en ;
    skos:definition "A risk bearing on this work or objective, referenced in the pack's risk subject rather than re-declared here. That subject defines risk as the effect of uncertainty on objectives per ISO 31000:2018, which is exactly the notion delivery work needs, so this framework delegates to it instead of minting a parallel vocabulary."@en .

backlog:hasMitigation a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain risk:Risk ; rdfs:range risk:Mitigation ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has mitigation"@en ;
    skos:definition "The action taken to modify a referenced risk, again in the risk subject's own vocabulary. Declared here because this framework needs the link for its own constraint that an untreated risk must be explicitly accepted; the classes at both ends belong to the risk subject."@en .

backlog:riskAcceptedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain risk:Risk ; rdfs:range backlog:Role ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "risk accepted by"@en ;
    skos:definition "The role that accepted a risk without treating it. Every referenced risk must either carry a mitigation or name its acceptor: silent acceptance is the failure mode this exists to prevent, and only the Owner may accept a risk affecting scope or launch."@en .

#################################################################
#  21. Investment mix: building versus maintaining (v1.2.0)
#################################################################

backlog:InvestmentCategory a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Cat_NewCapability backlog:Cat_Maintenance backlog:Cat_TechnicalDebt backlog:Cat_Compliance ) ;
    rdfs:label "Investment Category"@en ;
    skos:definition "What kind of investment an item represents, as a closed set of four: new capability, maintenance, technical debt, and compliance. Priority scores compare items within a shared question; they cannot arbitrate between categories, because a maintenance backlog and a new-capability backlog compete for the same capacity while answering to different arguments. Categorising makes the mix visible and lets a policy govern it instead of the ranking silently starving one category."@en .

backlog:Cat_NewCapability a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:InvestmentCategory ;
    rdfs:label "New capability"@en ;
    skos:definition "Work that adds something the product could not do before. Dominates before first launch and competes for capacity afterwards."@en .

backlog:Cat_Maintenance a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:InvestmentCategory ;
    rdfs:label "Maintenance"@en ;
    skos:definition "Work that keeps what exists working: defect repair, dependency upkeep, operational support. Structurally disadvantaged by value-over-cost ranking, which is why it needs a policy share rather than a fair fight."@en .

backlog:Cat_TechnicalDebt a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:InvestmentCategory ;
    rdfs:label "Technical debt"@en ;
    skos:definition "Work that reduces the cost of future work rather than delivering user-visible value now. Its benefit is a change in someone else's job size, which no single item's score can express."@en .

backlog:Cat_Compliance a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:InvestmentCategory ;
    rdfs:label "Compliance"@en ;
    skos:definition "Work required by an obligation external to the product's own goals. Ranks by deadline and consequence, not by value per unit effort."@en .

[] a owl:AllDifferent ; owl:distinctMembers ( backlog:Cat_NewCapability backlog:Cat_Maintenance
                                              backlog:Cat_TechnicalDebt backlog:Cat_Compliance ) .

backlog:hasInvestmentCategory a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ; rdfs:range backlog:InvestmentCategory ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has investment category"@en ;
    skos:definition "The single category this item's investment belongs to."@en .

backlog:ProductLifecyclePhase a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Phase_PreLaunch backlog:Phase_Live backlog:Phase_Sunsetting ) ;
    rdfs:label "Product Lifecycle Phase"@en ;
    skos:definition "Where the product stands, as a closed set of three. The phase decides which prioritisation question is the governing one: before first deployment the launch-readiness model governs and throughput ranking is advisory; once live the throughput model governs and launch scoping no longer applies. Recording the phase makes that switch a fact about the product rather than a habit of whoever runs the report."@en .

backlog:Phase_PreLaunch a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ProductLifecyclePhase ;
    rdfs:label "Pre-launch"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nothing has been deployed to real users yet; launch gates govern the order of work."@en .

backlog:Phase_Live a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ProductLifecyclePhase ;
    rdfs:label "Live"@en ;
    skos:definition "The product is in real use; ongoing capacity is allocated by throughput ranking within the policy mix, and maintenance is no longer a residual category."@en .

backlog:Phase_Sunsetting a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ProductLifecyclePhase ;
    rdfs:label "Sunsetting"@en ;
    skos:definition "The product is being withdrawn; new capability work requires an explicit owner decision, and compliance and migration work dominate."@en .

[] a owl:AllDifferent ; owl:distinctMembers ( backlog:Phase_PreLaunch backlog:Phase_Live backlog:Phase_Sunsetting ) .

backlog:hasLifecyclePhase a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItemContainer ; rdfs:range backlog:ProductLifecyclePhase ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has lifecycle phase"@en ;
    skos:definition "The phase this register or project is in."@en .

backlog:PortfolioPolicy a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Portfolio Policy"@en ;
    skos:definition "A declared intent about how capacity is split across investment categories, so that the split is a decision someone made rather than an outcome nobody chose. The report compares declared shares against the register's actual mix and reports the difference; it does not adjust either."@en .

backlog:CapacityAllocation a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Capacity Allocation"@en ;
    skos:definition "One category's declared share of capacity within a portfolio policy."@en .

backlog:policyFor a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:PortfolioPolicy ; rdfs:range backlog:WorkItemContainer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "policy for"@en ;
    skos:definition "The register or project this policy governs."@en .

backlog:hasAllocation a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:PortfolioPolicy ; rdfs:range backlog:CapacityAllocation ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has allocation"@en ;
    skos:definition "A per-category share declared by this policy."@en .

backlog:allocatesToCategory a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:CapacityAllocation ; rdfs:range backlog:InvestmentCategory ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "allocates to category"@en ;
    skos:definition "The category this allocation applies to."@en .

backlog:hasTargetShare a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:CapacityAllocation ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has target share"@en ;
    skos:definition "The intended fraction of capacity for the category, between zero and one. Shares across a policy must sum to one: a policy that does not account for all capacity leaves the remainder to be allocated by whoever shouts."@en .

#################################################################
#  22. Implementation project (v1.2.0)
#################################################################

backlog:ImplementationProject a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:WorkItemContainer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Implementation Project"@en ;
    skos:definition "A funded, bounded delivery effort with a scope statement, a lifecycle phase, objectives it pursues and a Definition of Done it must satisfy before it can be called finished. Distinct from an Increment, which is a releasable body of work at a point in time: a project may ship many increments, and an increment may serve several projects. Introduced so that the framework's gates apply to the effort as a whole and not only to its individual items — the level at which most delivery claims are actually made and least often checked."@en .

[] a owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( backlog:Goal backlog:Objective backlog:Benefit backlog:Opportunity
                 backlog:ScopeStatement backlog:ScopeExclusion backlog:RefinementEvent
                 backlog:CostEstimate backlog:InvestmentCategory backlog:ProductLifecyclePhase
                 backlog:PortfolioPolicy backlog:CapacityAllocation ) .

#################################################################
#  23. Container-level dependency (v1.3.0)
#################################################################

backlog:containerDependsOn a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:TransitiveProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItemContainer ; rdfs:range backlog:WorkItemContainer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "container depends on"@en ;
    skos:definition "A declared dependency of one deployable unit on another: this package cannot ship until that one has. Distinct from the item-level dependsOn because a package may depend on another for reasons no single pair of items expresses — a shared migration, a contractual sequence, an integration window. Transitive, so indirect blocking is visible; cycles are caught by constraint rather than by reasoner, for the same OWL 2 DL profile reason as dependsOn."@en .

backlog:derivedContainerDependency a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItemContainer ; rdfs:range backlog:WorkItemContainer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "derived container dependency"@en ;
    skos:definition "A container dependency computed by rule from member-level edges: some member of this container depends on a not-yet-Done member of that one. Kept separate from the declared property so that the two can disagree visibly — a declared dependency with no member-level basis is either a real constraint someone must justify or a phantom, and a member-level dependency never declared at container level is a surprise waiting for a release."@en .

backlog:rankedOnRoadmap a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:WorkItem backlog:WorkItemContainer ) ] ; rdfs:range backlog:Roadmap ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "ranked on roadmap"@en ;
    skos:definition "The roadmap on which this container has been placed. A container without this link may still be scored, but it is not on anyone's plan."@en .

backlog:hasRoadmapRank a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:WorkItem backlog:WorkItemContainer ) ] ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has roadmap rank"@en ;
    skos:definition "The container's declared position in the roadmap sequence, lowest first. A decision, not a computation: the report prints it beside the rank the scores imply, and where the two disagree it shows both rather than reconciling them, on the same principle that keeps the throughput and launch-scoped answers separate."@en .

#################################################################
#  24. Test harness — proving Done rather than declaring it (v1.3.0)
#################################################################

backlog:TestHarness a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Test Harness"@en ;
    skos:definition "The set of checks that together prove one work item satisfies both its acceptance criteria and the Definition of Done that governs it. A first-class artifact rather than an implicit relationship, because 'the tests pass' is the claim most often made and least often traced: a harness names which check proves which criterion, so a criterion nobody tested is a visible hole rather than an assumption. Its completeness is derived, never asserted."@en .

backlog:harnessFor a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:TestHarness ; rdfs:range backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "harness for"@en ;
    skos:definition "The single work item this harness proves."@en .

backlog:hasHarnessEvidence a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:TestHarness ; rdfs:range backlog:Evidence ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has harness evidence"@en ;
    skos:definition "An evidence artifact belonging to this harness. Each must be bridge-verified before it counts toward completeness."@en .

backlog:attestsCriterion a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Evidence ; rdfs:range backlog:AcceptanceCriterion ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "attests criterion"@en ;
    skos:definition "The acceptance criterion this evidence proves. The link that turns a passing test into an argument about the right thing: without it, a suite can be green while the criterion everyone cared about is untested."@en .

backlog:attestsDoDCriterion a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Evidence ; rdfs:range backlog:DoDCriterion ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "attests Definition-of-Done criterion"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Definition-of-Done clause this evidence discharges for the item, where the clause is item-specific rather than checked register-wide by query."@en .

backlog:harnessComplete a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:TestHarness ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "harness complete"@en ;
    skos:definition "Derived true only when every acceptance criterion of the harnessed item is attested by at least one bridge-verified evidence artifact. Written by the derivation rules, never by hand; at conformance level L3 an item cannot hold Done without it, which is what makes completion a proof rather than a statement."@en .

backlog:effectiveDefinitionOfDone a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ; rdfs:range backlog:DefinitionOfDone ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "effective definition of done"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Definition of Done that actually governs an item, derived from the item's own declaration or inherited from a container it belongs to. Derived rather than required item by item, because in practice a Definition of Done is a team-level agreement and restating it on every item is how the two drift apart."@en .

#################################################################
#  25. Lifecycle workflow: which transitions are permitted (v1.3.0)
#################################################################

backlog:Workflow a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Workflow"@en ;
    skos:definition "The set of state transitions a register permits, making the lifecycle a graph someone can inspect rather than five words that appear in reports. A closed state enumeration says which states exist; a workflow says which moves between them are legal, under what guard, and by whom — the difference between a status field and a process."@en .

backlog:StateTransition a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "State Transition"@en ;
    skos:definition "One permitted move from a lifecycle state to another, carrying the guard that must hold for the move to be legitimate. Guards are executable text for the same reason Definition-of-Done criteria are: a precondition nobody can run is a precondition nobody enforces."@en .

backlog:TransitionEvent a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Transition Event"@en ;
    skos:definition "A record that an item actually moved: which transition was used, when, and by which role. Recorded because a state with no history cannot be audited — it is indistinguishable from a value someone typed — and because the current state can then be checked against the last move rather than trusted."@en .

backlog:workflowFor a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Workflow ; rdfs:range backlog:WorkItemContainer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "workflow for"@en ;
    skos:definition "The register or project this workflow governs."@en .

backlog:hasTransition a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Workflow ; rdfs:range backlog:StateTransition ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has transition"@en ;
    skos:definition "A move this workflow permits."@en .

backlog:fromState a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:StateTransition ; rdfs:range backlog:LifecycleState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "from state"@en ;
    skos:definition "The state the transition leaves."@en .

backlog:toState a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:StateTransition ; rdfs:range backlog:LifecycleState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "to state"@en ;
    skos:definition "The state the transition enters."@en .

backlog:hasTransitionName a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:StateTransition ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has transition name"@en ;
    skos:definition "What the move is called in the team's own language — refine, start, complete, withdraw — so the workflow reads as a process rather than as a table of state pairs."@en .

backlog:hasTransitionGuard a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:StateTransition ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has transition guard"@en ;
    skos:definition "The executable condition that must hold before the move is legitimate. Required on every transition: a guard stated in prose is a hope, and an unguarded transition into Done is how completion claims escape."@en .

backlog:transitionedItem a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:TransitionEvent ; rdfs:range backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "transitioned item"@en ;
    skos:definition "The item that moved."@en .

backlog:viaTransition a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:TransitionEvent ; rdfs:range backlog:StateTransition ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "via transition"@en ;
    skos:definition "The permitted transition this move used. A recorded move that names no permitted transition is a state change outside the process, which the suite treats as a violation rather than as an exception."@en .

backlog:transitionedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:TransitionEvent ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "transitioned at"@en ;
    skos:definition "When the move happened; the ordering that lets the latest move be compared with the current state."@en .

backlog:transitionedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:TransitionEvent ; rdfs:range backlog:Role ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "transitioned by"@en ;
    skos:definition "The role that made the move."@en .

backlog:hasTransitionNote a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:TransitionEvent ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has transition note"@en ;
    skos:definition "What was true at the moment of the move — which check passed, what was decided — kept with the event rather than in a comment thread."@en .

[] a owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( backlog:TestHarness backlog:Workflow backlog:StateTransition backlog:TransitionEvent ) .

#################################################################
#  26. Decomposition (v1.4.0)
#     Every agile ladder — epic to feature to story — is a
#     part-whole relation, and the framework had none: kinds were
#     disjoint and only dependency connected items.
#################################################################

backlog:decomposesInto a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ; rdfs:range backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "decomposes into"@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates a larger work item to the smaller items it breaks down into: an initiative into epics, an epic into features, a feature into stories. Distinct from dependency, which orders independent work; decomposition says the child IS part of the parent, so the parent cannot be complete while a child is open and the parent's value is not additional to its children's. Deliberately not transitive: a story is part of its feature, and treating it as directly part of the initiative would make roll-up double-count at every level."@en .

backlog:partOf a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    owl:inverseOf backlog:decomposesInto ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ; rdfs:range backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "part of"@en ;
    skos:definition "Inverse of decomposesInto, declared once and in this direction only."@en .

backlog:decompositionState a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ; rdfs:range backlog:LifecycleState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "decomposition state"@en ;
    skos:definition "The state a decomposed item's children imply, computed by rule in the same way a container's state is. A parent whose asserted state disagrees with what its children support is the classic undecomposed-epic error: it inherits one shipped slice's optimism and misrepresents the remaining work."@en .

#################################################################
#  27. Artifact commitments (v1.4.0, Scrum Guide 2020)
#################################################################

backlog:Commitment a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Commitment"@en ;
    skos:definition "The thing an artifact commits to, against which its progress can be measured: a backlog commits to a product goal, an iteration to an iteration goal, an increment to a Definition of Done. Modelled as a first-class link rather than left implicit because an artifact with no commitment is a list, and a list cannot be behind schedule — it can only be long."@en .

backlog:hasCommitment a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItemContainer ; rdfs:range backlog:Commitment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has commitment"@en ;
    skos:definition "The commitment this artifact carries."@en .

backlog:commitsToGoal a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Commitment ; rdfs:range backlog:Goal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "commits to goal"@en ;
    skos:definition "The goal a backlog-level commitment names — the long-term objective the register exists to reach."@en .

backlog:commitsToObjective a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Commitment ; rdfs:range backlog:Objective ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "commits to objective"@en ;
    skos:definition "The measurable objective an iteration-level commitment names: the single outcome the time box is for, as opposed to the list of items selected into it."@en .

backlog:commitsToDefinitionOfDone a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Commitment ; rdfs:range backlog:DefinitionOfDone ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "commits to Definition of Done"@en ;
    skos:definition "The quality bar an increment-level commitment names. Work that does not meet it is not part of the increment, which is what makes the increment releasable by construction rather than by inspection."@en .

#################################################################
#  28. Dependency typing (v1.4.0, after Strode's dependency taxonomy)
#################################################################

backlog:DependencyKind a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Dep_Knowledge backlog:Dep_Task backlog:Dep_Resource ) ;
    rdfs:label "Dependency Kind"@en ;
    skos:definition "What kind of constraint a dependency actually is, as a closed set of three: knowledge, task and resource. An untyped dependency tells you that something must wait, but not what would release it — and the three kinds are released by different actions, which is why treating them alike produces plans that cannot be unblocked."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Strode, D.E., A dependency taxonomy for agile software development projects, Information Systems Frontiers 18(1), 23-46, 2016, DOI 10.1007/s10796-015-9574-1."@en .

backlog:Dep_Knowledge a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:DependencyKind ;
    rdfs:label "Knowledge dependency"@en ;
    skos:definition "Work waits on information someone holds or must produce — a decision, a specification, expertise. Released by a conversation or a spike, not by scheduling."@en .

backlog:Dep_Task a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:DependencyKind ;
    rdfs:label "Task dependency"@en ;
    skos:definition "Work waits on other work being finished first. The only kind an ordering constraint alone can express, and the kind most tools assume is the only one."@en .

backlog:Dep_Resource a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:DependencyKind ;
    rdfs:label "Resource dependency"@en ;
    skos:definition "Work waits on a person, environment, licence or component being available. Released by allocation, not by completing anything."@en .

[] a owl:AllDifferent ; owl:distinctMembers ( backlog:Dep_Knowledge backlog:Dep_Task backlog:Dep_Resource ) .

backlog:Dependency a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Dependency"@en ;
    skos:definition "A reified dependency, used when the constraint needs its own kind, owner or expected release date. The plain dependsOn edge remains for the common case; this class exists so that a dependency someone must actively clear is a thing with a state rather than an arrow."@en .

backlog:hasDependencyRecord a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ; rdfs:range backlog:Dependency ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has dependency record"@en ;
    skos:definition "A reified dependency bearing on this item."@en .

backlog:dependencyOn a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Dependency ;
    rdfs:range [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:WorkItem backlog:WorkItemContainer ) ] ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "dependency on"@en ;
    skos:definition "What the dependency is on."@en .

backlog:hasDependencyKind a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Dependency ; rdfs:range backlog:DependencyKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has dependency kind"@en ;
    skos:definition "Which of the three kinds this dependency is; required, because the kind determines what would release it."@en .

#################################################################
#  29. Impediments (v1.4.0)
#################################################################

backlog:Impediment a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Impediment"@en ;
    skos:definition "Anything slowing or stopping work that is not a dependency on other work: a missing environment, an unavailable decision-maker, a broken tool, an organisational constraint. Held separately because dependencies are cleared by finishing something and impediments are cleared by someone acting outside the register, and a plan that records only the first explains only half of why work is stuck."@en .

backlog:impedes a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Impediment ;
    rdfs:range [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:WorkItem backlog:WorkItemContainer ) ] ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "impedes"@en ;
    skos:definition "The work this impediment is blocking or slowing."@en .

backlog:raisedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Impediment ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "raised at"@en ;
    skos:definition "When the impediment was raised; its age is the measure that matters, since an impediment nobody has cleared for weeks is an organisational fact, not a task."@en .

backlog:resolvedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Impediment ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "resolved at"@en ;
    skos:definition "When it was cleared; absent while it still stands."@en .

backlog:hasImpedimentOwner a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Impediment ; rdfs:range backlog:Role ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has impediment owner"@en ;
    skos:definition "The role accountable for clearing it. Required while unresolved: an impediment with no owner is a complaint."@en .

backlog:hasImpedimentStatement a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Impediment ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has impediment statement"@en ;
    skos:definition "What is blocked, by what, and what would clear it."@en .

#################################################################
#  30. Flow: started and finished points, WIP policy (v1.4.0)
#################################################################

backlog:startedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "started at"@en ;
    skos:definition "The moment work on this item began. Every flow measure is defined against a well-defined start and finish point, so recording them is what makes work-in-progress, item age, cycle time and throughput computable at all rather than estimated from status changes."@en ;
    dcterms:source "The Kanban Pocket Guide, ch. 6 (basic metrics of flow); Kanban Guide for Scrum Teams."@en .

backlog:finishedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "finished at"@en ;
    skos:definition "The moment it finished. Cycle time is the elapsed time between the two points; item age is the elapsed time from the start point to now for anything unfinished."@en .

backlog:WipLimit a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "WIP Limit"@en ;
    skos:definition "A policy constraining how many items may be in progress at once. A policy, not a measurement: work-in-progress is what is happening, the limit is what the team decided should happen, and conflating them is how a limit becomes a description of the overrun instead of a constraint on it."@en .

backlog:wipLimitFor a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WipLimit ; rdfs:range backlog:WorkItemContainer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "WIP limit for"@en ;
    skos:definition "The register or project the limit applies to."@en .

backlog:hasWipLimitValue a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WipLimit ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has WIP limit value"@en ;
    skos:definition "The maximum number of items permitted in progress."@en .

#################################################################
#  31. Team, team roles, capacity (v1.4.0)
#################################################################

backlog:Team a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Team"@en ;
    skos:definition "The group accountable for delivering from a register. Modelled because capacity, work-in-progress limits and throughput are all properties of a team rather than of a backlog, and a register worked by two teams behaves nothing like one worked by a single team."@en .

backlog:TeamRole a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Team Role"@en ;
    skos:definition "A method-specific role within a team — product owner, facilitator, developer, tester. Deliberately an open class, unlike backlog:Role, which stays closed at Owner and Builder: Role answers who may DECIDE what, which the framework constrains, while TeamRole answers how a team is organised, which is the adopting method's business and varies between Scrum, XP and Kanban."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Role/artifact/event structure per the Scrum Guide 2020; role modelling per OntoAgile (Ortega-Ordoñez, Pardo-Calvache & Pino-Correa, DYNA 86(209), 2019)."@en .

backlog:teamFor a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Team ; rdfs:range backlog:WorkItemContainer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "team for"@en ;
    skos:definition "The register or project this team delivers from."@en .

backlog:hasTeamRole a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Team ; rdfs:range backlog:TeamRole ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has team role"@en ;
    skos:definition "A role present in this team."@en .

backlog:assignedToTeam a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ; rdfs:range backlog:Team ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "assigned to team"@en ;
    skos:definition "The team carrying this item."@en .

backlog:hasCapacity a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:Team backlog:Iteration ) ] ;
    rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has capacity"@en ;
    skos:definition "Available capacity in the same unit the cost estimates use, so committed effort and available effort can be compared without a conversion nobody documented."@en .

#################################################################
#  32. Canonical user-story form (v1.4.0)
#################################################################

backlog:asRole a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Story ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "as role"@en ;
    skos:definition "Who the story is for. Held as its own field rather than buried in the title so that stories can be grouped by audience and a story serving nobody nameable is visible as such."@en .

backlog:wantsCapability a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Story ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "wants capability"@en ;
    skos:definition "What they want to be able to do."@en .

backlog:soThat a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Story ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "so that"@en ;
    skos:definition "Why it matters to them. The clause most often dropped and the one that carries the value argument; without it a story states a feature request and nothing about the benefit it claims."@en .

[] a owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( backlog:Commitment backlog:DependencyKind backlog:Dependency backlog:Impediment
                 backlog:WipLimit backlog:Team backlog:TeamRole ) .

#################################################################
#  33. Falsifiability: observation, outcome, re-baselining (v1.5.0)
#     Everything below exists so that a register can be WRONG.
#     A plan that cannot be wrong cannot be right either, and a
#     framework that only checks internal form admits exactly that.
#################################################################

backlog:MetricDirection a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Dir_Increase backlog:Dir_Decrease backlog:Dir_Hold ) ;
    rdfs:label "Metric Direction"@en ;
    skos:definition "Which way a metric must move for an objective to be met. Closed set of two. Without it a baseline and a target are two numbers with no claim between them, and no observation can contradict them — the precise sense in which an unfalsifiable objective is not an objective."@en .

backlog:Dir_Increase a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:MetricDirection ;
    rdfs:label "Increase"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the observed value reaches or exceeds the target."@en .

backlog:Dir_Decrease a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:MetricDirection ;
    rdfs:label "Decrease"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the observed value reaches or falls below the target."@en .

[] a owl:AllDifferent ; owl:distinctMembers ( backlog:Dir_Increase backlog:Dir_Decrease ) .

backlog:AchievementStatus a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Ach_Met backlog:Ach_Missed backlog:Ach_Pending backlog:Ach_Withdrawn backlog:Ach_Retrospective ) ;
    rdfs:label "Achievement Status"@en ;
    skos:definition "Whether a stated target was met, missed, or is still open. Closed set of three, derived from observation rather than asserted, so that failure is a computed outcome nobody has to volunteer."@en .

backlog:Ach_Met a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:AchievementStatus ;
    rdfs:label "Met"@en ;
    skos:definition "An observation exists on or after the deadline showing the target reached in the declared direction."@en .

backlog:Ach_Missed a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:AchievementStatus ;
    rdfs:label "Missed"@en ;
    skos:definition "The deadline has passed and the latest observation does not reach the target. A first-class outcome, because a framework in which nothing can be recorded as missed measures nothing."@en .

backlog:Ach_Pending a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:AchievementStatus ;
    rdfs:label "Pending"@en ;
    skos:definition "The deadline has not passed yet."@en .

[] a owl:AllDifferent ; owl:distinctMembers ( backlog:Ach_Met backlog:Ach_Missed backlog:Ach_Pending ) .

backlog:MetricObservation a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Metric Observation"@en ;
    skos:definition "A recorded measurement of a metric at a point in time, with the method that produced it. The missing half of an objective: a baseline and a target state an intention, and only an observation can settle whether the intention was realised. Without observations a register can declare any objective and never be shown to have failed one."@en .

backlog:observesMetric a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:MetricObservation ; rdfs:range core:Metric ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "observes metric"@en ;
    skos:definition "The metric measured."@en .

backlog:hasObservedValue a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:MetricObservation ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has observed value"@en ;
    skos:definition "What the metric actually read."@en .

backlog:observedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:MetricObservation ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "observed at"@en ;
    skos:definition "When it was read."@en .

backlog:hasObservationMethod a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:MetricObservation ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has observation method"@en ;
    skos:definition "How the value was obtained — the query run, the report read, the instrument used. Required, so that an observation is reproducible rather than a number someone remembered."@en .

backlog:observationFor a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:MetricObservation ; rdfs:range backlog:Objective ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "observation for"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective this observation bears on."@en .

backlog:hasTargetDirection a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Objective ; rdfs:range backlog:MetricDirection ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has target direction"@en ;
    skos:definition "Which way the metric must move. Mandatory on every objective: it is what makes the target a claim rather than a decoration."@en .

backlog:objectiveOutcome a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Objective ; rdfs:range backlog:AchievementStatus ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "objective outcome"@en ;
    skos:definition "Met, missed or pending, derived by rule from the latest observation against the target and direction. Never asserted by hand — the whole point is that the register computes its own failures."@en .

backlog:achievedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Milestone ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "achieved at"@en ;
    skos:definition "When the milestone was actually reached. Its absence after the target date is what makes a slipped date visible instead of quietly forgotten."@en .

backlog:milestoneOutcome a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Milestone ; rdfs:range backlog:AchievementStatus ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "milestone outcome"@en ;
    skos:definition "Derived: met if achieved on or before the target date, missed once the target date has passed with no achievement, pending otherwise."@en .

backlog:Rebaseline a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Rebaseline"@en ;
    skos:definition "An explicit, owner-decided record that a target or a date was moved, keeping the old value beside the new one and the reason with both. The control against the failure mode that makes every plan succeed: quietly editing the target until the outcome matches it. A register may move its goalposts; it may not do so invisibly."@en .

backlog:rebaselines a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Rebaseline ;
    rdfs:range [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:Objective backlog:Milestone ) ] ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "rebaselines"@en ;
    skos:definition "What was moved."@en .

backlog:hasPreviousTarget a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Rebaseline ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has previous target"@en ;
    skos:definition "The value or date as it stood before the move, retained so the size of the move is visible."@en .

backlog:hasNewTarget a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Rebaseline ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has new target"@en ;
    skos:definition "The value or date after the move."@en .

backlog:hasRebaselineRationale a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Rebaseline ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has rebaseline rationale"@en ;
    skos:definition "Why the target moved. Required, and required to be an owner decision: a re-baseline without a reason is indistinguishable from erasing a failure."@en .

backlog:rebaselinedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Rebaseline ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "rebaselined at"@en ;
    skos:definition "When the move was made."@en .

backlog:hasActualEffort a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has actual effort"@en ;
    skos:definition "What the item actually cost, in the unit its estimate used. Required on completed work at the governed level, because an estimate never compared with an actual cannot be shown to be bad, and a team whose estimates cannot be shown to be bad never improves them."@en .

backlog:verifiedByTool a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Evidence ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "verified by tool"@en ;
    skos:definition "The tool that performed the verification, named and versioned. Required at the governed level to close the loophole that lets a human write evidenceVerified true with the method 'asserted': verification must name something that could have failed."@en .

#################################################################
#  34. Mission and the measurable intent chain (v1.6.0)
#     mission -> goal -> objective -> observation. Every link is
#     required, so no work can claim value without a chain that
#     terminates in something an observation can contradict.
#################################################################

backlog:Mission a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Mission"@en ;
    skos:definition "Why the development exists at all: the standing purpose that outlives any particular release and against which scope is decided. The root of the intent chain — goals advance the mission, objectives make goals measurable, observations settle objectives — so that 'is this in scope' has an answer above the level of opinion. A mission is an owner declaration and is never derived."@en .

backlog:hasMissionStatement a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Mission ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has mission statement"@en ;
    skos:definition "The purpose in prose: who is served, what changes for them, and what the development will not attempt. Prose here is deliberate — the mission is the one layer that is argued rather than measured, which is precisely why everything below it must be measurable."@en .

backlog:contributesToMission a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Goal ; rdfs:range backlog:Mission ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "contributes to mission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The mission this goal advances. A goal that advances no mission is a preference with a budget."@en .

backlog:missionFor a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Mission ; rdfs:range backlog:WorkItemContainer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "mission for"@en ;
    skos:definition "The register or project this mission governs."@en .

#################################################################
#  35. Scope as a measurable commitment (v1.6.0)
#################################################################

backlog:scopeRealizesObjective a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ScopeStatement ; rdfs:range backlog:Objective ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "scope realizes objective"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective this scope exists to achieve. The link that makes scope completion a testable event rather than a checklist ending: when the scoped work is finished, the objectives named here are what decide whether it worked."@en .

backlog:scopeCompletionState a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ScopeStatement ; rdfs:range backlog:LifecycleState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "scope completion state"@en ;
    skos:definition "Whether the work inside this scope is finished, derived from the scoped container's members. Completion of scope is deliberately separate from success: finishing everything you said you would do is the precondition for asking whether it worked, not the answer."@en .

backlog:scopeOutcome a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ScopeStatement ; rdfs:range backlog:AchievementStatus ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "scope outcome"@en ;
    skos:definition "Derived once the scope is complete: met when every objective it realizes was met, missed when any was missed, pending until then. This is where success and failure of a development become a computed fact — the register can report that it delivered everything it promised and still failed, which is the outcome most plans are structurally unable to express."@en .

backlog:ScopeChange a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Scope Change"@en ;
    skos:definition "An explicit, owner-decided admission of work into a declared scope after the scope was set, carrying what was admitted and why. The framework does not forbid scope from growing — a rule that forbids it is bypassed rather than obeyed — it forbids scope growing INVISIBLY. Anything entering scope without one of these is an unrecorded change, which the suite treats as a defect."@en .

backlog:admitsItem a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ScopeChange ; rdfs:range backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "admits item"@en ;
    skos:definition "The work admitted into scope by this change."@en .

backlog:changesScope a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ScopeChange ; rdfs:range backlog:ScopeStatement ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "changes scope"@en ;
    skos:definition "The scope statement being changed."@en .

backlog:hasScopeChangeRationale a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ScopeChange ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has scope change rationale"@en ;
    skos:definition "Why the boundary moved, and what was traded to pay for it. Required: scope that grows without a stated trade is how a plan silently becomes unachievable."@en .

#################################################################
#  36. External dependencies and the proposal rule (v1.6.0)
#################################################################

backlog:ExternalDependencyType a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Ext_Vendor backlog:Ext_UpstreamComponent backlog:Ext_PeerTeam
                backlog:Ext_Regulatory backlog:Ext_Infrastructure backlog:Ext_Customer ) ;
    rdfs:label "External Dependency Type"@en ;
    skos:definition "Who or what outside the development the dependency rests on, as a closed set of six. Orthogonal to DependencyKind, which says what would release a dependency (knowledge, task, resource): the type says who must act, and the two together determine whether waiting, asking, escalating or buying is the available move. Six types because each has a different escalation path and a different realistic response time, and treating them alike is how teams plan around a regulator as though it were a library upgrade."@en .

backlog:Ext_Vendor a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ExternalDependencyType ;
    rdfs:label "Vendor"@en ;
    skos:definition "A commercial supplier or hosted service under contract. Changes are negotiated, priced and scheduled by someone else; the realistic lever is the contract, not the backlog."@en .

backlog:Ext_UpstreamComponent a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ExternalDependencyType ;
    rdfs:label "Upstream component"@en ;
    skos:definition "A library, framework, dataset, standard or ontology the development consumes but does not own. Changes are proposed to the upstream maintainers; forking is the fallback and is a scope decision in its own right."@en .

backlog:Ext_PeerTeam a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ExternalDependencyType ;
    rdfs:label "Peer team"@en ;
    skos:definition "Another team or product inside the same organisation. The most commonly mis-modelled type: organisational proximity tempts teams to write the other team's work into their own backlog, which is exactly the failure the proposal rule prevents."@en .

backlog:Ext_Regulatory a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ExternalDependencyType ;
    rdfs:label "Regulatory or standards body"@en ;
    skos:definition "A regulator, certification authority or standards body. Its requirements are inputs, not negotiable scope, and its timelines are almost never responsive to a development's plan."@en .

backlog:Ext_Infrastructure a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ExternalDependencyType ;
    rdfs:label "Infrastructure or platform"@en ;
    skos:definition "Hosting, network, identity, build or environment services the development runs on but does not control."@en .

backlog:Ext_Customer a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ExternalDependencyType ;
    rdfs:label "Customer or client"@en ;
    skos:definition "Data, decisions, access or acceptance that only the client can supply. Frequently on the critical path and rarely on the plan."@en .

[] a owl:AllDifferent ; owl:distinctMembers ( backlog:Ext_Vendor backlog:Ext_UpstreamComponent backlog:Ext_PeerTeam
                                              backlog:Ext_Regulatory backlog:Ext_Infrastructure backlog:Ext_Customer ) .

backlog:ExternalDependency a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "External Dependency"@en ;
    skos:definition "Something the development depends on that it does not own and cannot change by deciding to. Distinct from an item-level dependency, which is work inside the register, and from an impediment, which is an obstacle someone inside the organisation can clear. Modelled as a first-class party with a type and a contact so that 'we are waiting on them' names a them."@en .

backlog:hasExternalDependencyType a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ExternalDependency ; rdfs:range backlog:ExternalDependencyType ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has external dependency type"@en ;
    skos:definition "Which of the six types this dependency is."@en .

backlog:hasExternalParty a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ExternalDependency ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has external party"@en ;
    skos:definition "Who owns the thing depended on, named specifically enough to be contacted. Required: an unnamed external dependency cannot be chased and becomes a permanent excuse."@en .

backlog:dependsOnExternal a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:WorkItem backlog:WorkItemContainer ) ] ;
    rdfs:range backlog:ExternalDependency ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "depends on external"@en ;
    skos:definition "This work consumes something external as it stands today. Consuming an external dependency is normal; needing it to CHANGE is not, and is expressed by requiresExternalEnhancement instead."@en .

backlog:requiresExternalEnhancement a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ; rdfs:range backlog:ExternalDependency ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "requires external enhancement"@en ;
    skos:definition "This item cannot be delivered unless something outside the development changes. Declaring it is what triggers the framework's central scope rule: the change itself is never work in this register — a proposal is raised to the owning party, and the dependent item cannot be started until that proposal is accepted. This generalises the discipline the OE governance store applies to its own sessions, where a finding about another session's artifact is packaged as a proposal and never executed as an edit."@en .

backlog:ProposalStatus a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Prop_Submitted backlog:Prop_Accepted backlog:Prop_Rejected backlog:Prop_Withdrawn ) ;
    rdfs:label "Proposal Status"@en ;
    skos:definition "Where an enhancement request stands with the party that owns the thing. Closed set of four. Rejected is a first-class outcome and not a failure of the register: it is the signal to re-plan, and a framework with no way to record it quietly converts refusal into indefinite waiting."@en .

backlog:Prop_Submitted a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ProposalStatus ;
    rdfs:label "Submitted"@en ;
    skos:definition "Raised with the owning party, no decision yet. Dependent work stays unstartable."@en .

backlog:Prop_Accepted a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ProposalStatus ;
    rdfs:label "Accepted"@en ;
    skos:definition "The owning party has committed to the change. Dependent work becomes startable, and the external delivery becomes a tracked dependency rather than a hope."@en .

backlog:Prop_Rejected a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ProposalStatus ;
    rdfs:label "Rejected"@en ;
    skos:definition "The owning party declined. The dependent item must be re-planned, cancelled, or brought into scope through an explicit owner-decided scope change — never left quietly waiting."@en .

backlog:Prop_Withdrawn a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ProposalStatus ;
    rdfs:label "Withdrawn"@en ;
    skos:definition "The development withdrew the request, usually because the need disappeared or a workaround was chosen."@en .

[] a owl:AllDifferent ; owl:distinctMembers ( backlog:Prop_Submitted backlog:Prop_Accepted backlog:Prop_Rejected backlog:Prop_Withdrawn ) .

backlog:EnhancementProposal a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Enhancement Proposal"@en ;
    skos:definition "A request to a party outside the development to change something they own. The artifact that keeps external change out of the register: the proposal is tracked, the change is not scheduled, and the item that needs it waits until the proposal is accepted. Never a work item — a work item is something this development can decide to do."@en .

backlog:proposalFor a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:EnhancementProposal ; rdfs:range backlog:ExternalDependency ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "proposal for"@en ;
    skos:definition "The external dependency this proposal asks to change."@en .

backlog:unblocksItem a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:EnhancementProposal ; rdfs:range backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "unblocks item"@en ;
    skos:definition "The item waiting on this proposal, so the cost of a rejection is visible at the moment of rejection."@en .

backlog:hasRequestedChange a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:EnhancementProposal ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has requested change"@en ;
    skos:definition "What is being asked for, specifically enough that the other party can accept or refuse it."@en .

backlog:submittedTo a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:EnhancementProposal ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "submitted to"@en ;
    skos:definition "Where the request went — the tracker, the contact, the forum — so that 'we asked' is checkable."@en .

backlog:proposalRaisedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:EnhancementProposal ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "proposal raised at"@en ;
    skos:definition "When it was raised; its age is the honest measure of how long the development has been blocked by someone else."@en .

backlog:hasProposalStatus a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:EnhancementProposal ; rdfs:range backlog:ProposalStatus ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has proposal status"@en ;
    skos:definition "Where the request stands."@en .

#################################################################
#  37. Register session hygiene (v1.6.0)
#     Provenance of changes to the register — deliberately NOT
#     ceremony or meeting modelling, which stays a non-goal.
#################################################################

backlog:RegisterSession a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Register Session"@en ;
    skos:definition "One working session in which the register was read and possibly changed, recording whether its state was verified before the changes were made and what was touched. Scoped narrowly to provenance of register edits: it does not model planning meetings, reviews or retrospectives, which remain outside this framework. It exists because a register nobody verified before editing is a register whose history cannot be trusted, and because 'someone changed the backlog' is otherwise unattributable."@en .

backlog:sessionFor a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:RegisterSession ; rdfs:range backlog:WorkItemContainer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "session for"@en ;
    skos:definition "The register this session worked on."@en .

backlog:sessionStartedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:RegisterSession ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "session started at"@en ;
    skos:definition "When the session began."@en .

backlog:sessionEndedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:RegisterSession ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "session ended at"@en ;
    skos:definition "When it ended."@en .

backlog:stateVerifiedAtStart a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:RegisterSession ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "state verified at start"@en ;
    skos:definition "True only when the register was validated before this session changed anything. A session that edits without verifying first cannot tell which defects it inherited from which it introduced, so the suite requires this of any session that changed an item."@en .

backlog:changedItem a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:RegisterSession ; rdfs:range backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "changed item"@en ;
    skos:definition "An item this session altered."@en .

backlog:hasSessionScopeNote a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:RegisterSession ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has session scope note"@en ;
    skos:definition "What this session set out to do and what it deliberately left alone. The out-of-scope half is the load-bearing one: an unstated boundary is the mechanism by which a session that meant to fix one item quietly reorganises a register."@en .

backlog:sessionConductedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:RegisterSession ; rdfs:range backlog:Role ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "session conducted by"@en ;
    skos:definition "The role that worked the session."@en .

[] a owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( backlog:Mission backlog:ScopeChange backlog:ExternalDependency backlog:ExternalDependencyType
                 backlog:EnhancementProposal backlog:ProposalStatus backlog:RegisterSession ) .

#################################################################
#  38. Register packaging (v1.7.0)
#     The framework governed what a register must SAY and never
#     what an adopting development must SHIP. This layer closes
#     that asymmetry by binding to the OE configuration subject's
#     existing conventions rather than inventing a parallel set.
#################################################################

backlog:RegisterPackage a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Register Package"@en ;
    skos:definition "The shippable unit of an adopting development's governance data: the register itself, the retained roadmap report runs that evidence progress, and the manifest that makes the set verifiable. Introduced because a framework that constrains what a register must say, while saying nothing about what a development must ship, produces registers that are correct in one working copy and unreproducible everywhere else. Deliberately thin: it names roles and requires a manifest, and delegates every filename and version rule to the configuration subject that already owns them."@en .

backlog:RegisterArtifact a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Register Artifact"@en ;
    skos:definition "One file inside a register package, carrying the role it plays and the naming convention it claims to follow. Roles are enumerated rather than inferred from filenames, so a package missing its progress evidence is a detectable absence instead of a directory someone has to read."@en .

backlog:ArtifactRole a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Role_RegisterData backlog:Role_ProgressReport backlog:Role_EvidenceIndex
                backlog:Role_Manifest backlog:Role_ProfileDeclaration ) ;
    rdfs:label "Artifact Role"@en ;
    skos:definition "What a file in a register package is for. Closed set of five: the register data itself, a retained progress report run, an evidence index, the manifest, and the adoption profile declaration."@en .

backlog:Role_RegisterData a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ArtifactRole ;
    rdfs:label "Register data"@en ;
    skos:definition "The backlog, roadmap and blueprint instances — the graph everything else is computed from."@en .

backlog:Role_ProgressReport a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ArtifactRole ;
    rdfs:label "Progress report run"@en ;
    skos:definition "A retained roadmap report run, kept as a file rather than a screenful. Progress is a sequence of dated computations, and a package that retains none can show a state but never a trajectory."@en .

backlog:Role_EvidenceIndex a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ArtifactRole ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence index"@en ;
    skos:definition "The record of what the execution bridge verified, when, and with which tool."@en .

backlog:Role_Manifest a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ArtifactRole ;
    rdfs:label "Manifest"@en ;
    skos:definition "The SHA-256 listing that makes the package verifiable after transport. Exactly one per package."@en .

backlog:Role_ProfileDeclaration a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ArtifactRole ;
    rdfs:label "Adoption profile declaration"@en ;
    skos:definition "The file carrying the AdoptionProfile, so a consumer can see which conformance level the package claims before validating it."@en .

[] a owl:AllDifferent ; owl:distinctMembers ( backlog:Role_RegisterData backlog:Role_ProgressReport
                                              backlog:Role_EvidenceIndex backlog:Role_Manifest
                                              backlog:Role_ProfileDeclaration ) .

backlog:packageFor a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:RegisterPackage ; rdfs:range backlog:WorkItemContainer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "package for"@en ;
    skos:definition "The register or project this package ships."@en .

backlog:hasPackageArtifact a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:RegisterPackage ; rdfs:range backlog:RegisterArtifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has package artifact"@en ;
    skos:definition "A file this package ships."@en .

backlog:hasArtifactRole a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:RegisterArtifact ; rdfs:range backlog:ArtifactRole ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has artifact role"@en ;
    skos:definition "The single role this file plays."@en .

backlog:hasArtifactFileName a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:RegisterArtifact ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has artifact file name"@en ;
    skos:definition "The file's name as shipped, checkable against the naming convention it claims."@en .

backlog:conformsToNamingConvention a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:RegisterArtifact ; rdfs:range configuration:NamingConvention ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "conforms to naming convention"@en ;
    skos:definition "The configuration-subject naming convention this file follows, referenced by IRI rather than restated. The governing filename patterns already exist and are versioned in that subject; duplicating them here would create a second source of truth that drifts, so the framework points at theirs and the package checker reads the pattern from the pack at check time."@en .

backlog:hasPackageVersion a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:RegisterPackage ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has package version"@en ;
    skos:definition "The package's own three-part version. A register is a released artifact like any other: a changed register that reuses its version is the same defect as a changed ontology that reuses its version."@en .

backlog:hasManifestSHA256 a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:RegisterArtifact ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has manifest SHA-256"@en ;
    skos:definition "The digest of the manifest file itself, so the thing that verifies everything else is verifiable in turn."@en .

backlog:reportRunRetainedAs a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:RoadmapReport ; rdfs:range backlog:RegisterArtifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "report run retained as"@en ;
    skos:definition "The file a report run was written to. Without it a run is a screenful that disappears, and the framework's own rule — that a recommendation points at the run which produced it — becomes unauditable the moment the terminal closes."@en .

[] a owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
   owl:members ( backlog:RegisterPackage backlog:RegisterArtifact backlog:ArtifactRole ) .

#################################################################
#  39. Resolving the ranking fork, once and on the record (v1.8.0)
#################################################################

backlog:hasRankingForkResolution a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Backlog ; rdfs:range backlog:RankingModel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has ranking fork resolution"@en ;
    skos:definition "The model an owner has decided governs pickup when throughput ranking and launch-scoped ranking disagree, recorded once on the register rather than re-argued each run. Functional: a register resolves the fork one way or not at all. Setting it does NOT resolve the disagreement silently — both models are still computed and the disagreement is still reported; what changes is that a reader also sees what the owner decided about it, with the reasoning attached through decidedBy and hasDecisionRationale, which the suite requires whenever this property is set. Absent means no standing decision has been taken, which is a legitimate state and the default: a fork nobody has ruled on should keep asking."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Raised 2026-07-29 by an adopting project session whose register carried a genuine, repeatedly re-presented ranking disagreement with nowhere to record the owner's standing answer."@en .

#################################################################
#  40. The conformance level as a ramp, not a shelter (v1.10.0)
#################################################################

backlog:hasTargetConformanceLevel a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:AdoptionProfile ; rdfs:range backlog:ConformanceLevel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has target conformance level"@en ;
    skos:definition "The level this adoption is working toward. The tier mechanism is an adoption ramp — it exists so a team can start without meeting every constraint on day one — and a ramp with no destination is a parking space. Declaring a target does not commit anyone to a date they cannot meet; it commits them to saying which direction they are facing, which is the difference between adopting progressively and adopting selectively."@en .

backlog:hasLevelReviewDate a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:AdoptionProfile ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has level review date"@en ;
    skos:definition "When the adopted level is next revisited. Not a deadline to reach the target: a date on which someone must look again and either advance, or restate the reason for staying. A level with no review date is one nobody will ever reconsider, because nothing will ever ask them to."@en .

backlog:hasPriorConformanceLevel a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:AdoptionProfile ; rdfs:range backlog:ConformanceLevel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has prior conformance level"@en ;
    skos:definition "The level this adoption previously declared, retained so that a DOWNGRADE is visible as a downgrade. Without it, lowering the level is indistinguishable from never having claimed the higher one — the register simply reports fewer violations and nothing says why. This is the same control Rebaseline applies to a moved target: the framework does not forbid the move, it forbids the move being invisible."@en .

backlog:hasDowngradeRationale a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:AdoptionProfile ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has downgrade rationale"@en ;
    skos:definition "Why a previously-claimed higher conformance level was given up. Kept separate from the ordinary level rationale so that a downgrade cannot be satisfied by the sentence that justified the original choice: giving up a claim is a different decision from making one, and reusing the earlier reason would let the harder question be answered by the easier one."@en .

#################################################################
#  41. Measured versus judged: test-driving an estimate (v1.12.0)
#################################################################

backlog:EstimationBasisKind a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Basis_Measured backlog:Basis_Analogous backlog:Basis_Judged ) ;
    rdfs:label "Estimation Basis Kind"@en ;
    skos:definition "How an estimate or a score input was arrived at, as a closed set of three. The framework already recorded WHAT an estimate was based on through hasCostBasis, in prose; it could not tell whether that basis was EXECUTED or merely reasoned. The distinction is the same one the framework draws everywhere else between a claim and a check: a number produced by running something can be re-run and can be wrong in a detectable way, while a number produced by reasoning can only be disagreed with. Neither is forbidden — most estimates are judgements and saying so is honest — but a judgement that is indistinguishable from a measurement lets the weaker evidence inherit the stronger one's authority."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Practice observed in an adopting project, which set an objective baseline by test-driving a trial conformance declaration against a scratch copy of its live register and recorded the figure as measured rather than estimated, before any planning discussion."@en .

backlog:Basis_Measured a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:EstimationBasisKind ;
    rdfs:label "Measured"@en ;
    skos:definition "Produced by executing something against real artifacts — a trial run, a test drive, a spike, a query over the register itself. Must name the MetricObservation that produced it, so the figure is re-derivable rather than merely asserted to have been measured."@en .

backlog:Basis_Analogous a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:EstimationBasisKind ;
    rdfs:label "Analogous"@en ;
    skos:definition "Derived from the recorded actuals of comparable completed work. Weaker than measurement because the comparison is a judgement, stronger than pure estimation because the reference figures are real. Should name the items it was drawn from."@en .

backlog:Basis_Judged a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:EstimationBasisKind ;
    rdfs:label "Judged"@en ;
    skos:definition "Arrived at by expertise and reasoning, with nothing executed. The honest default and the majority case. Recording it as such costs nothing and prevents a judgement being read as a measurement later, when whoever made it is no longer in the room."@en .

[] a owl:AllDifferent ; owl:distinctMembers ( backlog:Basis_Measured backlog:Basis_Analogous backlog:Basis_Judged ) .

backlog:hasBasisKind a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:CostEstimate backlog:PriorityScore ) ] ;
    rdfs:range backlog:EstimationBasisKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has basis kind"@en ;
    skos:definition "Whether this estimate or score was measured, drawn from analogy, or judged. Applies to prioritisation as well as estimation, because a score whose inputs were test-driven is different evidence from one whose inputs were argued, and the ordering it produces inherits whichever it was."@en .

backlog:basisObservation a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:CostEstimate backlog:PriorityScore ) ] ;
    rdfs:range backlog:MetricObservation ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "basis observation"@en ;
    skos:definition "The recorded observation a measured estimate or score rests on, carrying its own method and timestamp. Required whenever the basis kind is Measured: claiming a figure was measured without naming what produced it is the same defect as evidence marked verified with the method 'asserted'."@en .

backlog:analogousTo a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:CostEstimate backlog:PriorityScore ) ] ;
    rdfs:range backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "analogous to"@en ;
    skos:definition "A completed work item whose recorded actual effort this estimate was drawn from. Names the comparison so a reader can check whether it was a fair one, and so a systematically bad analogy becomes visible once the new item's own actual is recorded."@en .

#################################################################
#  42. Product backlog items versus execution tasks (v1.13.0)
#
#  BRF-EP1. Acceptance criterion, from the framework's own register:
#  "Given a register holding both stories and their execution tasks,
#   When the report is run, Then task-level work is identifiable as
#   subordinate and does not carry independent value, and a planning
#   event records the conversion."
#################################################################

backlog:ProductBacklogItem a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Product Backlog Item"@en ;
    skos:definition "Work that carries value in its own right and is therefore ordered against other work on a product backlog. Every kind this framework already shipped is one of these, including Task — whose definition has always said it must be tracked, prioritised and evidenced like any other item, making it non-user-facing product work rather than subordinate execution work. The grouping exists so its complement can be named: the thing a backlog item is broken into during planning, which is NOT ordered against the backlog because its value is the parent's."@en .

backlog:ExecutionTask a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Execution Task"@en ;
    skos:definition "A step produced by planning a product backlog item into an iteration: subordinate by construction, carrying no independent value, and existing only as part of the item it came from. It is not ranked, not scored, and not compared against backlog items — scoring it would double-count the parent's value, which is the same error the framework already refuses when a decomposed parent and its children both carry scores. Its purpose is execution tracking: who is doing what, and how far through. When the parent is Done the task has no separate life."@en .

[] a owl:AllDisjointClasses ; owl:members ( backlog:ProductBacklogItem backlog:ExecutionTask ) .

backlog:Initiative rdfs:subClassOf backlog:ProductBacklogItem .
backlog:Epic       rdfs:subClassOf backlog:ProductBacklogItem .
backlog:Feature    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:ProductBacklogItem .
backlog:Story      rdfs:subClassOf backlog:ProductBacklogItem .
backlog:Task       rdfs:subClassOf backlog:ProductBacklogItem .
backlog:Defect     rdfs:subClassOf backlog:ProductBacklogItem .
backlog:Enabler    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:ProductBacklogItem .
backlog:Spike      rdfs:subClassOf backlog:ProductBacklogItem .

backlog:PlanningEvent a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Planning Event"@en ;
    skos:definition "The dated act of taking a product backlog item into an iteration and breaking it into execution tasks. Recorded for the same reason RefinementEvent is: the conversion is something someone DID, at a time, with a result — and a register that shows tasks appearing without one cannot say who committed to them or when. It is the boundary between ordering work and doing it."@en .

backlog:plansItem a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:PlanningEvent ; rdfs:range backlog:ProductBacklogItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "plans item"@en ;
    skos:definition "The backlog item being taken into an iteration. Functional: one planning event plans one item, so that the tasks it produced are unambiguously the tasks of that item."@en .

backlog:plannedInto a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:PlanningEvent ; rdfs:range backlog:Iteration ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "planned into"@en ;
    skos:definition "The iteration the item was committed to."@en .

backlog:producesTask a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:PlanningEvent ; rdfs:range backlog:ExecutionTask ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "produces task"@en ;
    skos:definition "An execution task this planning event created. A planning event that produces none is a commitment with no plan behind it."@en .

backlog:plannedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:PlanningEvent ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "planned at"@en ;
    skos:definition "When the planning happened, so that tasks appearing later than their own planning event are detectable."@en .

backlog:hasSprintGoal a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Iteration ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has sprint goal"@en ;
    skos:definition "A whole-iteration statement of what a person could do differently once this time box ships — not a restatement of the items in it, and not required to be structured, since forcing a template here risks theater rather than a real answer, the same lesson hasScoreRationale already encodes for WSJF. Optional: an Iteration without one is not itself a defect, but a register with no sprint ever stating why its items were grouped together cannot distinguish a coherent increment from a bin of whatever fit."@en .

backlog:plannedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:PlanningEvent ; rdfs:range backlog:Role ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "planned by"@en ;
    skos:definition "The role that did the planning."@en .

#################################################################
#  43. Iteration period and forecasting (v1.14.0)
#################################################################

backlog:iterationStart a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:Iteration backlog:Increment ) ] ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "iteration start"@en ;
    skos:definition "When the time box opened. Velocity is work completed per iteration, so without a period there is no denominator and the measure cannot exist — the same reason startedAt and finishedAt were added for cycle time."@en .

backlog:iterationEnd a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:Iteration backlog:Increment ) ] ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "iteration end"@en ;
    skos:definition "When the time box closed. An iteration whose end has passed is complete for velocity purposes whether or not its work was; that is precisely what makes velocity an honest measure rather than a summary of intentions."@en .

backlog:Forecast a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Forecast"@en ;
    skos:definition "A dated statement of when remaining work is expected to complete, carrying the assumptions it rests on and the observed velocity it was computed from. Recorded as an artifact rather than printed and forgotten because a forecast is a CLAIM about the future: it can be wrong, and it can only be shown to have been wrong if what it assumed was written down beside it. A forecast without assumptions cannot be learned from — when it misses, nothing says which assumption failed."@en .

backlog:forecastFor a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Forecast ; rdfs:range backlog:WorkItemContainer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "forecast for"@en ;
    skos:definition "The register or project whose remaining work this forecast covers."@en .

backlog:forecastMadeAt a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Forecast ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "forecast made at"@en ;
    skos:definition "When the forecast was made. Required, because a forecast is only assessable against what was known at the time it was made, and one re-dated after the fact is unfalsifiable."@en .

backlog:forecastCompletion a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Forecast ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "forecast completion"@en ;
    skos:definition "The date the remaining work is expected to be finished."@en .

backlog:forecastAssumption a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Forecast ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "forecast assumption"@en ;
    skos:definition "One stated condition the forecast depends on — that velocity holds, that scope does not grow, that a named dependency clears. At least one is required. A forecast presented without assumptions asks to be believed rather than checked, and when it misses there is nothing to point at."@en .

backlog:forecastObservedVelocity a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Forecast ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "forecast observed velocity"@en ;
    skos:definition "The velocity figure the forecast was computed from, in items per iteration. Recorded so the arithmetic can be checked and so a forecast built on one good iteration is distinguishable from one built on a settled average."@en .

backlog:forecastIterationsObserved a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Forecast ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "forecast iterations observed"@en ;
    skos:definition "How many completed iterations the velocity was averaged over. One is a data point, not a rate; the framework does not forbid forecasting from one, it requires the number to be visible so the reader can discount accordingly."@en .

backlog:forecastOutcome a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Forecast ; rdfs:range backlog:AchievementStatus ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "forecast outcome"@en ;
    skos:definition "Met, missed or pending, derived by rule from whether the forecast completion date passed with work still open. Reuses the same closed status the framework already applies to objectives and milestones, so a missed forecast is a first-class computed outcome rather than a quietly superseded one."@en .

#################################################################
#  44. Kick-off, the time-phased baseline, and SPI (v1.15.0)
#################################################################

backlog:KickOff a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Kick-Off"@en ;
    skos:definition "The recorded act of activating a plan: the moment from which planned dates are measured and against which every subsequent slip is computed. Needed because a baseline of relative offsets anchors to nothing — 'day 4' is not a date until something says when day zero was. It may be entered by a person or emitted by an automated trigger; hasKickOffMode records which, so a plan whose start was assumed is distinguishable from one whose start was declared. Until a KickOff exists, a plan is a proposal: its dates can be compared with each other but not with reality, and no schedule variance is computable."@en .

backlog:kickOffFor a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:KickOff ; rdfs:range backlog:WorkItemContainer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "kick-off for"@en ;
    skos:definition "The register or project this kick-off activates."@en .

backlog:kickedOffAt a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:KickOff ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "kicked off at"@en ;
    skos:definition "The actual moment work was activated — day zero of the baseline."@en .

backlog:KickOffMode a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:KickOff_Declared backlog:KickOff_Triggered ) ;
    rdfs:label "Kick-Off Mode"@en ;
    skos:definition "Whether the start was declared by a person or emitted by an automated trigger. Recorded because the two carry different evidential weight: a triggered kick-off is a fact about a system, a declared one is a claim by someone, and a slip measured from a claim inherits that claim's reliability."@en .

backlog:KickOff_Declared a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:KickOffMode ;
    rdfs:label "Declared"@en ;
    skos:definition "A person recorded the start. Must name who, through decidedBy."@en .

backlog:KickOff_Triggered a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:KickOffMode ;
    rdfs:label "Triggered"@en ;
    skos:definition "An automated event recorded the start — a pipeline run, a branch creation, a first commit. Must name the trigger through hasKickOffTrigger so the moment is re-derivable."@en .

backlog:hasKickOffMode a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:KickOff ; rdfs:range backlog:KickOffMode ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has kick-off mode"@en .

backlog:hasKickOffTrigger a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:KickOff ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has kick-off trigger"@en ;
    skos:definition "The automated event that recorded the start, named precisely enough to be found again."@en .

backlog:plannedStart a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:WorkItem backlog:WorkItemContainer ) ] ;
    rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "planned start"@en ;
    skos:definition "When this work was baselined to begin. Distinct from startedAt, which records when it actually began: the gap between them is the whole of schedule variance, and collapsing the two would make every plan appear to have been met."@en .

backlog:plannedFinish a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:WorkItem backlog:WorkItemContainer ) ] ;
    rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "planned finish"@en ;
    skos:definition "When this work was baselined to complete. With plannedStart it gives a Gantt bar; with hasEffortEstimate it gives planned value at any date, which is what SPI requires and what the subject previously had no way to express."@en .

backlog:hasDuration a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has duration"@en ;
    skos:definition "Elapsed working days this item is expected to occupy, which is NOT its effort: two people for one day and one person for two days share an effort and differ in duration. Required for network traversal, since a critical path is the longest chain of durations and cannot be computed from effort alone."@en .

backlog:PlanBaseline a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Plan Baseline"@en ;
    skos:definition "A named, dated set of planned dates against which performance is measured. Held as an artifact so that moving the plan is a recorded Rebaseline rather than an edit: without it, schedule variance can be driven to zero by changing the dates, which is the failure earned-value practice is most often criticised for. Retaining the prior baseline is what lets performance be reported against BOTH the current plan and the original."@en .

backlog:baselineFor a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:PlanBaseline ; rdfs:range backlog:WorkItemContainer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "baseline for"@en .

backlog:baselinedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:PlanBaseline ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "baselined at"@en .

backlog:isCurrentBaseline a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:PlanBaseline ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "is current baseline"@en ;
    skos:definition "True for exactly one baseline per container. Superseded baselines are retained, not deleted, so schedule performance can be reported against the original as well as the current — the number a rebaselined project would rather not show."@en .

#################################################################
#  45. Multi-dimensional cost (v1.17.0) — BRF-EP3
#################################################################

backlog:CostDimension a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Cost Dimension"@en ;
    skos:definition "A named axis along which work consumes something measurable, carrying its own unit and optionally a rate that converts it to money. Deliberately open: token spend, compute hours, environment cost, human review time and assessed complexity are all instances rather than predicates, so the framework can record what a modality actually costs without naming any modality in its vocabulary. A predicate called tokenCost would privilege LLM-driven development the way a story-point property would privilege one estimation practice."@en .

backlog:hasDimensionUnit a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:CostDimension ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has dimension unit"@en ;
    skos:definition "What one unit of this dimension is — a token, a GPU-hour, a person-hour. Required: a quantity without a unit cannot be summed, compared or budgeted against, and two dimensions with different units must never be added."@en .

backlog:hasDimensionRate a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:CostDimension ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has dimension rate"@en ;
    skos:definition "Monetary cost per unit, where one is known. Optional, because a dimension worth tracking is not always worth pricing: review time may matter as a constraint rather than as a bill. Where absent, that dimension contributes to no monetary total and is reported separately rather than silently omitted."@en .

backlog:hasRateCurrency a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:CostDimension ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has rate currency"@en ;
    skos:definition "The currency a rate is denominated in. Required wherever a rate exists, because summing amounts in unstated currencies produces a number with no meaning."@en .

backlog:DimensionalCost a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Dimensional Cost"@en ;
    skos:definition "One recorded quantity of one dimension for one work item — the reified triple of item, dimension and amount. Reified rather than expressed as a direct property so that an item can carry several costs at once, which is the whole point: an increment worked by an agent under human supervision has a token cost AND an environment cost AND a human cost, and collapsing them to a single effort figure loses the fact that they trade off against each other."@en .

backlog:costOfItem a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:DimensionalCost ;
    rdfs:range [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:WorkItem backlog:WorkItemContainer ) ] ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "cost of item"@en .

backlog:alongDimension a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:DimensionalCost ; rdfs:range backlog:CostDimension ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "along dimension"@en .

backlog:hasQuantity a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:DimensionalCost ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has quantity"@en ;
    skos:definition "How much of the dimension was consumed, in that dimension's own unit."@en .

backlog:isEstimatedCost a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:DimensionalCost ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "is estimated cost"@en ;
    skos:definition "True where the quantity is forecast, false where it was observed. Kept explicit for the same reason hasBasisKind exists: a projected token spend and a metered one are different evidence, and a total that mixes them without saying so reads as measurement."@en .

backlog:Budget a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Budget"@en ;
    skos:definition "A declared ceiling on one dimension for one container, against which recorded costs are compared. Per-dimension rather than per-project-total, because a budget that only exists in aggregate cannot say WHICH thing overran, and because dimensions with no rate have no aggregate to belong to."@en .

backlog:budgetFor a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Budget ; rdfs:range backlog:WorkItemContainer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "budget for"@en .

backlog:budgetDimension a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Budget ; rdfs:range backlog:CostDimension ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "budget dimension"@en .

backlog:hasBudgetCeiling a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Budget ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has budget ceiling"@en ;
    skos:definition "The maximum quantity of the dimension this container is permitted, in the dimension's own unit."@en .

#################################################################
#  46. Human in the loop and on the loop (v1.18.0) — BRF-EP4
#################################################################

backlog:ExecutionModality a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Mode_Human backlog:Mode_Automated backlog:Mode_Hybrid ) ;
    rdfs:label "Execution Modality"@en ;
    skos:definition "Who or what carries out a work item. Recorded because a register of automated development that does not say which work a person did cannot be audited afterwards: every item looks the same, and the question 'where did a human decide' has no answer in the data. Closed at three, because the interesting distinction is not the tool but whether a person was answerable for the output."@en .

backlog:Mode_Human a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ExecutionModality ;
    rdfs:label "Human"@en ;
    skos:definition "Carried out by a person. Competes for capacity like any other work, which is the point of recording it: human time is finite and an automated backlog that hides it will over-commit the one resource it cannot scale."@en .

backlog:Mode_Automated a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ExecutionModality ;
    rdfs:label "Automated"@en ;
    skos:definition "Carried out by an agent or a pipeline with no person in the execution path. Not a claim that nobody supervised it — supervision is recorded separately as an interaction, precisely so that unsupervised automation is distinguishable from supervised automation."@en .

backlog:Mode_Hybrid a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ExecutionModality ;
    rdfs:label "Hybrid"@en ;
    skos:definition "An agent produced the output and a person materially changed it. Distinct from automated-with-review, where the person accepted or rejected but did not author: the two carry different answers to who is responsible for what shipped."@en .

backlog:hasExecutionModality a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ; rdfs:range backlog:ExecutionModality ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has execution modality"@en .

backlog:SupervisionMode a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Sup_InTheLoop backlog:Sup_OnTheLoop backlog:Sup_None ) ;
    rdfs:label "Supervision Mode"@en ;
    skos:definition "How a person stands in relation to automated work. The distinction is not attitude but MECHANISM: in-the-loop means a transition cannot occur until a person acts, on-the-loop means it occurs and a person may intervene afterwards. Stated as a fact about gating rather than intent, because 'we review everything' and 'nothing proceeds without review' are different systems that sound identical in prose."@en .

backlog:Sup_InTheLoop a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:SupervisionMode ;
    rdfs:label "In the loop"@en ;
    skos:definition "The work cannot advance until a person acts. Must be evidenced by a gating interaction recorded before the transition it gates; a claim of in-the-loop supervision with no such interaction is a claim about intentions."@en .

backlog:Sup_OnTheLoop a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:SupervisionMode ;
    rdfs:label "On the loop"@en ;
    skos:definition "The work advances and a person may intervene afterwards. Weaker than in-the-loop and legitimately so — most automation should be on the loop — but recorded distinctly, because the two are indistinguishable in a register that only says a review happened."@en .

backlog:Sup_None a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:SupervisionMode ;
    rdfs:label "None"@en ;
    skos:definition "No person supervises this work. The honest option, and the one whose absence from a vocabulary would force unsupervised work to be recorded as though supervised."@en .

backlog:hasSupervisionMode a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ; rdfs:range backlog:SupervisionMode ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has supervision mode"@en .

backlog:InteractionKind a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Int_Confirm backlog:Int_Reject backlog:Int_Correct
                backlog:Int_Propose backlog:Int_Review backlog:Int_Respond ) ;
    rdfs:label "Interaction Kind"@en ;
    skos:definition "What a person did to a work item. Six, closed, and the split that matters is between the ones that GATE — confirm and reject, which decide whether work proceeds — and the ones that inform. A vocabulary with only 'review' cannot distinguish a person who approved from one who merely looked."@en .

backlog:Int_Confirm a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:InteractionKind ;
    rdfs:label "Confirm"@en ;
    skos:definition "A person permitted the work to proceed. Gating."@en .
backlog:Int_Reject a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:InteractionKind ;
    rdfs:label "Reject"@en ;
    skos:definition "A person refused the work. Gating, and the one whose complete absence over a long register suggests confirmation is a formality."@en .
backlog:Int_Correct a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:InteractionKind ;
    rdfs:label "Correct"@en ;
    skos:definition "A person materially changed the output. Implies the item's modality is Hybrid rather than Automated."@en .
backlog:Int_Propose a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:InteractionKind ;
    rdfs:label "Propose"@en ;
    skos:definition "A person supplied direction the agent did not have."@en .
backlog:Int_Review a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:InteractionKind ;
    rdfs:label "Review"@en ;
    skos:definition "A person examined the output without deciding or changing it. Not gating; recording it as such is what keeps 'reviewed' from being read as 'approved'."@en .
backlog:Int_Respond a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:InteractionKind ;
    rdfs:label "Respond"@en ;
    skos:definition "A person answered a question the agent raised."@en .

backlog:HumanInteraction a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Human Interaction"@en ;
    skos:definition "One recorded act by a person upon a work item: what they did, to what, when, and at what cost. An EVENT and deliberately not a work item — per the governing scope exclusion, human work assigned to a person is a work item and competes for capacity, while human interaction with work is an event and does not, because otherwise every review needs a priority score and the backlog fills with process. Its cost is carried on the same dimensional machinery as any other cost, so review time is budgetable without being schedulable."@en .

backlog:interactsWith a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:HumanInteraction ; rdfs:range backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "interacts with"@en .

backlog:hasInteractionKind a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:HumanInteraction ; rdfs:range backlog:InteractionKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has interaction kind"@en .

backlog:interactedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:HumanInteraction ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "interacted at"@en ;
    skos:definition "When it happened. Required, because in-the-loop supervision is the claim that a person acted BEFORE a transition, and a claim about order needs times on both sides."@en .

backlog:interactedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:HumanInteraction ; rdfs:range backlog:Role ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "interacted by"@en ;
    skos:definition "The role that acted. An interaction attributable to nobody cannot be audited, which is the whole reason for recording it."@en .

backlog:gatesTransition a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:HumanInteraction ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "gates transition"@en ;
    skos:definition "True where the work could not have advanced without this act. The mechanical fact behind in-the-loop supervision: a register may claim in-the-loop, and this is what makes the claim checkable."@en .

#################################################################
#  47. L4 — the lineage enforced, not advised (v1.19.0)
#################################################################

backlog:L4_LineageEnforced a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ConformanceLevel ;
    rdfs:label "L4 Lineage Enforced"@en ;
    skos:definition "L3 plus: the intent chain is complete and connected end to end, every objective carries a measurement rather than an intention, work is groomed to the granularity that can actually be executed, and an iteration connects to what ships. The checks that are advisory at lower levels become violations here. Adopted where a register must PROVE a mission was accomplished rather than report that work was done — the difference being that completion is a fact about effort and accomplishment is a fact about the world, and only the second requires a measurement. Deliberately a separate level and not a promotion inside L3: a level is a claim an adopter makes, and promoting checks inside an existing one would break every adopter who made a different claim."@en .

backlog:DeploymentUnit a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:WorkItemContainer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Deployment Unit"@en ;
    skos:definition "What actually ships: the grouping released to users, distinct from an Iteration, which is a time box, and from an Epic, which is a theme. Named because conflating the three is the commonest drift this framework has observed — an epic treated as a deployment subject looks like a plan, schedules nothing, and cannot be released, since nothing states what of it would go out. A deployment unit answers 'what did users receive', an iteration answers 'when was it worked', and an epic answers 'why'."@en .

backlog:deploysFrom a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:DeploymentUnit ; rdfs:range backlog:Iteration ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "deploys from"@en ;
    skos:definition "The iteration whose completed work this deployment carries. The link that makes cadence and release the same story rather than two: without it a register can show iterations closing and releases shipping with nothing connecting them, which is exactly the state in which a slipped iteration and a delayed release look unrelated."@en .

backlog:deploysItem a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:DeploymentUnit ; rdfs:range backlog:ProductBacklogItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "deploys item"@en ;
    skos:definition "A backlog item this deployment carries to users. Ranged on ProductBacklogItem rather than WorkItem because an ExecutionTask has no independent value to deliver, and an Epic — while formally a ProductBacklogItem — is rejected here by shape at L4: a theme is not a thing users receive."@en .

backlog:hasDeploymentDate a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:DeploymentUnit ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has deployment date"@en .

#################################################################
#  48. How a deployment chose what it carries (v1.20.0) — BRF-EP9
#################################################################

backlog:SelectionBasis a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Sel_HighestScored backlog:Sel_Dependency backlog:Sel_Committed backlog:Sel_Opportunistic ) ;
    rdfs:label "Selection Basis"@en ;
    skos:definition "How a deployment's contents were chosen. Recorded because a release grouped by theme and a release of the most valuable available work are otherwise indistinguishable in the register, and only one of them is a prioritisation decision. Closed at four, because the interesting question is not which method was used but whether value was consulted at all."@en .

backlog:Sel_HighestScored a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:SelectionBasis ;
    dcterms:source "Bagnall, Rayward-Smith & Whittley, The Next Release Problem (2001) — select the subset of highest value subject to constraint"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Highest scored"@en ;
    skos:definition "The highest-scoring deliverable work available at the release boundary. The default a value-driven roadmap should be able to claim, and the only basis under which a passed-over higher score is a defect rather than a choice."@en .
backlog:Sel_Dependency a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:SelectionBasis ;
    dcterms:source "Carlshamre et al. (2001) and Ngo-The & Ruhe (2008) — value dependencies constrain selection independently of value"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Dependency forced"@en ;
    skos:definition "Lower-scored work taken because higher-scored work depends on it. Value was consulted and overridden by structure, which is legitimate and different from not consulting it."@en .
backlog:Sel_Committed a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:SelectionBasis ;
    dcterms:source "Release planning practice: a commitment already given is honoured independently of current ranking"@en ;
    rdfs:label "Previously committed"@en ;
    skos:definition "Work carried because it was promised, independently of its current score. Honest, and worth distinguishing: a register where every release reads this way is one where scoring changes nothing."@en .
backlog:Sel_Opportunistic a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:SelectionBasis ;
    backlog:isFrameworkOriginal "No source found. Release-planning literature names value, dependency and commitment as selection criteria and does NOT name a category for a release that was not a prioritisation decision at all. Declared framework-original rather than attributed to a source it does not have: the category exists because without it such a release must be recorded as though it had been prioritised, and that is the misreport the vocabulary is meant to prevent."@en ;
    rdfs:label "Opportunistic"@en ;
    skos:definition "Whatever happened to be finished. The honest option for a release that was not a prioritisation decision, and the one whose absence from the vocabulary would force such a release to be recorded as though it had been."@en .

backlog:hasSelectionBasis a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:DeploymentUnit ; rdfs:range backlog:SelectionBasis ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has selection basis"@en .

backlog:passedOver a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:DeploymentUnit ; rdfs:range backlog:ProductBacklogItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "passed over"@en ;
    skos:definition "A higher-scored deliverable item this deployment did not carry, named deliberately. An ordering is a model and is sometimes wrong, so a release may reasonably depart from it; what the framework refuses is the departure being invisible. Requires a rationale, on the same principle as the ranking-fork resolution already in this subject."@en .

backlog:hasSelectionRationale a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:DeploymentUnit ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has selection rationale"@en ;
    skos:definition "Why higher-scored deliverable work was passed over. Required wherever passedOver is asserted: naming what was skipped without saying why records the fact and loses the reason, which is the half that would let anyone disagree."@en .

#################################################################
#  49. The chain records its own order (v1.21.0)
#################################################################

backlog:fillsScope a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Objective ; rdfs:range backlog:ScopeStatement ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "fills scope"@en ;
    skos:definition "The scope boundary this objective was written to fill. The direction is the whole point: an objective cannot name a scope that does not yet exist, so asserting this link is only possible in a lineage where the boundary was fixed first. Every other link in the intent chain already works this way — a Goal points at its Mission, an Objective at its Goal, each from the later-written element to the earlier — and the order is therefore readable from the graph without any element carrying a date. scopeRealizesObjective points the other way and encodes the opposite order; both are kept, because which one a lineage uses is exactly the fact worth recording. NOT declared owl:inverseOf scopeRealizesObjective: a reasoner materialising either from the other would erase the distinction and make every lineage look like both."@en .

#################################################################
#  50. Gated adaptation of a lineage to scope-first (v1.22.0)
#################################################################

backlog:LineageAdaptation a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Lineage Adaptation"@en ;
    skos:definition "The governed conversion of one lineage from scope-last to scope-first. An artifact rather than an activity because the conversion makes claims — that the boundary still holds, or that it does not — and a claim needs somewhere to live and someone to have made it. Adapting without one is the in-place re-pointing the procedure forbids: it produces links asserting an order that was never followed."@en .

backlog:adaptsScope a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:LineageAdaptation ; rdfs:range backlog:ScopeStatement ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "adapts scope"@en ;
    skos:definition "The scope-last boundary this adaptation converts. Functional: one adaptation converts one boundary, so a register with several scopes adapts them one at a time, at each one's own increment."@en .

backlog:AdaptationStage a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Stage_Assess backlog:Stage_FitGap backlog:Stage_Ruling backlog:Stage_Relink ) ;
    rdfs:label "Adaptation Stage"@en ;
    skos:definition "Where an adaptation has reached. Ordered and gated: each stage carries a control point that must be recorded and must have passed before the next may begin. The ordering is not ceremony — a fit-gap run before the current order is established measures against an unknown baseline, and a re-link performed before the ruling is the in-place conversion the whole procedure exists to prevent."@en .

backlog:Stage_Assess a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:AdaptationStage ;
    rdfs:label "Assess"@en ;
    skos:definition "Establish which order the lineage was actually built in, from the link directions rather than from anyone's account of it. A lineage already scope-first needs no adaptation; one asserting both directions must be resolved before anything else, because it currently records no order at all."@en .

backlog:Stage_FitGap a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:AdaptationStage ;
    rdfs:label "Fit-gap"@en ;
    skos:definition "Compare the standing boundary against the work actually underway and name every item that falls outside it. This is the measurement the ruling depends on, and it is the step a careful reader skips: a boundary drawn around past work usually LOOKS like it still fits, because it was built to."@en .

backlog:Stage_Ruling a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:AdaptationStage ;
    rdfs:label "Ruling"@en ;
    skos:definition "The owner decides, on the fit-gap evidence, whether the boundary is unchanged or must be rewritten. An owner decision and not a derivation: a gap is a fact, but whether it means the scope was wrong or the work was is a judgement about intent that no query can make."@en .

backlog:Stage_Relink a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:AdaptationStage ;
    rdfs:label "Re-link"@en ;
    skos:definition "Carry out what the ruling decided, and only then. Under an unchanged boundary the objectives are re-linked forward with fillsScope. Under a rewritten one the new boundary is written first and its goals and objectives are revisited beneath it, because a rewritten scope with the old goals hanging under it is the self-confirming boundary again."@en .

backlog:hasAdaptationStage a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:LineageAdaptation ; rdfs:range backlog:AdaptationStage ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has adaptation stage"@en .

backlog:AdaptationGate a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Adaptation Gate"@en ;
    skos:definition "The control point on one stage: an executable check, the result it must produce, and the observed result. Modelled on CrossCuttingInvariant, which already carries a check query and an expected result, because a gate declared passed without a check that could have failed verifies nothing."@en .

backlog:gateFor a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:AdaptationGate ; rdfs:range backlog:LineageAdaptation ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "gate for"@en .

backlog:gatesStage a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:AdaptationGate ; rdfs:range backlog:AdaptationStage ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "gates stage"@en .

backlog:hasGateResult a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:AdaptationGate ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has gate result"@en ;
    skos:definition "What the check actually returned, recorded verbatim. Compared against the expected result rather than summarised as pass or fail, so a reader can see what was measured and not only the verdict."@en .

backlog:gatePassed a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:AdaptationGate ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "gate passed"@en .

backlog:FitGapFinding a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Fit-Gap Finding"@en ;
    skos:definition "One item of work found to fall outside the standing boundary, with what it pursues and why the boundary does not cover it. Individually named rather than counted, because a count says a scope needs rewriting and a list says what it must now say."@en .

backlog:findingOf a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:FitGapFinding ; rdfs:range backlog:LineageAdaptation ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "finding of"@en .

backlog:findingItem a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:FitGapFinding ; rdfs:range backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "finding item"@en .

backlog:AdaptationOutcome a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Adapt_BoundaryHolds backlog:Adapt_BoundaryRewritten backlog:Adapt_NotRequired ) ;
    rdfs:label "Adaptation Outcome"@en ;
    skos:definition "What the ruling decided. The branch the whole procedure turns on: an unchanged boundary is cheap and needs only re-linking, a rewritten one obliges the goals and objectives beneath it to be revisited, and not-required is the honest answer for a lineage already scope-first."@en .

backlog:Adapt_BoundaryHolds a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:AdaptationOutcome ;
    rdfs:label "Boundary holds"@en ;
    skos:definition "The fit-gap found nothing outside the scope, so the boundary stands and only the link direction changes. Legitimate, and the outcome most likely to be claimed without the measurement: a scope drawn around past work fits that work by construction, so this outcome must rest on a recorded fit-gap and not on inspection."@en .

backlog:Adapt_BoundaryRewritten a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:AdaptationOutcome ;
    rdfs:label "Boundary rewritten"@en ;
    skos:definition "The fit-gap found work the boundary does not cover, so the scope is rewritten and the lineage beneath it revisited. Requires a ScopeChange recording what was admitted and why."@en .

backlog:Adapt_NotRequired a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:AdaptationOutcome ;
    rdfs:label "Not required"@en ;
    skos:definition "The lineage was already scope-first. Recorded rather than left silent, so a register can show that adaptation was considered and found unnecessary."@en .

backlog:hasAdaptationOutcome a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:LineageAdaptation ; rdfs:range backlog:AdaptationOutcome ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has adaptation outcome"@en .

backlog:rewritesScopeAs a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:LineageAdaptation ; rdfs:range backlog:ScopeStatement ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "rewrites scope as"@en ;
    skos:definition "The new boundary a rewriting adaptation produced. A NEW ScopeStatement, never an edit of the old one: the old boundary is the record of what was believed at the time, and editing it would erase the reason the adaptation was needed."@en .

#################################################################
#  51. Initiative kind: development or maintenance (v1.23.0)
#
#  Sources, read rather than recalled:
#    ISO/IEC/IEEE 12207 — Development and Maintenance are distinct
#      life-cycle processes; maintenance "sustains the capability of
#      the system to provide a service".
#    ISO/IEC/IEEE 14764:2022 — four maintenance types over two
#      dimensions, timing x goal; also ModificationRequest and
#      ProblemReport as the triggering artifacts.
#    Lientz & Swanson (1980) — the original three categories that
#      14764 formalised and extended.
#################################################################

backlog:InitiativeKind a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Kind_InitialDevelopment backlog:Kind_EvolutionaryDevelopment
                backlog:Kind_Migration backlog:Kind_Retirement backlog:Kind_Maintenance ) ;
    rdfs:label "Initiative Kind"@en ;
    skos:definition "Whether an initiative creates capability that did not exist, or sustains capability already delivered. The distinction is ISO/IEC/IEEE 12207's, where Development and Maintenance are separate life-cycle processes, and it matters here because the two are governed differently: development work is bounded by a scope written in advance, while maintenance work is triggered by something arriving from outside — a modification request or a problem report — and its scope is set by what arrived."@en .

backlog:Kind_InitialDevelopment a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:InitiativeKind ;
    rdfs:label "Initial development"@en ;
    skos:definition "The first build of a product. Distinguished from every later one because there is no installed base to preserve, no migration to plan and no compatibility to break — the decisions are unconstrained in a way they will never be again. Project scale; produces the first major version."@en .

backlog:Kind_EvolutionaryDevelopment a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:InitiativeKind ;
    rdfs:label "Evolutionary development"@en ;
    skos:definition "A new version built after an earlier one is complete. Still project scale and still a major increment, but constrained where the first build was not: something already exists, is in use, and either has to keep working or has to be migrated deliberately. Calling this maintenance because it touches an existing product is the commonest miscategorisation, and the version increment is what settles it."@en .

backlog:Kind_Migration a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:InitiativeKind ;
    rdfs:label "Migration"@en ;
    skos:definition "Moving a product to a new environment or platform while preserving its capability. A distinct process in ISO/IEC/IEEE 14764 rather than a maintenance type, and project scale here because it usually forces a major increment: what users can do is unchanged, but what they must do to keep using it is not."@en .

backlog:Kind_Retirement a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:InitiativeKind ;
    rdfs:label "Retirement"@en ;
    skos:definition "Withdrawing a product from service. A 14764 process in its own right, and modelled because a register with no way to say a thing is being switched off records its last release as though it were still alive."@en .

backlog:Kind_Maintenance a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:InitiativeKind ;
    rdfs:label "Maintenance"@en ;
    skos:definition "Sustains capability already delivered, per ISO/IEC/IEEE 12207's maintenance process. Smaller in granularity than a project and moves a minor or patch version, never a major — that is the discriminator, and it is a fact about what shipped rather than a judgement about how the work felt. Requires a maintenance category, because 'maintenance' alone conflates fixing what is broken with improving what works."@en .

backlog:hasInitiativeKind a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Initiative ;
    rdfs:range backlog:InitiativeKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has initiative kind"@en .

backlog:MaintenanceTiming a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Timing_Reactive backlog:Timing_Proactive ) ;
    rdfs:label "Maintenance Timing"@en ;
    skos:definition "One of 14764's two classifying dimensions: whether the change answers something that has already happened, or anticipates something that has not. Recorded as an axis rather than folded into a category name so that a reader can disagree with the classification on its merits."@en .

backlog:Timing_Reactive a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:MaintenanceTiming ;
    rdfs:label "Reactive"@en ;
    skos:definition "The change answers something that has already occurred — a fault reported, an environment that has already moved."@en .
backlog:Timing_Proactive a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:MaintenanceTiming ;
    rdfs:label "Proactive"@en ;
    skos:definition "The change anticipates. Nothing has failed and nothing has forced it, which is why proactive work is the first cut when a budget tightens and the reason it must be argued rather than assumed."@en .

backlog:MaintenanceGoal a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Goal_Correction backlog:Goal_Enhancement ) ;
    rdfs:label "Maintenance Goal"@en ;
    skos:definition "14764's other dimension: whether the change restores intended behaviour or implements a new requirement. The standard is explicit that an enhancement is NOT a correction, and the line is where most classification disputes actually sit."@en .

backlog:Goal_Correction a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:MaintenanceGoal ;
    rdfs:label "Correction"@en ;
    skos:definition "Restores behaviour that was already intended. Nothing new is promised."@en .
backlog:Goal_Enhancement a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:MaintenanceGoal ;
    rdfs:label "Enhancement"@en ;
    skos:definition "Implements a new requirement. 14764: an enhancement is not a software correction — which is why calling an enhancement a fix is the commonest way unbudgeted scope enters a maintenance stream."@en .

backlog:hasMaintenanceTiming a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Initiative ;
    rdfs:range backlog:MaintenanceTiming ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has maintenance timing"@en .

backlog:hasMaintenanceGoal a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Initiative ;
    rdfs:range backlog:MaintenanceGoal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has maintenance goal"@en .

backlog:MaintenanceCategory a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Maint_Corrective backlog:Maint_Preventive
                backlog:Maint_Adaptive backlog:Maint_Perfective backlog:Maint_Additive ) ;
    rdfs:label "Maintenance Category"@en ;
    skos:definition "The 14764 category, DERIVED from timing and goal rather than chosen independently: reactive correction is corrective, proactive correction is preventive, reactive enhancement is adaptive, proactive enhancement is perfective. Additive is 14764:2022's fifth, a proactive enhancement that adds function to existing software; the standard notes it as optional and it is offered rather than required here."@en .

backlog:Maint_Corrective a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:MaintenanceCategory ;
    rdfs:label "Corrective"@en ;
    skos:definition "Reactive correction: modification after delivery to correct discovered problems (14764). Something is broken and has been noticed."@en .
backlog:Maint_Preventive a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:MaintenanceCategory ;
    rdfs:label "Preventive"@en ;
    skos:definition "Proactive correction: modification to correct latent faults before they manifest as failures (14764). Nothing has failed yet, which is why this work is chronically deferred."@en .
backlog:Maint_Adaptive a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:MaintenanceCategory ;
    rdfs:label "Adaptive"@en ;
    skos:definition "Reactive enhancement: modification to keep the product usable in a changed or changing environment (14764). The world moved; the software has to follow."@en .
backlog:Maint_Perfective a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:MaintenanceCategory ;
    rdfs:label "Perfective"@en ;
    skos:definition "Proactive enhancement: modification to provide enhancements for users, or to improve performance, maintainability or other attributes (14764). Nothing forced it."@en .
backlog:Maint_Additive a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:MaintenanceCategory ;
    rdfs:label "Additive"@en ;
    skos:definition "14764:2022's optional fifth type: an enhancement that adds function to existing software, distinguished from perfective by changing the existing product rather than improving its qualities. Offered because the standard names it; an adopter using only the classic four is conformant."@en .

backlog:hasMaintenanceCategory a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Initiative ;
    rdfs:range backlog:MaintenanceCategory ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has maintenance category"@en .

backlog:ModificationRequest a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Modification Request"@en ;
    skos:definition "14764's term for what arrives from outside and triggers maintenance work: a request to change a delivered product. Modelled because maintenance scope is set by what arrived rather than by a boundary written in advance, so the arriving request is the only place that scope can be read from."@en .

backlog:ProblemReport a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:ModificationRequest ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Problem Report"@en ;
    skos:definition "A modification request denoting a fault (14764). A subclass rather than a sibling because 14764 is explicit that problem reports are either submitted directly or established after impact analysis on a modification request — the fault is discovered, not declared at arrival."@en .

backlog:triggeredBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Initiative ;
    rdfs:range backlog:ModificationRequest ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "triggered by"@en ;
    skos:definition "The request this maintenance work answers. Its absence on maintenance work is the state in which nobody can say who asked."@en .

backlog:hasRequestText a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ModificationRequest ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has request text"@en .

backlog:requestedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ModificationRequest ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "requested at"@en .

#################################################################
#  52. The version increment an initiative produces
#
#  The owner's discriminator, and a better one than any prose test:
#  a project moves the MAJOR version, maintenance moves a minor or
#  patch. "Does this create new capability" is a judgement; "did
#  this force a major" is a fact about what shipped.
#################################################################

backlog:VersionIncrement a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Ver_Major backlog:Ver_Minor backlog:Ver_Patch backlog:Ver_None ) ;
    rdfs:label "Version Increment"@en ;
    skos:definition "The version movement an initiative produces, on the semantic-versioning reading this package already applies to itself. Recorded because it is the objective test of whether something was a project or maintenance: an initiative claiming maintenance while forcing a major is misclassified, whatever it was called at the time."@en .

backlog:Ver_Major a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:VersionIncrement ;
    rdfs:label "Major"@en ;
    skos:definition "Breaking change or a new product generation. Project scale by definition — if a piece of work forces one, calling it maintenance is wrong regardless of how small it looked."@en .
backlog:Ver_Minor a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:VersionIncrement ;
    rdfs:label "Minor"@en ;
    skos:definition "Backwards-compatible addition. The normal increment for additive and adaptive maintenance."@en .
backlog:Ver_Patch a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:VersionIncrement ;
    rdfs:label "Patch"@en ;
    skos:definition "Backwards-compatible fix, no vocabulary or interface change. The normal increment for corrective and preventive maintenance."@en .
backlog:Ver_None a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:VersionIncrement ;
    rdfs:label "None"@en ;
    skos:definition "Ships nothing versioned. The honest answer for an initiative that never reaches users — a spike, an investigation, a retirement of something already dark — and its absence from the vocabulary would force such work to claim an increment it did not produce."@en .

backlog:producesIncrement a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Initiative ; rdfs:range backlog:VersionIncrement ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "produces increment"@en ;
    skos:definition "The version movement this initiative produces. Functional: an initiative producing two different increments is two initiatives."@en .

#################################################################
#  53. A mission answers to something (v1.25.0)
#
#  Mission carried two properties: a free-text statement and a
#  pointer to the container. Nothing checked it against anything.
#  Objectives are measured, scope is fit-gapped, goals connect
#  upward — the mission, alone in the intent chain, was
#  unfalsifiable. In a framework whose entire argument is that
#  unfalsifiable claims drift, that is the one place drift was
#  guaranteed to go unnoticed, and it did: five missions were
#  authored by a session over four days, each attributed to the
#  owner, each summarising work already shipped, each narrower
#  than the last. The register read back exactly what had been
#  written into it, which is what a self-confirming claim does.
#################################################################

backlog:MissionOrigin a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Origin_OwnerStated backlog:Origin_SessionDrafted backlog:Origin_Derived ) ;
    rdfs:label "Mission Origin"@en ;
    skos:definition "Where a mission statement's words came from. decidedBy already records who is ACCOUNTABLE for a mission; it does not record who AUTHORED it, and the two silently diverge when a session drafts a statement and attributes it to the owner. The distinction only matters in one direction, which is why it is worth the vocabulary: a mission the owner stated can correct a session's reading of it, and a mission a session drafted cannot correct anything, because it is downstream of the very understanding it appears to justify."@en .

backlog:Origin_OwnerStated a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:MissionOrigin ;
    rdfs:label "Owner stated"@en ;
    skos:definition "The owner supplied the words. The only origin under which a mission is evidence of intent rather than a record of what was built."@en .
backlog:Origin_SessionDrafted a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:MissionOrigin ;
    rdfs:label "Session drafted"@en ;
    skos:definition "A session wrote the statement, whoever it was attributed to. Legitimate as a working artifact and dangerous as a source: a session drafting a mission after the work exists writes a summary of that work, and a summary cannot contradict its source."@en .
backlog:Origin_Derived a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:MissionOrigin ;
    rdfs:label "Derived"@en ;
    skos:definition "Restated from a document outside the register — a charter, a contract, a standard. Traceable to something, which is what separates it from a session draft."@en .

backlog:hasMissionOrigin a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Mission ; rdfs:range backlog:MissionOrigin ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has mission origin"@en .

backlog:missionSource a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Mission ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "mission source"@en ;
    skos:definition "Where the words came from, named precisely enough to be found again: a dated instruction, a charter section, a standard clause. Required for a derived mission and for an owner-stated one, because 'the owner said so' with no pointer is indistinguishable from a session's paraphrase of what it believed the owner meant."@en .

backlog:supersedesMission a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Mission ; rdfs:range backlog:Mission ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "supersedes mission"@en ;
    skos:definition "A mission this one replaces. The superseded mission is retained, never deleted: it records what a development believed its purpose was, and the difference between that and the corrected statement is the most useful thing a drift leaves behind."@en .

#################################################################
#  54. The intent chain is traversable end to end (v1.26.0)
#
#  Measured before writing: Mission -> Goal -> Objective -> WorkItem
#  traverses in one query. Scope does NOT sit on that path. It hangs
#  off the container via hasScopeStatement, and the mission hangs off
#  the same container via missionFor, so the two only meet through a
#  join on the container they share. In this package's own register
#  that is six missions and four scopes on one container: which scope
#  belongs to which mission is unanswerable.
#
#  The v3.1.0 ceremony says Mission -> Scope -> Goal -> Objective.
#  The vocabulary recorded three of those four steps. The step the
#  ceremony puts SECOND was the one with no link at all.
#################################################################

backlog:scopeForMission a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ScopeStatement ; rdfs:range backlog:Mission ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "scope for mission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The mission this boundary was drawn to serve. Direction is Scope -> Mission, matching every other link in the intent chain: the later-written element points at the earlier, so asserting it is only possible once the mission exists. Functional, because a boundary serving two missions is two boundaries — and a register that cannot say which scope belongs to which mission cannot check that its work sits inside the right one."@en .

backlog:roadmapRealises a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Roadmap ; rdfs:range backlog:Objective ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "roadmap realises"@en ;
    skos:definition "An objective this roadmap orders work toward. Without it a roadmap connects only to its backlog and sits outside the intent chain entirely: the ordering can be read, and what the ordering is FOR cannot. A roadmap ranking work that advances objectives it does not name is ordering by something other than the intent it claims to serve."@en .

#################################################################
#  55. Grooming: what a story was analysed FOR (v1.27.0) — BRF-EP12
#
#  Source: Satzinger, Jackson & Burd, "Systems Analysis and Design
#  in a Changing World", ch.6 — the design activities a piece of
#  work is analysed against before it is built. Adopted as CONCERNS
#  rather than as activities: the framework governs the register a
#  method produces, so what it can check is whether a story was
#  analysed against the concerns that apply to it, not whether a
#  team performed a named activity in a named order.
#
#  RefinementEvent already exists with an outcome, a time and an
#  actor. One event of ANY kind satisfied Ready, so a story could
#  reach execution with no analysis of what it actually needed.
#  What is added is which concern an event addressed.
#################################################################

backlog:DesignConcern a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Concern_Data backlog:Concern_Interface backlog:Concern_Interaction
                backlog:Concern_Architecture backlog:Concern_Security ) ;
    rdfs:label "Design Concern"@en ;
    skos:definition "A dimension a story must be analysed against before it can be built, taken from the design activities in Satzinger et al. ch.6. Closed at five because the source names five, not because five is a natural number of concerns; an adopter needing a sixth should say so rather than overload one of these. Concerns are what a story is analysed FOR — the framework cannot check that a team held a meeting, only that the analysis a story needed was recorded."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Satzinger, Jackson & Burd, Systems Analysis and Design in a Changing World, ch.6"@en .

backlog:Concern_Data a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:DesignConcern ;
    rdfs:label "Data"@en ;
    skos:definition "What persists, and its structure. Applies wherever a story creates, changes or retires stored state — the concern whose omission is cheapest to skip and most expensive to correct, because a schema in use is difficult to change."@en ;
    backlog:isFrameworkOriginal "The parent class borrows its five-way split from Satzinger et al. ch.6, whose 'designing databases' activity is the nearest named counterpart. No single converging seminal source was found for this concern at the level defined here: the source describes an activity performed once for a system, not a per-story analysis criterion a backlog item is checked against. Declared framework-original at this level of specificity rather than attributed to a source it does not have."@en .
backlog:Concern_Interface a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:DesignConcern ;
    rdfs:label "Interface"@en ;
    skos:definition "How the system is reached from outside: an API, a message contract, a file exchange. Applies wherever a story changes what another system may rely on."@en ;
    backlog:isFrameworkOriginal "The parent class borrows its five-way split from Satzinger et al. ch.6, whose 'designing system interfaces' activity is the nearest named counterpart. No single converging seminal source was found for this concern at the level defined here: the source describes an activity performed once for a system, not a per-story analysis criterion a backlog item is checked against. Declared framework-original at this level of specificity rather than attributed to a source it does not have."@en .
backlog:Concern_Interaction a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:DesignConcern ;
    rdfs:label "Interaction"@en ;
    skos:definition "How a person uses the capability. Applies wherever a story changes what a user does, as distinct from what the system stores or exposes."@en ;
    backlog:isFrameworkOriginal "The parent class borrows its five-way split from Satzinger et al. ch.6, whose 'designing the user interface' activity is the nearest named counterpart. No single converging seminal source was found for this concern at the level defined here: the source describes an activity performed once for a system, not a per-story analysis criterion a backlog item is checked against. Declared framework-original at this level of specificity rather than attributed to a source it does not have."@en .
backlog:Concern_Architecture a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:DesignConcern ;
    rdfs:label "Architecture"@en ;
    skos:definition "Where the capability sits in the system's structure, and what it depends on. Applies wherever a story introduces a component, a dependency or a deployment boundary."@en ;
    backlog:isFrameworkOriginal "The parent class borrows its five-way split from Satzinger et al. ch.6, whose 'architectural design' activity is the nearest named counterpart. No single converging seminal source was found for this concern at the level defined here: the source describes an activity performed once for a system, not a per-story analysis criterion a backlog item is checked against. Declared framework-original at this level of specificity rather than attributed to a source it does not have."@en .
backlog:Concern_Security a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:DesignConcern ;
    rdfs:label "Security"@en ;
    skos:definition "Who may do this, and what it exposes. Applies wherever a story touches identity, authorisation or data a policy governs."@en ;
    backlog:isFrameworkOriginal "The parent class borrows its five-way split from Satzinger et al. ch.6, whose 'controls and security' design activity is the nearest named counterpart. No single converging seminal source was found for this concern at the level defined here: the source describes an activity performed once for a system, not a per-story analysis criterion a backlog item is checked against. Declared framework-original at this level of specificity rather than attributed to a source it does not have."@en .

backlog:addressesConcern a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:RefinementEvent ; rdfs:range backlog:DesignConcern ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "addresses concern"@en ;
    skos:definition "The concern this refinement analysed. Without it a refinement records that someone looked at a story and not what they looked at, so a story with five applicable concerns and one meeting is indistinguishable from one that was fully analysed."@en .

backlog:hasApplicableConcern a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ; rdfs:range backlog:DesignConcern ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has applicable concern"@en ;
    skos:definition "A concern this story's work touches, and must therefore be analysed against. Declared per story rather than derived, because whether a story touches persistent state is a judgement about the work and no query can make it. The declaration is what makes the grooming check possible: a framework cannot know which concerns apply, only that the ones claimed to apply were addressed."@en .

backlog:hasNoApplicableConcern a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has no applicable concern"@en ;
    skos:definition "A written statement that no design concern applies to this story, with the reason. Required rather than allowing silence, because a story that declares nothing and a story genuinely needing no design analysis are the same in the data — and the first is the common case. Saying so costs a sentence and makes the claim arguable."@en .

#################################################################
#  56. What kind of work a task is (v1.28.0)
#
#  Source: ISO/IEC/IEEE 12207 technical processes, clause 6.4.
#  Taken whole rather than sampled, because the standard's list is
#  a superset of the disciplines usually named informally and the
#  omissions are the interesting part: a register that records only
#  implementation looks full while mission analysis, system
#  analysis, integration, validation and transition are invisible.
#
#  Attached to ExecutionTask, which until now carried NO properties
#  of its own — a task could say what it was called and not what
#  kind of work it was. PBI vs task was distinguished at BRF-EP1;
#  what a task DOES was never modelled.
#
#  Deliberately NOT a workflow. The framework governs the register
#  a method produces, so it records what kind of work a task was,
#  never that the processes were performed in a prescribed order.
#################################################################

backlog:TaskType a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Task_MissionAnalysis backlog:Task_StakeholderNeeds backlog:Task_RequirementsDefinition backlog:Task_ArchitectureDefinition backlog:Task_DesignDefinition backlog:Task_SystemAnalysis backlog:Task_Implementation backlog:Task_Integration backlog:Task_Verification backlog:Task_Validation backlog:Task_Transition backlog:Task_Operation backlog:Task_MaintenanceTask backlog:Task_Disposal ) ;
    rdfs:label "Task Type"@en ;
    skos:definition "The technical process a task belongs to, from ISO/IEC/IEEE 12207 clause 6.4. Closed at fourteen because the standard names fourteen; an adopter needing a fifteenth should say so rather than overload one. The value of the closed set is what it makes VISIBLE — a backlog with fifty implementation tasks and no validation task is stating something about itself that a free-text label would hide."@en ;
    dcterms:source "ISO/IEC/IEEE 12207, clause 6.4 Technical processes"@en .

backlog:Task_MissionAnalysis a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:TaskType ;
    rdfs:label "Mission analysis"@en ;
    skos:definition "6.4.1 Business or mission analysis. Establishes what problem is being solved and why, before any solution is described. The process the owner called domain engineering: it defines the problem space, and a task here produces understanding rather than a component."@en .
backlog:Task_StakeholderNeeds a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:TaskType ;
    rdfs:label "Stakeholder needs and requirements"@en ;
    skos:definition "6.4.2. Turns what people need into stated stakeholder requirements. Distinct from the next process because a stakeholder requirement says what someone needs, and a system requirement says what the system must therefore do — conflating them is how solutions get written into problems."@en .
backlog:Task_RequirementsDefinition a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:TaskType ;
    rdfs:label "System/software requirements definition"@en ;
    skos:definition "6.4.3. States what the system must do to meet the stakeholder requirements. The owner's business analysis sits across this and the previous process."@en .
backlog:Task_ArchitectureDefinition a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:TaskType ;
    rdfs:label "Architecture definition"@en ;
    skos:definition "6.4.4. Establishes the structure: components, their responsibilities, and the boundaries between them. Separate from design definition because architecture fixes what may not change cheaply later."@en .
backlog:Task_DesignDefinition a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:TaskType ;
    rdfs:label "Design definition"@en ;
    skos:definition "6.4.5. Provides detail sufficient to implement, within the architecture. Where the five DesignConcerns are analysed."@en ;
    dcterms:source "ISO/IEC/IEEE 12207:2017, clause 6.4.5 Design definition process. Clause number confirmed via web search of secondary sources, not against the primary paywalled standard text; needs final confirmation by a holder of standard's-body access."@en .
backlog:Task_SystemAnalysis a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:TaskType ;
    rdfs:label "System analysis"@en ;
    skos:definition "6.4.6. Analytical work supporting a decision — trade studies, feasibility, performance modelling. The owner's technical analysis. Named separately because it produces a decision and its rationale, not a component, and is otherwise recorded as design or lost."@en ;
    dcterms:source "ISO/IEC/IEEE 12207:2017, clause 6.4.6 System analysis process. Clause number confirmed via web search of secondary sources, not against the primary paywalled standard text; needs final confirmation by a holder of standard's-body access."@en .
backlog:Task_Implementation a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:TaskType ;
    rdfs:label "Implementation"@en ;
    skos:definition "6.4.7. Builds the element. The task type most registers record and often the only one, which is why a backlog can look full while every other process is invisible."@en ;
    dcterms:source "ISO/IEC/IEEE 12207:2017, clause 6.4.7 Implementation process. Clause number confirmed via web search of secondary sources, not against the primary paywalled standard text; needs final confirmation by a holder of standard's-body access."@en .
backlog:Task_Integration a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:TaskType ;
    rdfs:label "Integration"@en ;
    skos:definition "6.4.8. Assembles elements into a working whole. Distinct from implementation: a set of correct parts that do not compose is the failure this process exists to catch."@en ;
    dcterms:source "ISO/IEC/IEEE 12207:2017, clause 6.4.8 Integration process. Clause number confirmed via web search of secondary sources, not against the primary paywalled standard text; needs final confirmation by a holder of standard's-body access."@en .
backlog:Task_Verification a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:TaskType ;
    rdfs:label "Verification"@en ;
    skos:definition "6.4.9. Confirms the element meets its specified requirements — was it built right. Half of the owner's testing."@en ;
    dcterms:source "ISO/IEC/IEEE 12207:2017, clause 6.4.9 Verification process. Clause number confirmed via web search of secondary sources, not against the primary paywalled standard text; needs final confirmation by a holder of standard's-body access."@en .
backlog:Task_Validation a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:TaskType ;
    rdfs:label "Validation"@en ;
    skos:definition "6.4.11. Confirms the element serves its intended use in its intended environment — was the right thing built. The other half of testing, and the one skipped when verification passes and nobody asks whether the requirement was correct."@en .
backlog:Task_Transition a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:TaskType ;
    rdfs:label "Transition"@en ;
    skos:definition "6.4.10. Moves the element into operation: deployment, migration, cutover, training. The owner's deployment."@en .
backlog:Task_Operation a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:TaskType ;
    rdfs:label "Operation"@en ;
    skos:definition "6.4.12. Runs the system in service."@en .
backlog:Task_MaintenanceTask a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:TaskType ;
    rdfs:label "Maintenance"@en ;
    skos:definition "6.4.13. Sustains the delivered capability. The task-level counterpart of Kind_Maintenance at initiative level."@en .
backlog:Task_Disposal a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:TaskType ;
    rdfs:label "Disposal"@en ;
    skos:definition "6.4.14. Retires the element and deals with what it leaves behind."@en .

backlog:hasTaskType a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ExecutionTask ; rdfs:range backlog:TaskType ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has task type"@en ;
    skos:definition "Which technical process this task performs. Functional: a task spanning two processes is two tasks, and splitting it is what makes either one estimable."@en .

backlog:coversTaskType a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:DesignConcern ; rdfs:range backlog:TaskType ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "covers task type"@en ;
    skos:definition "The task type a design concern implies work in. This is the join between grooming and execution: analysing a story for the Data concern implies design-definition work, and a story groomed for a concern whose task type never appears among its tasks was analysed and then not acted on."@en .

backlog:Concern_Data          backlog:coversTaskType backlog:Task_DesignDefinition .
backlog:Concern_Interface     backlog:coversTaskType backlog:Task_DesignDefinition , backlog:Task_Integration .
backlog:Concern_Interaction   backlog:coversTaskType backlog:Task_DesignDefinition , backlog:Task_Validation .
backlog:Concern_Architecture  backlog:coversTaskType backlog:Task_ArchitectureDefinition .
backlog:Concern_Security      backlog:coversTaskType backlog:Task_DesignDefinition , backlog:Task_Verification .

#################################################################
#  57. Named team roles, and iteration capacity (v1.29.0)
#
#  BRF-EP14. TeamRole is deliberately OPEN, so shipping named
#  individuals prescribes nothing: a register using none of them
#  stays conformant, and one needing a role not listed adds it.
#  What the framework requires is that a role it SHIPS names the
#  source it came from — Ex_InventedPractice applied to itself.
#################################################################

backlog:hasRoleSource a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:TeamRole ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has role source"@en ;
    skos:definition "The standard or work a shipped role was taken from. Required on roles the framework ships and not on roles an adopter adds: the framework must be able to defend its own vocabulary, and cannot demand a citation for a role somebody needs locally."@en .

backlog:Role_SystemsAnalyst a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:TeamRole ;
    rdfs:label "Systems analyst"@en ;
    skos:definition "Elicits and states requirements, and carries the stakeholder-needs and requirements-definition work. Named because a register that records only builders cannot show who established what was to be built."@en ;
    backlog:hasRoleSource "Satzinger, Jackson & Burd, Systems Analysis and Design in a Changing World" .
backlog:Role_DesignAuthority a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:TeamRole ;
    rdfs:label "Design authority"@en ;
    skos:definition "Holds the design decisions a story must not silently contradict. Distinct from an architect: the authority owns consistency across decisions already taken, not the structure itself."@en ;
    backlog:hasRoleSource "Satzinger, Jackson & Burd, ch.6" .
backlog:Role_Architect a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:TeamRole ;
    rdfs:label "Architect"@en ;
    skos:definition "Owns the structure and the boundaries that cannot change cheaply later — the architecture-definition process."@en ;
    backlog:hasRoleSource "ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010 architecture description" .
backlog:Role_HCIResearcher a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:TeamRole ;
    rdfs:label "HCI researcher"@en ;
    skos:definition "Establishes how a person will use the capability, and whether they can. The interaction concern's counterpart in people rather than artefacts."@en ;
    backlog:hasRoleSource "ISO 9241-210 human-centred design for interactive systems" .
backlog:Role_Tester a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:TeamRole ;
    rdfs:label "Tester"@en ;
    skos:definition "Performs verification and validation. ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119-3 names this role plainly; the framework uses the standard's word rather than a house term, because a role named differently in every register cannot be compared across two."@en ;
    backlog:hasRoleSource "ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119-3 test documentation" .
backlog:Role_TestManager a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:TeamRole ;
    rdfs:label "Test manager"@en ;
    skos:definition "Owns the test approach and its completion criteria, as distinct from performing tests."@en ;
    backlog:hasRoleSource "ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119-3" .

backlog:hasCommittedEffort a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Iteration ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has committed effort"@en ;
    skos:definition "Effort committed at planning, recorded rather than derived. Derived would be the sum of what is planned in now, which changes as work is added — and the question the discipline asks is what was committed AT COMMITMENT, against the capacity known then. A number that moves cannot report an over-commitment."@en .

#################################################################
#  58. Drift mechanisms (v1.30.0)
#
#  Built after an owner review of a long session in which the same
#  failure recurred four times. The failures were not careless: each
#  was a fluent, plausible artifact that nothing could contradict.
#  These are the four, and the mechanism for each.
#
#  RC1  A session authors an intent element and attributes it to
#       the owner. Fixed for Mission at v1.70.0 and NOWHERE ELSE —
#       ScopeStatement, Goal and Objective still carry no origin,
#       so the same failure simply moved one level down and
#       recurred in the very release that corrected it.
#
#  RC2  Goals connect to the Mission DIRECTLY, bypassing the Scope.
#       The owner's model is Mission -> Scope -> Goals -> Objectives,
#       where goals are DERIVED FROM the scope precisely so the
#       scope's fit to the mission is tested. With goals attached
#       straight to the mission, a goal can serve a mission the
#       scope never admitted and nothing objects.
#
#  RC3  An objective's metric can be unreachable by the work beneath
#       it. Obj_Grounded was pursued by one epic that could not move
#       its metric by construction; the epic completed, the metric
#       did not move, and no constraint noticed.
#
#  RC4  The backlog is anchored on iterations, not on packages. 49
#       of 53 Done items sit in no DeploymentUnit, while the package
#       shipped 78 real releases the register never recorded.
#################################################################

backlog:IntentOrigin a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:IOrigin_OwnerStated backlog:IOrigin_SessionDrafted backlog:IOrigin_Derived ) ;
    rdfs:label "Intent Origin"@en ;
    skos:definition "Where an intent element's words came from, generalised from MissionOrigin to every element in the chain. The v1.70.0 fix recorded this for Mission alone, and the same failure recurred one level down within the same release: a session wrote the scope, the goals and the objectives beneath a corrected mission and attributed all of them to the owner. Recording authorship on one node and not its children moves the blind spot rather than closing it."@en .

backlog:IOrigin_OwnerStated a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:IntentOrigin ;
    rdfs:label "Owner stated"@en ;
    skos:definition "The owner supplied the words. The only origin under which an intent element is evidence of intent rather than a record of what was built."@en .
backlog:IOrigin_SessionDrafted a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:IntentOrigin ;
    rdfs:label "Session drafted"@en ;
    skos:definition "A session wrote it, whoever it was attributed to. Legitimate as a working artifact and dangerous as a source: a session drafting intent for work it is already doing writes a summary of that work, and a summary cannot contradict its source."@en .
backlog:IOrigin_Derived a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:IntentOrigin ;
    rdfs:label "Derived"@en ;
    skos:definition "Restated from something outside the register — a charter, a standard, or the element above it in the chain. Traceable, which is what separates it from a session draft."@en .

backlog:hasIntentOrigin a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:ScopeStatement backlog:Goal backlog:Objective ) ] ;
    rdfs:range backlog:IntentOrigin ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has intent origin"@en .

backlog:derivesFromScope a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Goal ; rdfs:range backlog:ScopeStatement ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "derives from scope"@en ;
    skos:definition "The boundary this goal was derived from. THE MISSING LINK: Goal previously carried exactly one property, contributesToMission, so goals attached straight to the mission and the scope sat outside the path between them. Goals are derived from the scope precisely so that the scope's fit to the mission is what gets tested — a goal reaching the mission without passing through a boundary tests nothing, and a goal serving a mission the scope never admitted raises no objection."@en .

backlog:metricMovableBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Objective ; rdfs:range backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "metric movable by"@en ;
    skos:definition "A work item that can actually shift this objective's measured value. Distinct from pursuesObjective, which records what work CLAIMS to serve an objective: an epic can pursue an objective and be incapable of moving its metric by construction, which is how a register reaches every epic Done with its objectives untouched and nothing flagged. Asserting movability is a claim someone can be wrong about, and being wrong about it is visible when the metric does not move."@en .

#################################################################
#  59. Practice grounding, machine-checkable (v1.31.0) — BRF-EP16
#
#  Ex_InventedPractice says no practice the framework REQUIRES may
#  lack a named external source. It was a scope exclusion a reader
#  had to honour, with nothing checking it — so two ungrounded
#  practices sat in the framework for twenty releases and the
#  objective measuring them never moved.
#################################################################

backlog:isFrameworkOriginal a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "is framework original"@en ;
    skos:definition "A written statement that this practice has no external source and why it is kept anyway. Required rather than allowing silence, because a practice nobody sourced and a practice deliberately invented are identical in the data — and the first is the common case. Declaring it makes the decision arguable; leaving it blank makes it invisible."@en .

#################################################################
#  60. Evidence proportionate to autonomy (v1.32.0)
#
#  The last gap in Obj_IntentToWork, refused since it was found for
#  want of a named source. Ex_InventedPractice held the line for
#  four releases; the source exists and was simply not looked for
#  hard enough.
#
#  ISO/IEC 42001:2023 requires human oversight PROPORTIONATE to a
#  system's autonomy and risk: more autonomous operation demands
#  more granular oversight, not the same amount. EU AI Act Art. 14
#  states the same principle for high-risk systems.
#
#  Applied here: work produced with no human in or on the loop
#  must carry stronger proof than work a person supervised, because
#  the supervision that would have caught an error did not happen.
#################################################################

backlog:requiresIndependentEvidence a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:SupervisionMode ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "requires independent evidence"@en ;
    dcterms:source "ISO/IEC 42001:2023 — human oversight proportionate to autonomy and risk; EU AI Act Art. 14"@en ;
    skos:definition "Whether work produced under this supervision mode must be attested by evidence a person did not merely assert. True for Sup_None: with nobody in or on the loop, the supervision that would have caught an error did not happen, so the proof must come from a check that could have failed rather than from a claim. False where a human gated or watched the work — their judgement is itself the oversight the standard asks for."@en .

backlog:Sup_None backlog:requiresIndependentEvidence true .
backlog:Sup_InTheLoop backlog:requiresIndependentEvidence false .
backlog:Sup_OnTheLoop backlog:requiresIndependentEvidence false .

#################################################################
#  61. What the scope REQUIRES, independent of what was built
#     (v1.33.0)
#
#  A scope described capabilities in prose and nothing enumerated
#  them, so coverage could only be measured against the work that
#  existed. Obj_ScopeDelivered counted stories pursuing an
#  objective under the scope and divided by the same set: both
#  sides of the fraction were the backlog, and it read 100% whether
#  the scope was satisfied or merely emptied.
#
#  That is the epic-driven lineage in its exact form. Not that
#  epics were written first — for the live mission they were not —
#  but that the SCOPE HAD NO CONTENT OF ITS OWN to be measured
#  against, so whatever the epics delivered became the definition
#  of what the scope wanted.
#
#  A ScopeDeliverable is what the scope requires, written when the
#  scope is written, before any goal or epic exists. Work is then
#  measured against it rather than against itself.
#################################################################

backlog:ScopeDeliverable a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Scope Deliverable"@en ;
    skos:definition "A capability the scope requires, enumerated when the scope is written and before any goal, epic or story exists. The thing that makes a boundary measurable: without it, coverage can only be computed over the work that happens to exist, so the backlog defines what the scope wanted and the scope can never refuse anything. Distinct from a work item — a deliverable says WHAT MUST BE TRUE, a story says what someone will do about it."@en ;
    dcterms:source "PMBOK scope baseline: the scope statement plus its deliverables; ISO 21500 scope definition"@en .

backlog:requiresDeliverable a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ScopeStatement ; rdfs:range backlog:ScopeDeliverable ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "requires deliverable"@en ;
    skos:definition "A capability this scope requires. Direction is Scope to Deliverable because the deliverables ARE the scope's content, written with it — unlike every other link in the chain, which points from later to earlier."@en .

backlog:satisfiesDeliverable a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ; rdfs:range backlog:ScopeDeliverable ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "satisfies deliverable"@en ;
    skos:definition "The scope deliverable this work item makes true. Points from the work to the requirement, so a work item cannot name a deliverable that does not yet exist — which is what forces the scope to be written first. A deliverable with no work satisfying it is a gap the register can state; work satisfying no deliverable is work the scope never asked for."@en .

backlog:hasDeliverableText a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ScopeDeliverable ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has deliverable text"@en ;
    skos:definition "What must be true for this deliverable to be met, stated so a reader can disagree. A deliverable phrased as an activity rather than a condition cannot be checked."@en .

#################################################################
#  62. The lineage as a PIPELINE (v1.34.0)
#
#  Owner correction, and it was right twice over. First, the
#  v1.62.0 exclusion forbids requiring a DATE — it says nothing
#  about enforcing order, and this session had conflated the two.
#  Second, an ontology has dependency relations and order can be
#  enforced through them.
#
#  The model is another registrant's: a pipeline whose every stage CONSUMES the
#  artifact the previous stage produced. The dependency is then an
#  artifact, not a claim. A stage output carries a DIGEST of the
#  graph as it stood at that stage, and the digest is recomputable
#  — so the claim "the scope existed before any goal" is checked by
#  reconstructing the scope-stage graph and hashing it, not by
#  believing a timestamp.
#
#  What this buys over the signature checks at v1.84.0: a
#  backwards-built lineage can fabricate stage outputs, but it
#  cannot make a fabricated digest match a recomputation unless it
#  actually constructs each stage's state — which is doing the work
#  in order.
#################################################################

backlog:LineageStage a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Stage_Mission backlog:Stage_Scope backlog:Stage_Goal
                backlog:Stage_Objective backlog:Stage_Backlog ) ;
    rdfs:label "Lineage Stage"@en ;
    skos:definition "A stage of the lineage pipeline. Ordered and closed: each stage consumes the output of the one before, so a stage cannot be entered without the previous stage having produced something."@en ;
    dcterms:source "another registrant domain-ontology pipeline: each stage consumes the artifact the previous stage produced"@en .

backlog:Stage_Mission a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:LineageStage ;
    rdfs:label "Mission stage"@en ;
    skos:definition "The mission is stated. Nothing below it exists yet."@en .
backlog:Stage_Scope a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:LineageStage ;
    rdfs:label "Scope stage"@en ;
    skos:definition "The boundary and its deliverables and exclusions are written, against a mission that already exists and no goal that does not."@en .
backlog:Stage_Goal a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:LineageStage ;
    rdfs:label "Goal stage"@en ;
    skos:definition "Goals are derived from the scope. Objectives do not exist yet."@en .
backlog:Stage_Objective a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:LineageStage ;
    rdfs:label "Objective stage"@en ;
    skos:definition "Objectives are set to measure the goals, with baselines taken before any work is chosen."@en .
backlog:Stage_Backlog a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:LineageStage ;
    rdfs:label "Backlog stage"@en ;
    skos:definition "Work items are chosen against deliverables that already exist."@en .

backlog:stagePredecessor a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:LineageStage ; rdfs:range backlog:LineageStage ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "stage predecessor"@en ;
    skos:definition "The stage that must have produced an output before this one may be entered. Functional and acyclic: the chain is a line, not a graph."@en .

backlog:Stage_Scope     backlog:stagePredecessor backlog:Stage_Mission .
backlog:Stage_Goal      backlog:stagePredecessor backlog:Stage_Scope .
backlog:Stage_Objective backlog:stagePredecessor backlog:Stage_Goal .
backlog:Stage_Backlog   backlog:stagePredecessor backlog:Stage_Objective .

backlog:StageOutput a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Stage Output"@en ;
    skos:definition "What a lineage stage produced, carrying a digest of the graph as it stood when the stage closed. The digest is what makes the pipeline checkable rather than declarative: it is recomputed from the current register by restricting to the elements that stage may contain, so a fabricated output fails unless the state it claims was actually constructed."@en .

backlog:outputOfStage a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:StageOutput ; rdfs:range backlog:LineageStage ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "output of stage"@en .

backlog:consumesOutput a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:StageOutput ; rdfs:range backlog:StageOutput ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "consumes output"@en ;
    skos:definition "The previous stage's output this one was built from. The dependency that makes the sequence real: a stage output that consumes nothing is a stage that began from no prior state, which is only true of the first."@en .

backlog:closedAtCommit a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:StageOutput ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "closed at commit"@en ;
    skos:definition "The governed-repository commit at which this stage closed. THE ONLY EXTERNALLY WITNESSED ANCHOR available: a commit is append-only and held by a remote the author does not control, so a stage claiming to have closed at a commit can be checked against a history nobody can rewrite. Established by experiment — a digest over the register alone is computable from the final state and therefore proves nothing about order, while a commit is not. Its limit is equally real: git witnesses order BETWEEN commits and says nothing about order WITHIN one, so a lineage authored in a single commit is unordered evidence however it was actually built."@en .

backlog:hasStateDigest a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:StageOutput ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has state digest"@en ;
    skos:definition "SHA-256 over the canonical serialisation of every element this stage may contain, taken when the stage closed. Recomputable: restrict the current register to that stage's element types, canonicalise, hash, compare. A digest that does not reproduce means the state claimed was never the state that existed."@en .

backlog:producedByStage a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:Mission backlog:ScopeStatement backlog:Goal backlog:Objective backlog:WorkItem ) ] ;
    rdfs:range backlog:StageOutput ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "produced by stage"@en ;
    skos:definition "The stage output this element belongs to. An element cannot reference an output that does not exist, so asserting it requires the stage to have closed first."@en .

#################################################################
#  63. A deliverable names the mission clause it came from
#     (v1.35.0)
#
#  Audit finding: seven of this register's ten deliverables quote a
#  clause of the owner's mission statement, and three do not. Those
#  three were added at the owner's request in a later session and
#  the mission text was never amended — so the SCOPE became wider
#  than the MISSION and nothing objected.
#
#  This is the mirror of the defect corrected at v1.83.0. There the
#  scope had no content and the backlog defined it; here the scope
#  has content the mission never asked for. A boundary can fail in
#  both directions and only one was checked.
#################################################################

backlog:derivesFromMissionClause a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ScopeDeliverable ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "derives from mission clause"@en ;
    skos:definition "The words of the mission this deliverable was read from, quoted. Quoted rather than referenced because a citation can point at a mission that has since changed, while a quotation can be compared against the current text. A deliverable that cannot quote a clause is asking for something the mission does not say — which may be right, and requires the mission to be amended rather than the gap ignored."@en .

#################################################################
#  64. A package is identified, then delivered (v1.36.0)
#
#  Package has existed since early in this framework and has NEVER
#  been instantiated — zero instances across 91 releases, while
#  three DeploymentUnits were written. The two were conflated: a
#  Package is the deployable business function, identified when the
#  work is planned; a DeploymentUnit is the RECORD that it shipped.
#
#  The cost of the conflation was visible last release. Asked to
#  identify packages, this session wrote DeploymentUnits describing
#  what iterations WOULD ship, and four L4 clauses rejected each —
#  correctly, because a delivery record cannot describe unfinished
#  work. With no other concept available the conclusion drawn was
#  that packages cannot exist until the work is done. That was
#  wrong: the concept existed and was unused.
#
#  The mission says iterations are "planned to finish their PACKAGE
#  CONTENT" — a phrase that presupposes the package is identified
#  before the iteration runs.
#################################################################

backlog:deliversPackage a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:DeploymentUnit ; rdfs:range backlog:Package ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "delivers package"@en ;
    skos:definition "The package this deployment shipped. Nothing pointed at a Package before this property existed, which is why the class sat unused: it could be declared and not referred to. The direction is delivery to package, later to earlier, so a deployment cannot name a package that was never identified."@en .

backlog:targetsIteration a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Package ; rdfs:range backlog:Iteration ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "targets iteration"@en ;
    skos:definition "The iteration expected to finish this package's content. A package may target more than one when its content does not fit a single box — which is the case the framework must express, because splitting the work rather than stretching the box is the rule and a package spanning two iterations is what that looks like."@en .

backlog:hasPackageVersion a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Package ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has package version"@en ;
    skos:definition "The version this package will carry when released. Recorded at identification, so the increment is a decision taken with the content in view rather than a label applied afterwards."@en .

#################################################################
#  65. Model artefacts (v1.37.0) — BRF-EP17
#
#  Built to the decisions this session's grooming refinements
#  recorded, not to a fresh design:
#
#   - Diagram is NOT a separate class. A diagram is a ModelArtifact
#     whose kind is a diagram kind; a separate class would force
#     every query to union two.
#   - Artefacts hang off ExecutionTask, not Story. The task performs
#     the technical process, and a story with three design tasks
#     could not otherwise say which produced what.
#   - The two-way structure/behaviour split is a SEPARATE property,
#     so a query can ask for all behaviour artefacts without listing
#     seven kinds.
#
#  Source: UML 2.5 (OMG) — 14 diagram kinds in two categories.
#
#  Every class below is reachable: producesModelArtifact names
#  ModelArtifact as its range. The reachability gate now runs before
#  release, and without it UseCase and Specification would have
#  shipped unreferenceable — measured, not supposed.
#################################################################

backlog:ModelCategory a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Cat_Structure backlog:Cat_Behaviour ) ;
    rdfs:label "Model Category"@en ;
    skos:definition "Whether a model describes what the system IS or what it DOES. UML's own top-level split, kept as a property rather than left implicit in the kind so that asking for every behaviour artefact does not mean listing seven kinds and silently missing one when an eighth is added."@en ;
    dcterms:source "UML 2.5 (OMG): structure diagrams and behaviour diagrams"@en .

backlog:Cat_Structure a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ModelCategory ;
    rdfs:label "Structure"@en ;
    skos:definition "What the system is: its parts and their arrangement."@en .
backlog:Cat_Behaviour a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ModelCategory ;
    rdfs:label "Behaviour"@en ;
    skos:definition "What the system does: its dynamics over time."@en .

backlog:ModelKind a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Kind_ClassDiagram backlog:Kind_ObjectDiagram backlog:Kind_PackageDiagram backlog:Kind_CompositeStructure backlog:Kind_ComponentDiagram backlog:Kind_DeploymentDiagram backlog:Kind_ProfileDiagram backlog:Kind_UseCaseDiagram backlog:Kind_ActivityDiagram backlog:Kind_StateMachineDiagram backlog:Kind_SequenceDiagram backlog:Kind_CommunicationDiagram backlog:Kind_TimingDiagram backlog:Kind_InteractionOverview ) ;
    rdfs:label "Model Kind"@en ;
    skos:definition "The kind of model an artefact is, from the UML 2.5 taxonomy. Closed at fourteen because the standard names fourteen. An adopter modelling in a non-UML notation records the nearest kind and says so in the artefact's description rather than the framework inventing a fifteenth."@en ;
    dcterms:source "UML 2.5 (OMG), clause 6: 14 diagram kinds"@en .

backlog:Kind_ClassDiagram a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ModelKind ;
    rdfs:label "Class diagram"@en ;
    backlog:hasModelCategory backlog:Cat_Structure .
backlog:Kind_ObjectDiagram a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ModelKind ;
    rdfs:label "Object diagram"@en ;
    backlog:hasModelCategory backlog:Cat_Structure .
backlog:Kind_PackageDiagram a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ModelKind ;
    rdfs:label "Package diagram"@en ;
    backlog:hasModelCategory backlog:Cat_Structure .
backlog:Kind_CompositeStructure a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ModelKind ;
    rdfs:label "Composite structure diagram"@en ;
    backlog:hasModelCategory backlog:Cat_Structure .
backlog:Kind_ComponentDiagram a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ModelKind ;
    rdfs:label "Component diagram"@en ;
    backlog:hasModelCategory backlog:Cat_Structure .
backlog:Kind_DeploymentDiagram a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ModelKind ;
    rdfs:label "Deployment diagram"@en ;
    backlog:hasModelCategory backlog:Cat_Structure .
backlog:Kind_ProfileDiagram a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ModelKind ;
    rdfs:label "Profile diagram"@en ;
    backlog:hasModelCategory backlog:Cat_Structure .
backlog:Kind_UseCaseDiagram a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ModelKind ;
    rdfs:label "Use case diagram"@en ;
    backlog:hasModelCategory backlog:Cat_Behaviour .
backlog:Kind_ActivityDiagram a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ModelKind ;
    rdfs:label "Activity diagram"@en ;
    backlog:hasModelCategory backlog:Cat_Behaviour .
backlog:Kind_StateMachineDiagram a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ModelKind ;
    rdfs:label "State machine diagram"@en ;
    backlog:hasModelCategory backlog:Cat_Behaviour .
backlog:Kind_SequenceDiagram a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ModelKind ;
    rdfs:label "Sequence diagram"@en ;
    backlog:hasModelCategory backlog:Cat_Behaviour .
backlog:Kind_CommunicationDiagram a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ModelKind ;
    rdfs:label "Communication diagram"@en ;
    backlog:hasModelCategory backlog:Cat_Behaviour .
backlog:Kind_TimingDiagram a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ModelKind ;
    rdfs:label "Timing diagram"@en ;
    backlog:hasModelCategory backlog:Cat_Behaviour .
backlog:Kind_InteractionOverview a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ModelKind ;
    rdfs:label "Interaction overview diagram"@en ;
    backlog:hasModelCategory backlog:Cat_Behaviour .

backlog:hasModelCategory a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ModelKind ; rdfs:range backlog:ModelCategory ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has model category"@en .

backlog:ModelArtifact a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Model Artifact"@en ;
    skos:definition "What an analysis or design task produced: a model, named and typed. The framework records THAT an artefact exists, its kind, and what it describes — never the artefact's content, because a register holding diagrams becomes a modelling tool and the framework governs records about work rather than the work products themselves."@en .

backlog:producesModelArtifact a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:ExecutionTask backlog:RefinementEvent ) ] ;
    rdfs:range backlog:ModelArtifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "produces model artifact"@en ;
    skos:definition "The model this task or refinement produced. Domain is both because a design TASK produces artefacts during execution and a REFINEMENT produces them during grooming — the second is the join that makes grooming say what it yielded rather than only that it happened. Not functional: one task may produce a class diagram and a sequence diagram together."@en .

backlog:hasModelKind a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ModelArtifact ; rdfs:range backlog:ModelKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has model kind"@en .

backlog:describesItem a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ModelArtifact ; rdfs:range backlog:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "describes item"@en ;
    skos:definition "The work item this model is about. Distinct from the task that produced it: a sequence diagram made while designing one story may describe another."@en .

#################################################################
#  66. What a story carries (v1.38.0) — the mission's second half
#
#  The owner's mission: "a story carries its steps, interactions,
#  state changes, test cases and test data." Six concepts, built
#  here in one release because they are one sentence and splitting
#  them would leave a story able to say what a user does and not
#  what changes, or what to test and not what to test it with.
#
#  Built to the decisions the v1.94.0 grooming refinements
#  recorded, not redesigned:
#   - InteractionStep carries an ORDINAL, not a linked list: the
#     common question is what step 3 is.
#   - StateChange states are FREE TEXT, not an enumeration: the
#     framework governs registers for any domain and cannot know a
#     domain state machine.
#   - TestData does NOT carry the data, only what fixture state it
#     establishes; a register holding payloads becomes a data store.
#   - TestCase gains exercisesCriterion AND is named as a range by
#     coveredByCase, so it is reachable — the trap that lost
#     Package for 91 releases.
#
#  Sources: UML 2.5 interaction and state machine diagrams;
#  ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119-3 test case and test data specification.
#################################################################

backlog:Specification a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Specification"@en ;
    skos:definition "What a story does, stated so it can be checked against what was built. Distinct from an acceptance criterion: a criterion says whether the story is DONE, a specification says what it DOES. A story with criteria and no specification can be signed off without anyone able to say what was signed off."@en ;
    dcterms:source "ISO/IEC/IEEE 29148 requirements specification"@en .

backlog:hasSpecification a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ; rdfs:range backlog:Specification ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has specification"@en .

backlog:InteractionStep a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Interaction Step"@en ;
    skos:definition "One thing a person does, in order. Carries an ordinal rather than a link to the next step: a linked list makes insertion cheap and querying expensive, and the question asked of a specification is what step three is, not what follows step two."@en ;
    dcterms:source "UML 2.5 interaction diagrams (sequence, communication)"@en .

backlog:hasInteractionStep a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Specification ; rdfs:range backlog:InteractionStep ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has interaction step"@en .

backlog:hasStepOrdinal a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:InteractionStep ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has step ordinal"@en .

backlog:hasStepActor a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:InteractionStep ; rdfs:range backlog:TeamRole ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has step actor"@en ;
    skos:definition "Who performs this step. Range is TeamRole, which is open, so an adopter names their own actors without the framework prescribing a cast."@en .

backlog:hasStepAction a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:InteractionStep ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has step action"@en .

backlog:StateChange a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "State Change"@en ;
    skos:definition "What the system becomes, as distinct from what a person did. States are free text rather than an enumeration: the framework governs registers for any domain and cannot know a domain state machine. What it CAN check is that a change actually changes something."@en ;
    dcterms:source "UML 2.5 state machine diagrams"@en .

backlog:hasStateChange a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Specification ; rdfs:range backlog:StateChange ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has state change"@en .

backlog:hasFromState a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:StateChange ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ; rdfs:label "has from state"@en .
backlog:hasToState a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:StateChange ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ; rdfs:label "has to state"@en .
backlog:hasTrigger a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:StateChange ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ; rdfs:label "has trigger"@en .

backlog:TestCase a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Test Case"@en ;
    skos:definition "A named check that could fail. Evidence previously pointed at a fixture path and a verdict; a test case lets a reader disagree with the CHECK rather than only with the claim that checking happened."@en ;
    dcterms:source "ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119-3 test case specification"@en .

backlog:coveredByCase a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:AcceptanceCriterion ; rdfs:range backlog:TestCase ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "covered by case"@en ;
    skos:definition "A test case exercising this criterion. Exists in this direction as well as exercisesCriterion so TestCase is NAMED AS A RANGE: a class no property points at is one nobody uses, which is how Package sat unused for 91 releases."@en .

backlog:exercisesCriterion a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:TestCase ; rdfs:range backlog:AcceptanceCriterion ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "exercises criterion"@en .

backlog:TestData a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Test Data"@en ;
    skos:definition "The fixture state a test case runs against, described rather than contained. The framework records that a case needs a particular starting state and what that state is; holding the data itself would make the register a data store rather than a record about work."@en ;
    dcterms:source "ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119-3 test data requirements"@en .

backlog:runsOnData a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:TestCase ; rdfs:range backlog:TestData ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "runs on data"@en .

backlog:hasFixtureState a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:TestData ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has fixture state"@en .

#################################################################
#  67. States come from domain analysis (v1.39.0)
#
#  OWNER CHALLENGE, and it was right. The claim that "the framework
#  cannot know a domain state machine" was true and IRRELEVANT: the
#  framework does not need to know the states, it needs to know
#  WHERE THEY CAME FROM — and the lineage already carries that.
#
#  Task_MissionAnalysis is domain engineering. Task_RequirementsDefinition
#  is business analysis. Kind_StateMachineDiagram is a state machine.
#  All three existed. StateChange simply did not point at any of them,
#  so a state name was floating text with no source — exactly the
#  ungrounded-practice defect the framework forbids everywhere else,
#  reappearing inside a concept built to close it.
#
#  Free text remains, because the NAME of a state is a domain word.
#  What changes is that the name must be declared by an artefact
#  some analysis task produced.
#################################################################

backlog:declaredByArtifact a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:StateChange ; rdfs:range backlog:ModelArtifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "declared by artifact"@en ;
    skos:definition "The model artefact that establishes the state space this change moves within — normally a state machine diagram produced by mission analysis or requirements definition. Without it a state name has no source, and two stories can name the same state meaning different things with nothing to notice. The framework still does not know what the states ARE; it knows which analysis declared them, which is what makes the name checkable."@en ;
    dcterms:source "ISO/IEC/IEEE 12207 6.4.1 business or mission analysis; UML 2.5 state machine diagrams"@en .

backlog:declaresState a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ModelArtifact ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "declares state"@en ;
    skos:definition "A state this artefact establishes. Repeatable: one state machine declares many. This is where a domain's states enter the register — from analysis work, recorded as an artefact, rather than appearing first in whichever story happened to mention them."@en .

#################################################################
#  68. A scope says WHERE the work is (v1.40.0)
#
#  OWNER FINDING, and the cause is the FRAMEWORK, not only the
#  session that used it. ScopeDeliverable is defined as "a
#  capability the scope requires... a deliverable says WHAT MUST BE
#  TRUE". When the mission is already stated as conditions, that
#  definition produces deliverables that RESTATE THE MISSION —
#  "governance is expressed as ontology" is the mission's own
#  sentence with the subject changed.
#
#  A scope that restates its mission is unfalsifiable in a way the
#  v1.83.0 fix did not catch: it has content, the coverage figure
#  can fall, and every deliverable is still just the mission said
#  twice. Nobody can act on it, because it names no place.
#
#  ScopeArea names WHERE. It carries the artefacts to be worked on
#  and what is in them, measured. A deliverable then says what must
#  become true OF AN AREA, and the pair is actionable: the area
#  says where to go, the deliverable says what done looks like when
#  you get there.
#################################################################

backlog:ScopeArea a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Scope Area"@en ;
    skos:definition "A named place the work must reach: files, modules, or a body of existing material, with what they contain measured rather than described. Distinct from a deliverable, which says what must become true. A scope of deliverables alone restates its mission and cannot be acted on; a scope of areas alone is a task list with no finish condition. Both are needed, and the framework previously had only one."@en ;
    dcterms:source "PMBOK scope baseline: the work breakdown identifies the work, the scope statement its acceptance"@en .

backlog:coversArea a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ScopeStatement ; rdfs:range backlog:ScopeArea ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "covers area"@en .

backlog:areaLocation a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ScopeArea ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "area location"@en ;
    skos:definition "Where this area is, precisely enough to open: a path, a module, a named document. Repeatable when an area spans several."@en .

backlog:areaMeasure a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ScopeArea ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "area measure"@en ;
    skos:definition "What is in this area, counted at the time the scope was drawn. A measure rather than an adjective, so the work can be sized and so anyone can check later whether the area shrank."@en .

backlog:deliverableForArea a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ScopeDeliverable ; rdfs:range backlog:ScopeArea ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "deliverable for area"@en ;
    skos:definition "The area this deliverable states the finish condition for. A deliverable naming no area is a condition floating free of any place — which is how a scope comes to restate its mission."@en .

#################################################################
#  69. Goals face two ways (v1.41.0)
#
#  OWNER FINDING: goals must serve the mission INDEPENDENTLY of the
#  scope, and must also guarantee the scope completes without
#  redundant work. Those are different obligations and a goal set
#  can satisfy one while failing the other, silently:
#
#    scope-facing only  — every area finishes, and the mission is
#                         still unmet because the areas were the
#                         wrong ones. The boundary was insufficient
#                         and nothing said so.
#    mission-facing only — the mission's condition holds and half
#                         the scope was never touched, or work
#                         happened somewhere the scope never named.
#
#  GoalFacing makes the obligation explicit and lets a shape ask
#  whether both are present. It does not make a goal set correct;
#  it makes an incomplete one visible, which is the most a
#  constraint can do about sufficiency.
#################################################################

backlog:GoalFacing a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Facing_Mission backlog:Facing_Scope backlog:Facing_Containment backlog:Facing_Exclusion ) ;
    rdfs:label "Goal Facing"@en ;
    skos:definition "Which obligation a goal carries. A lineage needs all three and they are not interchangeable: a mission-facing goal can be met with the scope half-untouched, a scope-facing goal can be met with the mission unreached, and both can be met while work happened in places the scope never named."@en .

backlog:Facing_Mission a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:GoalFacing ;
    rdfs:label "Mission-facing"@en ;
    skos:definition "Holds when the mission's own condition is true, independently of how much of the scope was done. This is the goal that catches an insufficient boundary: if every area finishes and this goal is still unmet, the scope was wrong."@en .
backlog:Facing_Scope a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:GoalFacing ;
    rdfs:label "Scope-facing"@en ;
    skos:definition "Holds when every area the scope names has reached its finish condition. This is the goal that catches abandonment: a mission-facing goal can read met because the easy half was done."@en .
backlog:Facing_Containment a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:GoalFacing ;
    rdfs:label "Containment-facing"@en ;
    skos:definition "Holds when no work was done outside the areas the scope names. Without it a lineage can reach its mission and its scope while spending effort nobody asked for, and the register reports success — redundant work is invisible to any measure that only counts what was required."@en .

backlog:hasGoalFacing a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Goal ; rdfs:range backlog:GoalFacing ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has goal facing"@en .

backlog:goalCoversArea a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Goal ; rdfs:range backlog:ScopeArea ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "goal covers area"@en ;
    skos:definition "An area this goal is answerable for. Required of a scope-facing goal so that an area belonging to no goal is visible: an area nothing is answerable for will be done last or not at all."@en .

#################################################################
#  70. Scope has two layers, and an outside (v1.42.0)
#
#  Researched, not assumed. PMBOK distinguishes PRODUCT scope —
#  the features and functions of what is delivered — from PROJECT
#  scope — the work required to produce them. The framework had
#  neither distinction: ScopeArea and ScopeDeliverable were untyped,
#  so "which functionality" and "which work" were the same field.
#
#  PMBOK also treats explicit EXCLUSIONS as the principal safeguard
#  against scope creep. The framework had ScopeExclusion and
#  nothing measuring whether an exclusion held — an exclusion that
#  is never checked is a sentence, not a safeguard.
#
#  Facing_Exclusion completes the goal set: a goal that states what
#  must NOT happen, measurable, so a preventive mechanism has
#  something to attach to.
#################################################################

backlog:ScopeLayer a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Layer_Product backlog:Layer_Work ) ;
    rdfs:label "Scope Layer"@en ;
    skos:definition "Whether a boundary statement is about WHAT IS DELIVERED or about WHAT WORK IS DONE. PMBOK separates product scope from project scope, and the two fail differently: a product-layer error ships the wrong capability, a work-layer error spends effort in the wrong place. A framework carrying only one of them cannot tell those apart."@en ;
    dcterms:source "PMBOK: product scope (features and functions) versus project scope (work required)"@en .

backlog:Layer_Product a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ScopeLayer ;
    rdfs:label "Product layer"@en ;
    skos:definition "What functionality is or is not implemented. Answers: will the delivered thing do this?"@en .
backlog:Layer_Work a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ScopeLayer ;
    rdfs:label "Work layer"@en ;
    skos:definition "Which work is or is not done. Answers: will effort go here? A capability may be in the product scope while the work to build it sits outside this project's work scope — bought, inherited, or deferred."@en .

backlog:hasScopeLayer a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( backlog:ScopeArea backlog:ScopeDeliverable backlog:ScopeExclusion ) ] ;
    rdfs:range backlog:ScopeLayer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has scope layer"@en .

backlog:ProductScopeKind a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Kind_Functional backlog:Kind_NonFunctional ) ;
    rdfs:label "Product Scope Kind"@en ;
    skos:definition "Within product scope, whether a deliverable names a capability the artifact has (functional) or a quality it exhibits while having it (non-functional). A product-scope error of the first kind ships the wrong capability; of the second, ships the right capability unreliably, opaquely, or unsafely — the two fail as differently as product and work scope already do."@en ;
    dcterms:source "ISO/IEC 25010: System and Software Quality Requirements and Evaluation — functional suitability versus the eight non-functional quality characteristics"@en .

backlog:Kind_Functional a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ProductScopeKind ;
    rdfs:label "Functional"@en ;
    skos:definition "The deliverable names a capability the artifact has. Answers: does it do this?"@en .
backlog:Kind_NonFunctional a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ProductScopeKind ;
    rdfs:label "Non-functional"@en ;
    skos:definition "The deliverable names a quality the artifact exhibits while it does what it does — how reliably, transparently, or safely, not what. Answers: does it do this well?"@en .

backlog:hasProductScopeKind a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ScopeDeliverable ;
    rdfs:range backlog:ProductScopeKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has product scope kind"@en ;
    skos:definition "Narrower than hasScopeLayer's own three-class domain, deliberately: only a Deliverable makes the kind of capability-or-quality claim this axis distinguishes — a ScopeArea names a place and a ScopeExclusion names a work-boundary, neither of which is itself functional or non-functional."@en .

backlog:Facing_Exclusion a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:GoalFacing ;
    rdfs:label "Exclusion-facing"@en ;
    skos:definition "An INVERSE GOAL: it states what must not happen and is met by absence. Distinct from containment-facing, which asks whether work stayed inside the areas — an inverse goal names a specific thing the scope refused and can be measured against, so a preventive mechanism has something to attach to. PMBOK calls explicit exclusions the principal safeguard against scope creep; an exclusion nothing measures is a sentence rather than a safeguard."@en ;
    dcterms:source "PMBOK: project exclusions as the safeguard against scope creep"@en .

backlog:guardsExclusion a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Goal ; rdfs:range backlog:ScopeExclusion ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "guards exclusion"@en ;
    skos:definition "The exclusion this inverse goal keeps. Required of an exclusion-facing goal, so that an exclusion nothing guards is visible: the framework can then say which refusals are enforced and which were only written down."@en .

#################################################################
#  71. An objective that can be monitored, not only judged
#     (v1.43.0)
#
#  Measured before writing: an objective carries a baseline, a
#  target, a direction and a deadline, and observations carry a
#  value and a date. PROGRESS is therefore computable. PREDICTION
#  is not — nothing states what value was EXPECTED by a date, so no
#  observation can be early or late. An objective can only read met
#  or unmet, and only when the deadline arrives.
#
#  That is the difference between a measure and a monitor. A
#  monitor tells you a month early that the rate will not reach the
#  target; this framework has been telling owners at the end.
#################################################################

backlog:ObjectiveCheckpoint a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Objective Checkpoint"@en ;
    skos:definition "A value expected by a date, set when the objective is set. What turns a measure into a monitor: with it an observation can be compared against what was expected and reported as ahead, on track or behind, before the deadline. Without it the only question the register can answer is the last one."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Earned value management: planned value as a time-phased baseline against which actuals are compared"@en .

backlog:hasCheckpoint a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Objective ; rdfs:range backlog:ObjectiveCheckpoint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has checkpoint"@en .

backlog:checkpointDate a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ObjectiveCheckpoint ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ; rdfs:label "checkpoint date"@en .
backlog:expectedValue a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ObjectiveCheckpoint ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "expected value"@en ;
    skos:definition "The value the metric should have reached by this date if the objective is on course. Set at objective time and not revised: a checkpoint moved to match what happened measures nothing."@en .

backlog:MeasurementKind a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Meas_Counted backlog:Meas_Derived backlog:Meas_Judged ) ;
    rdfs:label "Measurement Kind"@en ;
    skos:definition "How a metric's value is obtained. Recorded because the three carry different weight: a counted value can be reproduced by anyone, a derived value depends on a query being right, and a judged value depends on whoever judged. An objective set on judgement is legitimate and must not be mistaken for one set on a count."@en .

backlog:Meas_Counted a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:MeasurementKind ;
    rdfs:label "Counted"@en ;
    skos:definition "Obtained by counting artefacts anyone can count: files, triples, rulings. Reproducible without trusting the counter."@en .
backlog:Meas_Derived a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:MeasurementKind ;
    rdfs:label "Derived"@en ;
    skos:definition "Computed by a named query over the register. Reproducible if the query is shipped, which is why the query must be."@en .
backlog:Meas_Judged a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:MeasurementKind ;
    rdfs:label "Judged"@en ;
    skos:definition "A person decided. Legitimate, and the weakest of the three: it cannot be reproduced, only agreed with or disputed."@en .

backlog:hasMeasurementKind a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Objective ; rdfs:range backlog:MeasurementKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ; rdfs:label "has measurement kind"@en .

backlog:hasMeasurementQuery a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Objective ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has measurement query"@en ;
    skos:definition "The query that produces this objective's value. Required of a derived objective so the number can be re-derived rather than believed — a derived figure whose query is not shipped is a judged figure wearing better clothes."@en .

#################################################################
#  72. A direction for an objective that must not move
#
#  Found by the inverse goals failing the L1 clause "target equals
#  baseline". For an ordinary objective that clause is right: a
#  target equal to the baseline asks for nothing.
#
#  An INVERSE goal is different in kind. Its objective is a CEILING
#  — the count must never exceed zero — not a trajectory from a
#  baseline to a target. Bending the baseline to satisfy the clause
#  would have made the number say something false; the clause was
#  right and the vocabulary was missing a case.
#################################################################

backlog:Dir_Hold a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:MetricDirection ;
    rdfs:label "Hold"@en ;
    skos:definition "The value must not move from its baseline. Used by an inverse goal, whose objective is a ceiling rather than a trajectory: the count of things that should not happen must stay where it is. Distinct from Dir_Decrease with a target already reached, which claims an improvement that never occurred."@en .

#################################################################
#  73. The same subject seen from both layers (v1.44.0)
#
#  Found by test drive, not by reading the diagram. Four areas have
#  an objective whose baseline EQUALS the area's measure exactly —
#  18 rulings and baseline 18, 23 tables and baseline 23, 186 rows
#  and baseline 186, 3 checks and baseline 3. The fifth had none.
#
#  It is not a missing objective. Area_QueryableGovernance and
#  Area_Governance are ONE subject seen twice: the work layer says
#  18 rulings sit in a document, the product layer says an adopter
#  can query 0 of them. Obj_RulingsQueryable 18 -> 0 measures both,
#  because the rulings becoming triples is the same event.
#
#  Giving the fifth area its own objective would count the same
#  work twice and let the scope read half-done when it was finished.
#  What was missing is the RELATION saying the two are one subject.
#################################################################

backlog:productViewOf a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ScopeArea ; rdfs:range backlog:ScopeArea ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "product view of"@en ;
    skos:definition "This product-layer area is the same subject as that work-layer area, seen from what an adopter receives rather than from where the effort goes. Stated so the pair is not counted twice: an area that is a product view of another needs no objective of its own, because the objective on the work-layer area measures the same event. Without this relation the two look like separate subjects and the scope reads half-finished when it is done."@en .

#################################################################
#  74. Grooming happens FOR an iteration (v1.45.0)
#
#  OWNER MODEL, and the framework could not express the last part
#  of it. A RefinementEvent records WHEN grooming happened and not
#  WHICH iteration it was preparing for — so grooming-per-sprint
#  could be performed and never checked.
#
#  The consequence was visible at v1.109.0: all nine stories were
#  detailed in one pass, three sprints ahead. That is not wrong in
#  itself, and it is not what the owner's model asks for, and
#  nothing in the register could tell the two apart.
#################################################################

backlog:groomsForIteration a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:RefinementEvent ; rdfs:range backlog:Iteration ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "grooms for iteration"@en ;
    skos:definition "The iteration this refinement was preparing an item for. What makes just-in-time grooming checkable rather than merely intended: without it a register cannot distinguish a story groomed the sprint before it starts from nine stories detailed at once, and the second is how detail goes stale before anyone builds against it."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Scrum Guide: product backlog refinement as an ongoing activity preparing items for upcoming sprints"@en .

#################################################################
#  75. Governance as ontology (v1.46.0) — It7, BRF-EP21
#
#  Built to the specification the story carries: read a ruling
#  heading, create a GovernanceRuling with its identifier,
#  statement and rationale; then locate the shape whose message
#  states it and link the two.
#
#  The 18 rulings move from markdown headings to triples. Nothing
#  is invented — each already has an identifier, a statement and a
#  rationale in the lineage discipline, and the class is the place
#  they can be queried from.
#################################################################

backlog:GovernanceRuling a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Governance Ruling"@en ;
    skos:definition "A decision about how the framework is governed, recorded so it can be queried rather than read. Eighteen existed as markdown headings in the lineage discipline: enforceable in the sense that shapes implemented them, and unreachable in the sense that no query could return one. A ruling nobody can retrieve cannot be checked against the shapes that claim to enforce it."@en ;
    dcterms:source "LINEAGE_OPERATING_DISCIPLINE, rulings G1 through G18"@en .

backlog:hasRulingIdentifier a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:GovernanceRuling ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ; rdfs:label "has ruling identifier"@en .
backlog:hasRulingStatement a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:GovernanceRuling ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has ruling statement"@en ;
    skos:definition "What the ruling decides, in one sentence. Separate from the rationale because the decision must be checkable against a shape and the reasoning must not be."@en .
backlog:hasRulingRationale a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:GovernanceRuling ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ; rdfs:label "has ruling rationale"@en .

backlog:enforcedByShape a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:GovernanceRuling ; rdfs:range rdfs:Resource ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "enforced by shape"@en ;
    skos:definition "The shape that implements this ruling. The link the discipline document could only assert in prose: previously a reader compared a heading against a suite by hand, and nothing objected when a ruling lost its shape. A ruling naming no shape is a decision nothing carries out."@en .

backlog:rulingSource a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:GovernanceRuling ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "ruling source"@en ;
    skos:definition "Where the ruling was written and by whom, quoted. Every one of the eighteen was ruled by the owner during a session; recording that keeps a ruling distinguishable from a convention a session adopted."@en .

#################################################################
#  76. The first exported table — It7, BRF-EP22 story 1
#
#  The completeness reporter's LAYERS list decided which classes
#  count as lineage layers and in what order. It lived in python:
#  changing what the framework considers a layer meant editing a
#  script, and no query could ask what the layers are.
#
#  Declared here as an ordered class list. The script reads it in
#  story 2; this story establishes the ontology side, because
#  deleting the python literal before the ontology carries it would
#  leave the reporter unable to run.
#################################################################

backlog:LineageLayer a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Lineage Layer"@en ;
    skos:definition "A class the completeness reporter counts as a layer of the intent chain, with the position it occupies. Exported from a python list where the order was expressed by list position and the meaning by a variable name — neither queryable, and both changeable by editing a script."@en .

backlog:layerClass a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:LineageLayer ; rdfs:range rdfs:Resource ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ; rdfs:label "layer class"@en .
backlog:layerOrdinal a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:LineageLayer ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "layer ordinal"@en ;
    skos:definition "Where the layer sits in the chain. An integer rather than list position, so the order survives being queried in any sequence — a list's meaning depends on nothing reordering it."@en .
backlog:layerAbsenceCost a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:LineageLayer ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "layer absence cost"@en ;
    skos:definition "What is lost when this layer is missing. The reporter held these strings inline; they are the reason a layer matters and belong with the layer."@en .

backlog:layerLabel a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:LineageLayer ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "layer label"@en ;
    skos:definition "How the layer is named in a report, which is not always its class name — Epic and Feature are one layer displayed as 'Epic/Feature'. Found when the reader was written: the exported table had dropped two of the original tuple's four fields, and a reader that returns fewer fields than its consumer unpacks fails at the first row."@en .

backlog:layerTier a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:LineageLayer ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "layer tier"@en ;
    skos:definition "The conformance level at which this layer's absence starts to matter. Held in the python table as a bare string and now queryable: which layers an L2 register may omit is a governance question, not a reporting detail."@en .

#################################################################
#  77. A story that carries a countable batch (v1.47.0)
#
#  Found by test drive when the table objective went behind its
#  checkpoint. One table per story reaches zero in late April
#  against a January deadline; batching fits, and batching hides.
#
#  The risk is specific and it scales linearly: a story is Done or
#  not, while the objective counts tables. A batch of eight with
#  one table blocked reports ZERO progress where one-per-story
#  would have reported seven. Measured: a blocked story hides 5%
#  of the work at batch 1, 18% at 4, 36% at 8, 50% at 11.
#
#  hasBatchSize and hasBatchCompleted let a story report partial
#  progress without being Done, so the measure stops depending on
#  the increment. The baseline stays recorded for comparison.
#################################################################

backlog:hasBatchSize a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has batch size"@en ;
    skos:definition "How many countable units this story carries. Declared so a reader can see that a single Done or not-Done covers more than one thing — a story of eight is eight chances to be wrong verified by one acceptance criterion, and that must be visible rather than inferred from the title."@en .

backlog:hasBatchCompleted a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItem ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has batch completed"@en ;
    skos:definition "How many of the batch are finished. The point of the pair: an objective counting units can read partial progress from a story that is not Done. Without it a batch decouples the measure from the increment, and seven finished tables inside one blocked story report as none."@en .

#################################################################
#  78. An iteration's length is measured, not assumed (v1.48.0)
#
#  OWNER FINDING: the dates were fiction. Measured from the real
#  publish commits, It7 took 32 minutes and It8 took 28. The
#  register declared fourteen days each — an overstatement of 667x
#  — and scheduled iterations for November when the day was the
#  27th of August.
#
#  Nothing caught it because iterationStart and iterationEnd are
#  asserted dateTimes and no clause compares them to anything that
#  happened. The same defect as committed effort one release ago:
#  a number stated rather than derived.
#
#  hasObservedDuration records what an iteration ACTUALLY took, in
#  minutes, so the next one can be planned from evidence. A
#  developer that runs in minutes and plans in fortnights is not
#  planning; it is copying a human cadence it does not have.
#################################################################

backlog:hasObservedDuration a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Iteration ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has observed duration"@en ;
    skos:definition "Minutes the iteration actually took, from the commit closing the previous one to the commit closing this. Recorded because the declared start and end are assertions and nothing compared them to the work: an autonomous developer completing a nine-point iteration in half an hour, planning in fortnights, is copying a cadence it does not have."@en .

backlog:hasDurationSource a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Iteration ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has duration source"@en ;
    skos:definition "Where the observed duration was read from — normally the two publish commits that bound the iteration. Required so a duration can be re-derived rather than believed, which is what separates it from the declared dates it replaces."@en .

#################################################################
#  79. Which quality metrics move with population (v1.49.0)
#
#  BRF-EP24, found by the script-decision audit. The quality
#  assessor held `if name in ("ClassRichness", "AveragePopulation")`
#  — a real classification about which OntoQA metrics respond to
#  individuals being added, written twice as a python tuple.
#
#  It decides which figures get a second reading against a merged
#  fixture. That is a statement about the metrics, not about the
#  script, so the ontology is where it belongs: an adopter adding
#  their own metric cannot register it as population-sensitive by
#  editing a tuple they do not ship.
#################################################################

backlog:QualityMetric a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Quality Metric"@en ;
    skos:definition "A structural measure of the ontology itself, from the OntoQA family. Named here so that which metrics exist, and which respond to population, is a queryable statement rather than a tuple inside the script that reports them."@en ;
    dcterms:source "OntoQA: Metric-Based Ontology Quality Analysis (Tartir et al.)"@en .

backlog:isPopulationSensitive a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:QualityMetric ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "is population sensitive"@en ;
    skos:definition "Whether this metric changes when individuals are added. The framework ABox holds framework-level individuals only, so population-sensitive metrics understate by design and are read twice — once alone and once with an adopter fixture merged. Both readings are reported rather than the flattering one, and which metrics need the second is now something a query can answer."@en .

backlog:hasMetricName a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:QualityMetric ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ; rdfs:label "has metric name"@en .

#################################################################
#  80. Not every table belongs in the ontology (v1.50.0)
#
#  Found counting the remaining tables for It10. There are 26, not
#  the 22 the objective assumed — the count GREW because this
#  lineage shipped new scripts carrying new tables.
#
#  More important: only 14 of them hold CLASSIFICATION knowledge.
#  The other 12 are operational — which directories to drop from a
#  distribution, which file extensions are text, which paths are
#  historical. Three tests, all agreeing:
#
#   1. The mission asks for classes, relationships, properties and
#      instances OF THE DOMAIN. DROP_DIRS is build configuration.
#   2. Nothing an adopter can observe breaks if it stays in python:
#      the ontology says nothing about distributions, so no query
#      becomes answerable.
#   3. Exporting it would ADD a class the exported material does
#      not require — which is what Obj_NoNewClasses is held at zero
#      to prevent. The migration would breach the containment goal
#      in the act of satisfying the scope goal.
#
#  TableKind makes the distinction queryable so the objective can
#  count the right population instead of every capitalised name.
#################################################################

backlog:TableKind a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Table_Classification backlog:Table_Operational ) ;
    rdfs:label "Table Kind"@en ;
    skos:definition "Whether a module-level table states something about the DOMAIN or something about how the tooling runs. The distinction decides whether a table belongs in the ontology: exporting operational configuration would put build settings in an ontology about backlogs, and would add classes the exported material does not require."@en .

backlog:Table_Classification a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:TableKind ;
    rdfs:label "Classification"@en ;
    skos:definition "States what something IS — which task types exist, which metrics respond to population, which stages a pipeline has. Belongs in the ontology: an adopter cannot extend it by editing a script they do not ship."@en .
backlog:Table_Operational a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:TableKind ;
    rdfs:label "Operational"@en ;
    skos:definition "States how the tooling runs — paths, globs, file extensions, which directories a build drops. Belongs in the script: it describes the script's own behaviour, and moving it to the ontology would make the ontology answer questions about builds rather than about work."@en .

backlog:CodeTable a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Code Table"@en ;
    skos:definition "A module-level table in the tooling, recorded so the migration can count the population it actually targets. Named rather than inferred: a count of capitalised assignments grew from 23 to 26 during this lineage because the work itself added scripts, and an objective whose denominator moves cannot be met."@en .

backlog:hasTableKind a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:CodeTable ; rdfs:range backlog:TableKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ; rdfs:label "has table kind"@en .
backlog:tableInModule a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:CodeTable ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ; rdfs:label "table in module"@en .
backlog:tableExported a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:CodeTable ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ; rdfs:label "table exported"@en .

#################################################################
#  81. Stage content and report structure, exported — It10
#
#  STAGE_TYPES decided WHAT EACH LINEAGE STAGE MUST CONTAIN, in
#  python. It is the most consequential table in the tooling: the
#  ceremony this framework enforces was defined by a dictionary
#  that no query could reach, so an adopter following the published
#  ceremony and an adopter checked by the verifier were reading two
#  different specifications.
#
#  stageRequiresType makes the ceremony's own content queryable.
#  CONTAINER_TYPES and SECTIONS follow: what counts as a container,
#  and what a roadmap report is obliged to cover.
#################################################################

backlog:stageRequiresType a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:LineageStage ; rdfs:range rdfs:Resource ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "stage requires type"@en ;
    skos:definition "A class the register must carry by the time this stage closes. Exported from the pipeline verifier, where the ceremony's content was a python dictionary: the framework enforced a ceremony nobody could query, so the published description and the enforced one could drift with nothing comparing them."@en .

backlog:ContainerRole a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Container Role"@en ;
    skos:definition "A class the reporting tools treat as a container of work items. Exported so an adopter defining their own container can be counted by the reports without editing them."@en .

backlog:containerClass a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ContainerRole ; rdfs:range rdfs:Resource ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ; rdfs:label "container class"@en .

backlog:ReportSection a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Report Section"@en ;
    skos:definition "A section a roadmap report is obliged to produce. Exported so that what a report must cover is a governance statement rather than a list inside the reporter — dropping a section previously meant deleting a string, and nothing recorded that the report had narrowed."@en .

backlog:sectionOrdinal a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ReportSection ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ; rdfs:label "section ordinal"@en .
backlog:sectionKey a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ReportSection ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ; rdfs:label "section key"@en .

#  Stage content, exported from the pipeline verifier.
backlog:Stage_Mission backlog:stageRequiresType backlog:Mission .
backlog:Stage_Scope backlog:stageRequiresType backlog:Mission , backlog:ScopeStatement , backlog:ScopeExclusion , backlog:ScopeDeliverable .
backlog:Stage_Goal backlog:stageRequiresType backlog:Mission , backlog:ScopeStatement , backlog:ScopeExclusion , backlog:ScopeDeliverable , backlog:Goal .
backlog:Stage_Objective backlog:stageRequiresType backlog:Mission , backlog:ScopeStatement , backlog:ScopeExclusion , backlog:ScopeDeliverable , backlog:Goal , backlog:Objective .
backlog:Stage_Backlog backlog:stageRequiresType backlog:Mission , backlog:ScopeStatement , backlog:ScopeExclusion , backlog:ScopeDeliverable , backlog:Goal , backlog:Objective , backlog:Initiative , backlog:Epic , backlog:Story , backlog:ExecutionTask .

#################################################################
#  82. Evidence kinds and foreign namespaces — It11
#
#  KINDS in the evidence bridge pairs an evidence CLASS with a
#  python function that checks it. Only half of that belongs here:
#  WHICH evidence kinds the bridge covers is a statement about the
#  domain; HOW each is checked is the script's own behaviour and
#  stays where it runs.
#
#  Exporting the pair would have put a lambda's identity in an
#  ontology. Exporting neither would leave the coverage question
#  unanswerable. The split is the finding, and it is the same
#  distinction TableKind draws one level up.
#################################################################

backlog:bridgeCoversEvidence a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:AdoptionProfile ; rdfs:range rdfs:Resource ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "bridge covers evidence"@en ;
    skos:definition "An evidence class the evidence bridge verifies. Exported from a python list that paired each class with its checking function: which kinds are covered is a domain statement and belongs here, while how each is checked is the script's behaviour and stays there. An adopter adding an evidence kind can now see whether the bridge covers it without reading the bridge."@en .

backlog:ForeignNamespace a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Foreign Namespace"@en ;
    skos:definition "A namespace this package uses and does not own. Recorded because it is a governance statement rather than a lookup: an adopter must not assume a term from one of these is theirs to change, and that was previously a dictionary inside a readiness script."@en .

backlog:namespaceIRI a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ForeignNamespace ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ; rdfs:label "namespace IRI"@en .
backlog:namespaceOwner a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ForeignNamespace ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ; rdfs:label "namespace owner"@en .

backlog:ShapeSuite a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Shape Suite"@en ;
    skos:definition "A SHACL suite this package expects to find when checking registration readiness. Exported so that which suites a register must satisfy is answerable by query rather than by reading a list of filename globs."@en .

backlog:suiteName a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ShapeSuite ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ; rdfs:label "suite name"@en .
backlog:suitePattern a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ShapeSuite ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "suite pattern"@en ;
    skos:definition "The filename glob locating the suite. Operational, and kept beside the name deliberately: separating them would leave the ontology naming suites nothing could find."@en .

#################################################################
#  83. Header words, and a rule that caught its own author
#
#  The script-decision audit shipped at It9 reported zero. It now
#  reports two, and both are in the row checker written in the SAME
#  iteration: a tuple of header words and a bold-text test.
#
#  This is the finding worth keeping. An audit that passes once is
#  a measurement; an audit that keeps passing is a constraint. The
#  count went 0 to 2 because the work continued, exactly as the
#  table count went 23 to 26 — a denominator moves when the thing
#  measuring it is also the thing being built.
#
#  The header words are a real classification: which first-cell
#  values name a column rather than a term. That belongs here. The
#  bold test is presentation and stays in the script, marked.
#################################################################

backlog:TableHeaderWord a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Table Header Word"@en ;
    skos:definition "A first-cell value that names a COLUMN rather than a term, so a row carrying it is a header and not a claim about the ontology. Exported from a python tuple: which words head a column is a statement about how this package writes documentation, and an adopter writing tables in their own idiom could not extend the list without editing a checker they do not ship."@en .

backlog:headerWord a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:TableHeaderWord ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ; rdfs:label "header word"@en .

backlog:TermAbbreviation a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Term Abbreviation"@en ;
    skos:definition "A short form the documentation uses for a term whose full name is longer — L2 for L2_EvidenceBound. Recorded because a checker resolving identifiers literally reads a documented abbreviation as an absent term, and the honest fix is to teach the checker the abbreviation rather than to rewrite the document or exclude the row."@en .

backlog:abbreviationOf a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:TermAbbreviation ; rdfs:range rdfs:Resource ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ; rdfs:label "abbreviation of"@en .
backlog:abbreviationText a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:TermAbbreviation ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ; rdfs:label "abbreviation text"@en .

#################################################################
#  84. A criterion names the artefact that satisfies it
#
#  ROOT CAUSE of a story closed with its work undone. EP_RuleExec_S1
#  specified an expected-polarity property on every fixture and a
#  gate reading it instead of the filename. The story is Done, has
#  a test case, has test data, has a task, and the property DOES
#  NOT EXIST.
#
#  It passed because one TestEvidence attested FIVE criteria across
#  three stories, describing what the iteration did as a whole.
#  Every clause in the suite was satisfied: evidence present,
#  verified true, criteria attested, harness complete. None asked
#  the only question that mattered — does the thing this criterion
#  describes exist?
#
#  Measured: 24 of 49 evidence records attest more than one
#  criterion. Batched evidence is how a criterion gets carried by
#  a claim about its neighbours.
#
#  satisfiedByArtifact names, per criterion, the artefact that
#  makes it true — a class, a property, a file. Checkable, because
#  an IRI either resolves in the shipped ontology or does not.
#################################################################

backlog:satisfiedByArtifact a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:AcceptanceCriterion ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "satisfied by artifact"@en ;
    skos:definition "The named thing whose existence makes this criterion true — a term IRI, or a path with the symbol inside it. Per criterion and not per story, because a story with two criteria can have one satisfied and the other not, and a single evidence record covering both cannot say which. Named rather than described: a description is checked by reading, and reading is what let a criterion pass on a claim about its neighbours."@en .

backlog:CriterionSatisfaction a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Criterion Satisfaction"@en ;
    skos:definition "The record that one criterion was verified against one artefact, at a moment. Separate from TestEvidence, which records that testing HAPPENED: this records what the testing FOUND, for a single criterion. The distinction is the finding — evidence batched across five criteria satisfied every clause in the suite while one of the five was untrue."@en .

backlog:satisfiesCriterion a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:CriterionSatisfaction ; rdfs:range backlog:AcceptanceCriterion ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ; rdfs:label "satisfies criterion"@en .
backlog:artifactResolved a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:CriterionSatisfaction ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "artifact resolved"@en ;
    skos:definition "Whether the named artefact was found when the criterion was checked. False is a legitimate and useful value: it records a criterion that was looked for and not found, which is what nothing in the framework could previously say."@en .

#################################################################
#  85. Fixture polarity, declared — the work EP_RuleExec_S1
#      promised and did not do.
#
#  A fixture's expected polarity was inferred from its FILENAME:
#  a file with "negative" in the name was expected to fail. Rename
#  the file and the expectation changes with it, silently, and a
#  positive fixture renamed for tidiness becomes a negative one
#  that passes by failing.
#################################################################

backlog:hasExpectedPolarity a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:AdoptionProfile ; rdfs:range backlog:FixturePolarity ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "has expected polarity"@en ;
    skos:definition "Whether the fixture this profile governs is expected to validate cleanly or to be rejected. Declared on the profile because that is already the fixture's own statement about itself; a separate registry would be a second place a fixture is described. Replaces inference from the filename, where a rename changed what the suite expected of a file without changing the file."@en .

backlog:FixturePolarity a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Polarity_Positive backlog:Polarity_Negative ) ;
    rdfs:label "Fixture Polarity"@en .
backlog:Polarity_Positive a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:FixturePolarity ;
    rdfs:label "Positive"@en ;
    skos:definition "The fixture is expected to validate with no violation. A positive fixture that fails is a regression."@en .
backlog:Polarity_Negative a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:FixturePolarity ;
    rdfs:label "Negative"@en ;
    skos:definition "The fixture is expected to be rejected. A negative fixture that PASSES is the more dangerous failure: it means the clause it was built to prove no longer fires."@en .

#################################################################
#  86. A story is more than one task and one test
#
#  OWNER FINDING, and the measurement is stark. Fifty-five Done
#  stories: ONE task each, ONE acceptance criterion each, and
#  forty-six of them carry NO test case at all.
#
#  The framework could express better throughout. TaskType has
#  fourteen values from ISO 12207 and forty-four of fifty-one tasks
#  chose Task_Implementation. TestCase and TestData shipped at
#  v1.97.0 and forty-six stories never used them. Nothing REQUIRED
#  the decomposition, so the cheapest shape won every time — a
#  capability that is available and not obligatory is a capability
#  that gets skipped under time pressure, and this session was
#  always under time pressure.
#
#  Field practice, researched: a story is decomposed until each
#  task is a few hours' work; developers carry out story analysis
#  for design and implementation while testers perform test
#  analysis and produce the test cases, both inside the sprint; and
#  a story carries a FULL SET of acceptance tests before it is
#  planned, not one.
#
#  TestScenario names the situation a case exercises, because one
#  criterion normally needs several — the nominal path, the
#  boundary, the rejection — and a criterion with one case has
#  tested one of them.
#################################################################

backlog:TestScenario a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf core:Artifact ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Test Scenario"@en ;
    skos:definition "A situation a criterion must hold in — the nominal path, a boundary, a rejection, an empty case. Named because a criterion with one test case has been tested in one situation and reads as fully covered: the count of cases says how much testing happened and the count of scenarios says how much of the criterion it reached."@en ;
    dcterms:source "ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119-3: test case specification covers identified test conditions"@en .

backlog:coversScenario a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:TestCase ; rdfs:range backlog:TestScenario ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ; rdfs:label "covers scenario"@en .
backlog:scenarioOf a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:TestScenario ; rdfs:range backlog:AcceptanceCriterion ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ; rdfs:label "scenario of"@en .
backlog:scenarioKind a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:TestScenario ; rdfs:range backlog:ScenarioKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ; rdfs:label "scenario kind"@en .

backlog:ScenarioKind a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Scen_Nominal backlog:Scen_Boundary backlog:Scen_Rejection backlog:Scen_Absent ) ;
    rdfs:label "Scenario Kind"@en ;
    skos:definition "What kind of situation a scenario is. Four, because they fail differently: the nominal path proves the feature works, a boundary proves it stops where it should, a rejection proves it refuses what it must, and an absent case proves it survives having nothing to work with. A criterion covered only nominally is the commonest shape and the least informative."@en .

backlog:Scen_Nominal a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ScenarioKind ;
    rdfs:label "Nominal"@en ;
    skos:definition "The expected path with expected input. Proves the feature works and nothing else."@en .
backlog:Scen_Boundary a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ScenarioKind ;
    rdfs:label "Boundary"@en ;
    skos:definition "The edge of the allowed range. Proves the feature stops where it should."@en .
backlog:Scen_Rejection a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ScenarioKind ;
    rdfs:label "Rejection"@en ;
    skos:definition "Input the feature must refuse. Proves a constraint fires — the case a negative fixture exists for."@en .
backlog:Scen_Absent a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ScenarioKind ;
    rdfs:label "Absent"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nothing to work with: an empty set, a missing file, an unpopulated graph. Proves the feature fails loudly rather than silently returning nothing — the case that caught a malformed FILTER reporting zero violations and zero warnings."@en .

#################################################################
#  87. A shape names the fixture that proves it — A2
#
#  G22 recurs because a clause and its proof are separate files
#  with nothing joining them. The clause-proof checker infers the
#  link by MATCHING MESSAGE TEXT, which is fragile by construction:
#  reword a message and the clause silently becomes unproven, or
#  worse, matches a different clause and reports proven.
#
#  provenByFixture is a declared link on the shape. With it, an
#  unproven clause is a structural fact a query can find, not the
#  output of a heuristic — and the heuristic can be checked against
#  the declaration rather than trusted.
#
#  The 222 shapes are NOT annotated retroactively here. Doing so
#  would mean asserting 222 links this session did not verify, and
#  a link asserted without checking is the defect G21 names. The
#  property ships, the checker reads it where present, and the
#  count of shapes carrying one is reported.
#################################################################

backlog:provenByFixture a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain rdfs:Resource ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "proven by fixture"@en ;
    skos:definition "The fixture whose failure demonstrates this shape fires. Declared on the shape rather than inferred from its message, because a message is prose and rewording it silently unproves the clause or, worse, matches a different one and reports it proven. A shape carrying no such link is not necessarily unproven — it is unverifiable, which is the weaker and more honest claim."@en .

backlog:fixtureCaseName a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain rdfs:Resource ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "fixture case name"@en ;
    skos:definition "The individual inside the fixture that triggers this shape. Named because a fixture usually carries several cases and reporting that SOME case in a file fired says nothing about which clause was reached — the same batching defect as one evidence record attesting five criteria."@en .

#################################################################
#  88. A capability names what obliges its use — A1
#
#  G20 recurs because a class and the constraint that requires it
#  are separate objects with no relation between them. Package sat
#  unused for 91 releases; TaskType shipped with fourteen values
#  and 44 of 51 tasks chose one; TestCase shipped and 46 of 55
#  stories never used it; CodeTable carried hasTableKind with
#  nothing requiring it.
#
#  Every one was a capability delivered and not adopted, and in
#  every case the gap was invisible because nothing joined the
#  thing built to the thing that would make anyone use it.
#
#  obligedBy names the shape. adoptionRationale states, for a class
#  that is DELIBERATELY optional, why — because "no obligation" and
#  "obligation forgotten" look identical, and 18 classes currently
#  carry no shape mentioning them at all.
#################################################################

backlog:obligedBy a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain rdfs:Resource ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "obliged by"@en ;
    skos:definition "The shape that makes this class or property obligatory somewhere. Declared on the term because a capability and its obligation are otherwise separate objects with nothing joining them, which is how a class ships and is never adopted. A term with no obligation is not necessarily wrong — it may be deliberately optional — but the framework should be able to tell the two apart."@en .

backlog:adoptionRationale a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain rdfs:Resource ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "adoption rationale"@en ;
    skos:definition "Why this term carries no obligation. Required of a deliberately optional term so that optional-by-decision is distinguishable from optional-by-omission — the second is what left Package unused for 91 releases while every check passed."@en .

#################################################################
#  Adoption ruled per class, read rather than labelled in bulk.
#
#  Eighteen orphans. Five are genuinely obliged and the checker
#  could not see it because their shapes reference INSTANCES rather
#  than naming the class — Package, TestData, ContainerRole,
#  ForeignNamespace, QualityMetric all have shapes, and a text
#  match for "backlog:Package" missed PackageShape targeting it by
#  a different route.
#
#  The other thirteen are optional for stated reasons, and the
#  reasons differ in kind: two are abstract parents whose children
#  carry the obligation; five are adopter-facing vocabulary a
#  register may legitimately not use; two are documentation
#  conveniences; four are ISO 12207 terms an adopter may fold into
#  ordinary work items.
#
#  Blanket-labelling all eighteen would have hidden the five real
#  ones, which is why each was read.
#################################################################

backlog:BacklogConcept backlog:adoptionRationale "An abstract parent. Its children carry the obligations; requiring the parent directly would double every check." .
backlog:CapabilityClass backlog:adoptionRationale "Adopter-facing vocabulary for describing what a register governs. Optional by design: a register that governs one thing needs no capability taxonomy." .
backlog:ComplianceObligation backlog:adoptionRationale "For adopters under external regulation. This register has none, and requiring one would make an unregulated adopter invent a compliance record to pass." .
backlog:Defect backlog:adoptionRationale "Adopter-facing. A register tracking defects uses it; one tracking only forward work does not, and the framework should not force a defect log on a greenfield backlog." .
backlog:Enabler backlog:adoptionRationale "SAFe vocabulary for architectural work. Optional because a register may classify such work as an ordinary Epic and lose nothing." .
backlog:EnforcementDomain backlog:adoptionRationale "Groups shapes for reporting. Descriptive rather than governing; requiring it would oblige a register to categorise the framework rather than its own work." .
backlog:ModificationRequest backlog:adoptionRationale "ISO 12207 change vocabulary. Optional: a register may record change as a new WorkItem rather than a request against an existing one." .
backlog:ProblemReport backlog:adoptionRationale "As ModificationRequest — ISO 12207 vocabulary an adopter may or may not separate from ordinary work items." .
backlog:RoadmapElement backlog:adoptionRationale "An abstract parent, as BacklogConcept." .
backlog:Spike backlog:adoptionRationale "Timeboxed investigation. Optional because a register may record investigation as an ordinary Story with a Task_SystemAnalysis task." .
backlog:TableHeaderWord backlog:adoptionRationale "Documentation vocabulary read by the row checker. Requiring instances would oblige every adopter to describe our table conventions." .
backlog:TermAbbreviation backlog:adoptionRationale "As TableHeaderWord: a display convenience, not a governing concept." .

#################################################################
#  89. Every number says where it came from — A3
#
#  G24 recurs because nothing marks a figure as derived or
#  asserted. hasCommittedEffort was compared against hasCapacity
#  and BOTH were assertions: an iteration held 15 points while
#  declaring 9 and every check passed. iterationStart and
#  iterationEnd were asserted dateTimes compared to nothing — two
#  iterations took 32 and 28 minutes against a declared fourteen
#  days, an overstatement of 667 times.
#
#  MeasurementKind already does this for Objective and for nothing
#  else. There are 36 numeric properties in this vocabulary and 35
#  of them carry no statement about their own provenance.
#
#  numberOrigin is declared on the PROPERTY, not on each value: a
#  property is derived or asserted by its nature, and saying so 36
#  times is a vocabulary decision rather than 36 000 data
#  decisions. derivationQuery ships the query for a derived one, so
#  the figure can be recomputed rather than believed.
#################################################################

backlog:NumberOrigin a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Num_Derived backlog:Num_Asserted backlog:Num_Measured ) ;
    rdfs:label "Number Origin"@en ;
    skos:definition "Where a numeric property's values come from. Three, because they carry different weight and the framework has confused them: a derived value is computable from other data and can be checked, an asserted value is a judgement and can only be agreed with, and a measured value comes from outside the register and can be re-taken. A number compared only against another number of unstated origin proves nothing."@en .

backlog:Num_Derived a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:NumberOrigin ;
    rdfs:label "Derived"@en ;
    skos:definition "Computable from other data in the register. Must ship its query, or it is an assertion wearing a derivation's clothes."@en .
backlog:Num_Asserted a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:NumberOrigin ;
    rdfs:label "Asserted"@en ;
    skos:definition "A judgement someone recorded. Legitimate and the weakest: it cannot be recomputed, only disputed. Two asserted numbers agreeing with each other is the defect that let an iteration hold fifteen points while declaring nine."@en .
backlog:Num_Measured a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:NumberOrigin ;
    rdfs:label "Measured"@en ;
    skos:definition "Taken from outside the register — a commit timestamp, a file count, a clock. Re-takeable by anyone with the same source, which is what distinguishes it from an assertion."@en .

backlog:numberOrigin a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain rdf:Property ; rdfs:range backlog:NumberOrigin ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "number origin"@en ;
    skos:definition "Declared on the PROPERTY rather than on each value: a property is derived or asserted by its nature, so this is a vocabulary decision made once rather than a data decision made per figure."@en .

backlog:derivationQuery a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain rdf:Property ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "derivation query"@en ;
    skos:definition "How a derived property's value is computed. Required of a derived property so the figure can be recomputed rather than believed — an unshipped derivation is an assertion, and the difference is invisible from the value alone."@en .

#################################################################
#  Origin declared for all 36 numeric properties, read one by one.
#
#  The last bulk ruling in this package was wrong six times out of
#  six, so each was decided by asking what would have to be true to
#  recompute it.
#
#  THREE are derived and ship their query. hasCommittedEffort is
#  the one that caused G24 — it was compared against hasCapacity
#  while both were assertions, and an iteration held fifteen points
#  declaring nine. hasScoreValue is WSJF arithmetic that this
#  session got wrong by hand five times in one release.
#
#  FIVE are measured: they come from a clock, a commit, or a count
#  outside the register, and can be re-taken.
#
#  The remaining twenty-eight are ASSERTED and say so. That is not
#  a failing — a capacity is a judgement and cannot be otherwise —
#  but a judgement compared against another judgement proves
#  nothing, and until now the framework could not tell the reader
#  which comparisons those were.
#################################################################

backlog:expectedValue backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Asserted .
backlog:forecastIterationsObserved backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Measured .
backlog:forecastObservedVelocity backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Measured .
backlog:hasActualEffort backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Measured .
backlog:hasBaselineValue backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Asserted .
backlog:hasBatchCompleted backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Derived ;
    backlog:derivationQuery "SELECT (COUNT(?u) AS ?v) WHERE { $this backlog:batchUnitDone ?u }" .
backlog:hasBatchSize backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Asserted .
backlog:hasBudgetCeiling backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Asserted .
backlog:hasCapacity backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Asserted .
backlog:hasCommittedEffort backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Derived ;
    backlog:derivationQuery "SELECT (SUM(?e) AS ?v) WHERE { ?i backlog:memberOfContainer $this ; backlog:hasEffortEstimate ?e }" .
backlog:hasConfidence backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Asserted .
backlog:hasDepFactor backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Asserted .
backlog:hasDimensionRate backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Asserted .
backlog:hasDuration backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Asserted .
backlog:hasEffort backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Asserted .
backlog:hasEffortEstimate backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Asserted .
backlog:hasEstimateConfidence backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Asserted .
backlog:hasGateOrder backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Asserted .
backlog:hasImpact backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Asserted .
backlog:hasJobSize backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Asserted .
backlog:hasLaunchPriority backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Asserted .
backlog:hasObservedDuration backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Measured .
backlog:hasObservedValue backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Measured .
backlog:hasQuantity backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Asserted .
backlog:hasReach backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Asserted .
backlog:hasRiskReductionValue backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Asserted .
backlog:hasRoadmapRank backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Asserted .
backlog:hasScoreValue backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Derived ;
    backlog:derivationQuery "SELECT ((?bv + ?tc + ?rr) / ?js AS ?v) WHERE { $this backlog:hasUserBusinessValue ?bv ; backlog:hasTimeCriticality ?tc ; backlog:hasRiskReductionValue ?rr ; backlog:hasJobSize ?js }" .
backlog:hasStepOrdinal backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Asserted .
backlog:hasTargetShare backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Asserted .
backlog:hasTargetValue backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Asserted .
backlog:hasTimeCriticality backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Asserted .
backlog:hasUserBusinessValue backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Asserted .
backlog:hasWipLimitValue backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Asserted .
backlog:layerOrdinal backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Asserted .
backlog:sectionOrdinal backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Asserted .

#################################################################
#  90. A lineage is set down, not left running
#
#  OWNER FINDING, three parts, each measured.
#
#  1. Mission_BuildSoftware_v2 has 10 of 10 deliverables satisfied
#     by Done work and is still LIVE. The framework can supersede a
#     mission — replaced by a better statement of the same intent —
#     and has no word for one that was ACHIEVED. So a finished
#     lineage stays in scope: 294 clauses query its 55 closed
#     stories on every run, forever.
#
#  2. Ach_Withdrawn is used TWICE in the register and declared
#     NOWHERE. AchievementStatus is closed at three by owl:oneOf,
#     so two individuals sit outside a closed enumeration — a
#     contradiction, and no clause noticed. Declared here.
#
#  3. Ach_Retrospective is the missing state the owner's rule
#     needs. Goal_BuildContained counts work done outside scope
#     during a FINISHED development: the count is fixed at 1
#     forever and no future work can reduce it. That is not a goal
#     — a goal steers — it is a record. It was added retroactively
#     at v1.102.0 and said so at the time.
#
#  The owner's rule: a lineage with an unreached goal is NOT
#  retired; the goal's status changes first, if it is no longer
#  needed. Ach_Retrospective is that change, and it is narrower
#  than withdrawal: withdrawn means the measure was wrong,
#  retrospective means it was right and can only report.
#################################################################

backlog:Ach_Withdrawn a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:AchievementStatus ;
    rdfs:label "Withdrawn"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective was retired because it measured the wrong thing — normally a superseded mission's progress. Used in this register since v1.82.0 and never declared until v1.128.0, which is a term in use sitting outside its own closed enumeration."@en .

backlog:Ach_Retrospective a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:AchievementStatus ;
    rdfs:label "Retrospective"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective measures a fact about finished work and cannot move: the event it counts already happened. Distinct from Withdrawn, which says the measure was wrong — retrospective says it was right and can now only report. A retrospective objective must never read Met by having its number adjusted; it reads what it read, permanently."@en .

backlog:MissionOutcome a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:Out_InFlight backlog:Out_Achieved backlog:Out_Abandoned ) ;
    rdfs:label "Mission Outcome"@en ;
    skos:definition "Where a mission stands as a whole. Superseding is not an outcome — it replaces a statement of intent with a better one and the intent continues. These three are what happens to the intent itself, and the framework had none of them: a mission achieved and a mission still running were indistinguishable, so a finished lineage stayed in scope of every query forever."@en .

backlog:Out_InFlight a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:MissionOutcome ;
    rdfs:label "In flight"@en ;
    skos:definition "Work continues. The default, and the only state in which a mission belongs in the live register."@en .
backlog:Out_Achieved a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:MissionOutcome ;
    rdfs:label "Achieved"@en ;
    skos:definition "Every goal is reached or has a status saying why it no longer steers. A mission may only reach this state when NO goal is left unreached and unexplained — a lineage carrying a live unmet goal is not finished, it is stalled, and retiring it would bury the reason."@en .
backlog:Out_Abandoned a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:MissionOutcome ;
    rdfs:label "Abandoned"@en ;
    skos:definition "Work stopped without the goals being reached, and the reason is recorded. Named so that abandonment is a decision with an author rather than a lineage that quietly stopped being mentioned."@en .

backlog:hasMissionOutcome a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Mission ; rdfs:range backlog:MissionOutcome ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ; rdfs:label "has mission outcome"@en .

backlog:outcomeRationale a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Mission ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "outcome rationale"@en ;
    skos:definition "Why the mission reached this outcome, quoting the evidence. Required for Achieved and Abandoned so that setting a lineage down is an act with a reason rather than a status someone changed."@en .

backlog:retiredAtCommit a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Mission ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "retired at commit"@en ;
    skos:definition "The commit at which the lineage was set down. An external witness, as closedAtCommit is for a stage: without it a retirement date is an assertion and the framework already learned what asserted dates are worth."@en .

#################################################################
#  91. A lineage is a thing, not a pattern of links — META
#
#  OWNER RULING, and the analysis that led to it. Seven lineages
#  live in one register file: six finished or superseded, one
#  active, all validated on every run. They are near-disjoint —
#  only 22 cross-lineage references exist and every one is
#  memberOfContainer pointing at a shared container.
#
#  So why does a finished lineage stay in scope? Because THERE IS
#  NO LINEAGE OBJECT. A lineage was a pattern of links from a
#  mission down through goals to work items, and a pattern cannot
#  be picked up and moved. Partitioning by inferring the closure
#  broke 297 constraints: harnesses, refinements and planning
#  events have no lineage of their own, so they stayed behind while
#  the items they point at moved.
#
#  Lineage is instantiated as INDIVIDUALS, not generated as a class
#  per lineage. Seven lineages would otherwise mean seven classes,
#  every new lineage a TBox change, and the ontology growing with
#  the work rather than governing it. The framework already puns
#  this way — stageRequiresType points from an individual to a
#  class and has shipped since v1.117.
#
#  MEASURED before building: not loading the archive saves 60s of
#  139s, 43 percent. The saving comes from the file being absent,
#  not from a filter inside a clause — a filter still costs per
#  focus node.
#################################################################

backlog:Lineage a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Lineage"@en ;
    skos:definition "One complete run from a mission to its outcome, as a first-class object that can be counted, queried and moved. A lineage was previously a pattern of links and nothing more, which is why a finished one could not be set aside: patterns cannot be picked up. Instantiated as individuals rather than generated as a class per lineage, so that starting a lineage is data and not a change to the vocabulary."@en .

backlog:belongsToLineage a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:BacklogConcept ; rdfs:range backlog:Lineage ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "belongs to lineage"@en ;
    skos:definition "The lineage this individual was created during. Carried by EVERY individual, not only work items: the partition that broke 297 constraints failed precisely because harnesses, refinements and planning events had no lineage and had to be inferred from what they pointed at. An individual with no lineage cannot be archived, because nothing can say which archive it belongs in."@en .

backlog:lineageForMission a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Lineage ; rdfs:range backlog:Mission ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "lineage for mission"@en ;
    skos:definition "The mission this lineage pursues. One mission per lineage: a mission amended mid-flight is the same lineage under a better statement, which is what supersedesMission records."@en .

backlog:lineageOrdinal a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Lineage ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "lineage ordinal"@en ;
    skos:definition "Where this lineage falls in the sequence of runs. Recorded so that history has an order independent of dates, which this framework has already found to be unreliable."@en ;
    backlog:numberOrigin backlog:Num_Asserted .

backlog:lineageArchived a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Lineage ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "lineage archived"@en ;
    skos:definition "Whether this lineage's individuals have been moved out of the live register. Distinct from its mission's outcome: a lineage may be achieved and not yet archived, and stating both separately means the move is an act with its own record rather than a side effect of a status."@en .

backlog:archiveFile a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:Lineage ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "archive file"@en ;
    skos:definition "Where an archived lineage now lives. Named rather than implied, because the saving comes from the file NOT BEING LOADED, and a reader must be able to find what was set aside without guessing a filename."@en .

#################################################################
#  92. A container serves one lineage
#
#  The archive split was blocked by five shared containers — one
#  Register, one Roadmap, one Scope, one Commitment, one
#  DefinitionOfDone — serving all seven lineages at once. They
#  could not move to the archive because live work uses them, and
#  could not stay whole because they reference retired work.
#
#  Measured per container: Roadmap references six lineages, Scope
#  three, Commitment and DoD one each, Register none directly. So
#  the sharing is real but shallow, and the fix is a container per
#  lineage rather than a general-purpose splitter.
#
#  containerForLineage says which lineage a container serves. A
#  container serving none is FRAMEWORK-WIDE and stays live by
#  design — the Register itself is the obvious case, since a
#  register that archives with its contents cannot be read.
#################################################################

backlog:containerForLineage a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItemContainer ; rdfs:range backlog:Lineage ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "container for lineage"@en ;
    skos:definition "The lineage this container serves. Absent means framework-wide, which is a claim rather than an omission: a container with no lineage stays live when every lineage around it is archived, and that must be a decision someone took."@en .

backlog:containerScope a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:WorkItemContainer ; rdfs:range backlog:ContainerScope ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ; rdfs:label "container scope"@en .

backlog:ContainerScope a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    owl:oneOf ( backlog:CScope_Lineage backlog:CScope_Framework ) ;
    rdfs:label "Container Scope"@en ;
    skos:definition "Whether a container belongs to one lineage or to the framework. Stated rather than inferred from whether containerForLineage happens to be present, because absent-by-decision and absent-by-omission look identical — the distinction this framework has already had to learn twice."@en .

backlog:CScope_Lineage a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ContainerScope ;
    rdfs:label "Lineage-scoped"@en ;
    skos:definition "Serves one lineage and archives with it."@en .
backlog:CScope_Framework a owl:NamedIndividual , backlog:ContainerScope ;
    rdfs:label "Framework-scoped"@en ;
    skos:definition "Serves the framework across lineages and stays live. The Register is the case that forces this to exist: a register that archives with its contents cannot be read."@en .

#################################################################
#  93. Whether a checker accepts a graph is a declared fact — A4
#
#  Corrected twice before landing here. A hardcoded python tuple,
#  then a source-shape guess that misclassified backlog_validate,
#  then a bare-run behavioural test that misclassified six checkers
#  legitimately reporting real data. Three attempts to guess a fact
#  that has no reliable shortcut in either source code or runtime
#  output.
#
#  ToolScript declares it once, verified against observed behaviour
#  rather than trusted — the same discipline CodeTable applied to
#  classification tables buried in python.
#################################################################

backlog:ToolScript a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf backlog:BacklogConcept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "Tool Script"@en ;
    skos:definition "A shipped checker, recorded so the fact of whether it accepts a graph via CLI argument is data rather than a guess made three different ways in three different releases."@en .

backlog:scriptFileName a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ToolScript ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ; rdfs:label "script file name"@en .

backlog:acceptsGraphPath a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain backlog:ToolScript ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://example.org/backlog> ;
    rdfs:label "accepts graph path"@en ;
    skos:definition "Whether this checker takes a graph file via CLI argument and must therefore refuse rather than default to empty when given none. False means the checker locates its own data internally and a bare run examining real data is correct, not blind."@en .
